Barack Obama wins Nobel Peace Prize as fourth U.S. president
U.S. President Barack Obama arrives in Oslo, Norway for 26 hours to receive the Nobel Peace Prize as the fourth U.S. president in history.
In addition, he receives the diploma as well as the medal and a cheque of $1.4 million for his extraordinary efforts to intensify world diplomacy and cooperation among peoples.
The White House chief arrives in the northbound metropolis with his wife Michelle tomorrow and will be busy all the time.
Initially, he pays a visit to the Nobel Institute, where he meets for the first time with the five committee members who elected him from 172 people and 33 organizations in October.
The presidential pair then scheduled a meeting with the Norwegian king Harald V and Queen Sonja.
In the afternoon, the visit reaches its climate with the ceremony in which Obama takes over the prestige prize.
He receives this as the fourth U.S. president, but only as the third to receive the prize directly in office.
The White House predicted that Obama will talk about the war of Afghanistan when taking over the prize.
The president doesn't want to dodge this issue because he knows he's accepting the prize as a president who is currently waging war in two countries.
A few days ago, he intensified the war effort by sending more troops to Afghanistan, which none of his critics forget to highlight.
At the ceremony, Obama also received the Gol Medal, the diploma and the cheque for ten million Swedish kronor (about 24 million Czech crowns).
He intends to donate the money to charity, but he has not yet decided on a concrete charitable work.
The event programme then envisages a banquet with the president and his wife, which, in addition to Norway's king and queen, will also include the prime minister and another 250 invited guests.
Obama has been very reluctance from the outset to the price.
For example, he argued that he didn’t quite deserve the prize.
On several occasions, he also repeated that it is the price not only for him, but for all those who aspire to the same values as him.
Immediately after the announcement, he pointed out that for him this is "call for action".
Conservationists are accused of extortion
Leitmeritz police accused the chairman of the citizens' association "Naturschutzgemeinschaft Leitmeritz" because of blackmail.
In several cases last year, he lodged appeals against some construction procedures, for the withdrawal of his appeals he demanded money from the investors in his favor, the Leitmeritz police spokeswoman, Alena Romova, said.
The chairman of the Leitmeritz nature conservation community is Lubomir Studnicka.
Now he is in custody and it threatens him with a prison sentence of up to three years.
The sorting garbage containers in Brnn don't smell good
In Prague, people can sort the beverage packaging, in the South Moravian villages you will find the sorted garbage containers again at every turn.
And in Brno?
There you would have to reserve a few minutes to find the suitable garbage cans.
I don't want to say that the waste separation in Brno wouldn't work at all.
But it seems to me if the councillors care insufficiently about this problem, even if we are the second largest city in the republic.
Brno is located in waste separation in the jungle league, not only because we as citizens are only allowed to dispose of paper, glass and plastics in the sorting containers, but above all due to the number of rubbish bins for such waste.
Did they ever try to dispose of the PET bottles in a collection basket, for example?
This is just art, above all it takes time and strong nerves.
The only spot I have found by chance so far is the Moravia square opposite the hit goose.
Mr Onderka should not annoy us his colleagues, but this seems far too little to me.
Apart from the fact that the waste separation is also in other parts not a licking of honey.
Whole ranks of my friends, therefore, prefer not to sort the waste at all.
From laziness.
It's too far for the garbage cans.
I don't wonder.
Even after the celebration, I offered to dispose of some glass and plastic bottles.
But on Konicova Strasse I didn't see any paint waste bins in plain sight.
Luckily, I came to the right place while driving trams.
However, the rubbish almost ran over.
This is still the center of Brno, which represents the whole city, isn’t it?!
However, ecology and some kind of aesthetics probably leaves the Brno socialists cold.
They would rather buy the city an ice hockey league, which makes us shame all over the Republic, instead of buying a few more garbage cans and offering the sorting of other raw materials for a cleaner Brno.
I’m glad that I was able to confirm my assumption with official statistics after searching the internet for a while.
According to the data of the company EKO-KOM, Brno is really the worst in the whole district of South Moravia.
"Paroubek" budget has stripped the money for pensions and sick pay
After the Left Party, despite the government's view, gave farmers and civil servants more money, there is no longer any money in the budget for pensions, sincences and building savings business.
But also not for the national debt interest or for the international court disputes.
That's causing even more problems that will be in government in the second half of next year, says the finance minister, Eduard Janota.
When the Social Democrats and Communists enforced the pay rise for some groups, they expected the government to borrow the money for the sensitive expenses, old-age pensions or for the sick pay. "That's just a postponement of the problems into the future, mining," says Janota.
From the loans, higher and higher interest rates are paid, and you have to reimburse the money at some point.
Too much to choose from, where to take, the treasury is empty.
If the social benefits or costs for state-owned enterprises are not reduced, the tax must be increased.
For all those to whom the Left Party has now given more money: the fire brigade, the teachers, the farmers.
and later for their children as well.
People don't have to be afraid for pensions or the sickness pay, from whose $1.8 billion has disappeared, they're getting that money.
But at the price that the state is even more indebted - next year, not only 163 billion will be missing in the state treasury, but far more.
Despite the efforts of Minister Janota, the debt will rise faster and faster, and together with the costs and interest rates.
The state will need the money for compensation, then it will no longer be enough to increase VAT only slightly or to shorten the maternity allowance, as the government did in its counter-deficience package.
Due to the transfer of money in favour of some electoral groups, five billions will also be missing next year for the expansion of highways and railways.
These are predominantly the sections won in the competition, which can no longer be redesigned and possibly discounted.
At most, which is also not free.
This means delay or discontinuation of thirty large structures, the creation of Europe money is also at risk, warns the spokesman of the Ministry of Transport, Karel Hanzelka.
The CSSD has proposed that it solve the outflow of money Solomonically - it attacks the CEZ stock dividends, in which the state is the owner of the majority, and from these the money is then poured out into the transport fund.
The problem is that the amount of the dividend is never safe and it is by no means an infinite source.
Last year, CEZ €18 billion flowed into the budget.
Every year, the dividends are used to pay the environmental damage liquidations, the pension account is replenished and this money also serves as a reserve for the planned pension reform.
With this trick, according to the rules of the EU, the actual budget deficiency rises to the level of 5.7 percent GDP.
However, the carnival of the upheavals does not end.
Almost three billion fell the budget for various compensation for victims of communism and of crimes, or for expenses for the judicial disputes, even for building savings.
Whether the state can cope without this money is not certain.
It's pure gambling.
If we lose an international judicial dispute, then we have to pay for that anyway, Janota clarifies.
The minister must then save the money internally or, at worst, attack the government budget reserve.
One hundred millions are also missing for the two-billiollion "Staatskasse" project that has already begun, which should make it possible to monitor on-line what the individual authorities spend the money for.
The first stage starts in January.
Should the finance minister not find the money elsewhere, the project would have to be stopped and in this case sanctions come, Janota warned.
Your next smartphone will dominate two operating systems
Americans are expectinging a phone for the future, on which the user can switch between different operating systems by pressing a single button.
The presented plans look promising.
It's enough to press just one button, and Windows Mobile turns into Android within a few seconds.
It is precisely this possibility that the American company VMware wants to present, focusing primarily on the development of virtual software for computers.
We enable you to carry out two user profiles simultaneously on your mobile phone.
You can switch between these, you can separate them into two home and work profiles.
Both will work in parallel, Srinivas Krishnamurti of VMware indicated during an interview for Computerworld magazine.
It was presented in November last year and presented for the first time a few days ago.
There will be 2012 in the market.
The virtualization of smart phones is not fiction.
VMware has already successfully demonstrated the smartphone test pattern with two operating systems to the journalists.
One was a modified Nokia N800 with a RAM memory of 128 MB, on which at the same ran the aforementioned Windows Mobile and Android systems at the same time.
The development of the new technology for mobile phones is in full swing.
VMware is now working together with European and American operators on smartphone virtualization and to the customer, the devices are to come within 2012.
Shortage of snow on the mountains torments hotel owners
Snowless pistes don't just worry the Giant Mountains residents.
The lack of snow also deters people from ordering ski stays in hotels and guesthouses.
That’s why you can still get free rooms for all winter appointments including Christmas and New Year’s Eve in Giant Mountains.
Our sites are frequently visited.
People are going through the offers, determining the prices, but by now they are afraid to order binding.
And when they call us, they immediately ask if, in our opinion, this year's Christmas will be on snow or "on mud", the operator of the information portal Spindl.Info, Martin Jandura.
If you want the New Year's Eve stay in the spindle mill exactly according to your ideas, you should no longer hesitate with the order.
If you prefer saving and where the quality of the stay only comes second, you can try to wait a few more days.
In the meantime, the Spindelmühle hotel owners only offer weekly stays for New Year’s Eve.
I think they're going to try to sell them for a while, but then they, too, have to give in and respond to shorter times.
And then the free rooms will dwindle quickly, Jandura estimated.
Free beds on all winter dates are always offered by the travel agency Ingtours in Vrchlabi.
Now we are most interested in the Christmas week, which is not yet sold out.
In our offer, about half of the seats are still available.
That’s why we’ve already prepared some cheaper Christmas stays for the last moment, the director of Ingtours, Petr Schiefert.
New Year's Eve in Giant Mountains can also be offered by the Vrchlabi travel agency, but there are less vacancies here.
Good sales are also found in the first fourteen days in February, the remaining winter appointments are sold on average.
This year, the interest for the winter stays is also negatively influenced by the fact that there is always no snow in the Giant Mountains.
People are waiting to see if snow falls.
No one wants to spend the end of the year in the mountains without snow.
If the slopes are snowed within a week, Christmas will also be sold out.
Conversely and slightly better than last year, it is possible to occupy the rooms at the Hotel Horizont in Pecpod Snezkou.
We're about five percent better than the previous year.
On New Year's Eve and Christmas we only have a few free rooms.
There is great interest during the Polish holidays in January, and in February the business will also go well, informed the director of the best Pec hotel, Karel Rada.
The Hotel Omnia is also staffed at the central parking lot in Janske Lazne for 80 percent of the New Year’s stays.
On the other hand, half of the beds remain free at Christmas in the newest hotel in Janske Lazne.
This year we were pleasantly surprised by January, for which we are currently about 60 percent occupied.
February is now a bit weaker, but at the moment when the snow arrives, the interest in the stays in the Giant mountains certainly also increases, the director of the Hotel Omnia, Erik Sporysch.
In the meantime, the Riesengebirge hotel owners are waiting for a greater snow supply.
On Thursday it should cool down and if it comes to rainfall, this should be snow on the mountains.
But after that a warming is to come again, Jiri Jakubsky informed from the location of the Czech weather office in Hradec Kralove.
USA: Repetition is mother of prudeness
It's almost funny with what student aspiration Barack Obama, the president with his reputation of the smartest, repeats his predecessor's strategy on the Afghanistan issue, which he himself thought was the dumbest.
When he finally sweated out his eagerly anticipated Doctrine in Afghanistan, it turned out that he wrote it off via the copying paper from the Bush-Iraq scenario three years ago.
Not only does he use the phrase "just like in Iraq" in the text himself several times, he has even used the name of the Bush Declaration of January 2007 as the name of his own declaration: "New Forward" without procrastinating.
Three years ago, he criticized the Bush idea - sending out fresh troop contingents to Iraq plagued by the civil war - as an "irresponsible and, in its conclusions, carastrophic decision."
However, following months showed that the Bush strategy based on military recommendations was the only possible and in this respect very successful that it is no longer speaking at all in the news about Iraq today.
Bush's reputation has not been improved by this, because objectively he has left his successor a much more grateful starting situation than it could be expected.
In Afghanistan, Barack Obama hopes the miracle repeats itself.
The militaries have once again asked him to increase the troop contingent, especially the commander-in-chief of the Afghanistan operation General Stanley McCristal, who harshly criticized the procrastinating shooters in the White House even despite the subordination habits.
He demanded 40,000 troops to be able to reverse the situation for the better.
The president hesitated for three months, but didn't come up with a better idea, and so he cheated the general by at least 10,000 troops.
From Europe, he squeezed the commitment for another 5,000, even if he himself originally expected at least 10,000.
Highly problematic about the entire Obama strategy is his intention to withdraw the troops from Afghanistan in 18 months and to complete this process within three years. The whole Obama doctrine leaves more questions than answers.
Some doubt that the halved increase will have the same effect as the measure in Iraq.
Even despite the fratricidal war in recent years, the Iraq society is relatively well-structured, its leaders honor the values.
The Afghan situation is characterized by the contraindication - you cannot rely on anyone and no agreements are held.
Under these conditions, it's hard to preparing the Afghan security forces, who are accustomed to changing their minds every day.
The political institutions, even if they have been formally correctly founded, remain only apparent buildings, behind which patriarchal tribal relations will continue to proliferate.
Balancing interests between the different ethnic groups is unusually complicated, especially because of the clutterancy of the circumstances that complicate the Pakistan and Iran influences even further.
The establishment of an "actionable state", which is the minimalist goal of the whole operation, is also very unrealistic.
Time horizon of the Obama strategy alienated by his voluntarism
Over the past eight years, the situation in Afghanistan has deteriorated only further and is now on the verge of explosion.
Believing that the little soldiers who broke out into the war cry about the "early homecoming" create a miracle here can only create a truly romantic soul that cannot be impressured by facts.
From a political point of view, setting the date for troop withdrawal is pure irresponsibility, because the Taliban will take up that as the indirect confession of American defeat.
The last NATO summit showed that the willingness of Europeans to participate in the battles is running out.
Europe’s politicians can’t and don’t want to explain to their constituents how the security of Germany or Italy is related to the war in the Hindu Kush foothills.
Another possible factor that can radically influence the situation is the development of events in Iran.
Should the situation continue to deteriorate and the increased sanctions do not affect the Iranians, a very serious question arises for the United States whether the problem cannot be solved with troops.
This question must be answered during the period in which America is progressing after Obama for the winning deduction from Afghanistan.
However, any solution will also represent a dramatic turn of the Afghan situation.
The only consolation patch for Barack Obama is the fact that there are still three years left until the presidential election, and thus there is still plenty of time for him to resort to a different strategy if it would turn out to be a total failure.
Bank Goldman Sach's managers no longer receive premium bonuses in cash
The senior executives at American bank Goldman Sachs will no longer receive premium bonuses in cash this year.
This has been announced by the company, which responds to the sharp criticism of its waging practices.
A group of 30 top managers will receive shares instead of the money, which can only be sold in five years.
In addition, the shares can be deprived of managers in the case if they act excessively risky.
Goldman Sachs, according to Reuters, puts itself at the forefront of efforts to connect Wall Street's premium bonuses with long-term performance.
I believe that Wall Street is aware of the wider direction in which it has to go, the former banker of JPMorgan, Douglas Elliott, led.
The problem lies in the details, he added.
Goldman Sach's target after setting aside nearly $17 billion for premium bonuses for the first three quarters of this year.
The total amount of premium bonuses over the course of this year is likely to exceed $20 billion, according tover the Reuters agency, despite the measure indicated today.
At the beginning of the financial crisis, high premium bonuses in the banking sector have become the hottest political issue in a number of countries.
Britain reported on Wednesday that it intends to burden the banker's bonuses, which exceed £25,000, one-time with a tax of 50 per cent.
France is also preparing similar steps.
This year, women have won the Nobel Prizes in all disciplines, except in physics.
Five record-breaking women took over this year’s Nobel Prizes in the categories and for literature today in Stockholm from the hands of the King Carl XVI Gustaf.
In addition to the four female scientists, there was also the German writer of Romanian descendation, Herta Müller, among them.
Two American biologists, Elizabeth Blackburn and Carol Greider, together with their compatriot Jack Szostak, have received the Nobel Prize in medicine for research in the chromoson field.
The prize for chemistry has been awardeed by the Israeli Ada Jonath together with the Americans, Venkatraman Ramakrishnan and Thomas Steitz, for enlightening the ribosome structure and function.
The last laureatin awarded the Nobel Prize in Economics, which the American Elinor Ostrom and her compatriot Oliver Williamson have received for analysis of the economic report.
The only specialist category in which no woman received a Nobel Prize this year was physics.
Today, the scientists Charles Kao received this award for research in the fiber optics sector and George Smith together with Willard Boyl for the CCD chip invention, which is the basis of all digital chambers, fax machines or astronomical telescopes.
Each of these laureates have received the diploma, the Nobel medal and certificate of obtaining the cash prize.
This amounts to ten million Swedish crowns in each category.
If there are several laureates in each category, the cash prize is divided among them.
According to tradition, the American president, Barak Obama, has already received the most anticipated Nobel Peace Prize in Oslo this afternoon.
In his speech, he acknowledged the controversy of his appointment because he is only at the very beginning of his path, and is also at the head of the country that is waging two wars - in Iraq and Afghanistan.
While respecting his policies, however, he added that wars in certain situations are necessary for the struggle for world peace, even if the price is high.
Minister Janota considers resigning. Klaus has invited him to the castle
President Vaclav Klaus receives the finance minister, Eduard Janota, early on Friday, who is thinking about staying in the government because he disagrees with the implementation of the state budget for next year in the form as released by the Assembly of Representatives on Wednesday.
It is believed that the budget, and perhaps also Janota's remaining in the cabinet, will be the themes of Friday's meeting.
The budget is also criticized by Klaus.
According to him, the budget deficit is too high, which also deepens the crisis of public finances.
The presidential spokesperson, Radim Ochvat, informed about the meeting.
Janota wants to negotiate his further stay in his function on Monday with the Premier, Jan Fischer.
Under the budget law, which Klaus now receives for signature, the Czech Republic was supposed to operate with a deficit of 163 billion crowns.
In parliament, however, votes were held on Wednesday on the Left Party proposals on the upheaval of money and the money for state civil servants' salaries, for the upkeeping of social benefits and direct wages of farmers.
The government has countered the Social Democracy's proposals that they covertly increase the budget deficit.
Between the Left Party on the one hand and the government, Klaus and the right-wing party, on the other hand, a sharp domestic political dispute has arisen, because of which the question mark about the further work of the Fischer civil servants' cabinet arises, on which the ODS, CSSD and the Green Party have agreed in the spring.
Critics claim that the budget, as adopted by Parliament, virtually liquidates the package of austerity measures enforced by the fishermen's government in the autumn, and which, according to their understanding, is intended to end public debt.
Klaus, who can sign the law on the state budget but doesn't have to, explained today at the discussion breakfast with the entrepreneurs that politicians have neglected the "serious problem" that "the unsustainable deficit" of public finances has been neglected for a long time.
In his opinion, there is no longer a good solution today.
An improvement could only bring about a strong government, which could build on a broader consensus of the political parties.
The ODS chairman, Mirek Topolanek, pointed out that the budget is gruesome, and the government should weage its further existence.
The ODS deputy chairman, Petr Necas, told the CTK that the concept of civil servants' cabinet, with support from the CSSD, ODS and Green Party, obviously no longer works.
Premier Fischer referred to similar words in the editorial board as "strong pronouncements".
The CSSD chief, Jiri Paroubek, responded to Topolanek's words that the budget deficit was gruestious that the former government of Topolanek had prepared for this year, exceeding 200 billion crowns.
Already on Wednesday, the CSSD rated the approval of the state budget for next year as a success.
The People's Party was also satisfied.
Mandela was played by Morgan Freeman in Clint Eastwood's new film
The South Africans claim that the world's new Hollywood film "Invictus: The Undefeated" shows much of the country, of whose struggle and victories, despite some criticisms that the lead role was cast by an American actor.
The story tells about sports, racial conditions and Nelson Mandela.
The tireless fighter against the racist apartheid government system in the South African Republic, as well as its first black president, Nelson Mandela, is portrayed in his life story by the American Oscar actor, Morgan Freeman.
Freeman said that he was asked by ninety-year-old Mandela if he could portray him in the Eastwood film.
Matt Damon takes on another meaningful role.
I told him, "If I'm going to play you, I'll have to meet you.
The seventy-two-year-old actor, who had received the award for his role in the Eastwood drama from the "Million-dollar Baby" boxing area, has already portrayed the leader of the movement for the abolition of slavery, which has finally become a slave owner, a fictitious U.S. present, and also in "Evan Almighty," but only rarely someone who is still alive and is as important to many people.
The former South African national was in prison for 27 years for his active activity in ending apartheid in the South African Republic.
He was released in 1990 and became president of the country for four years.
In 1993 he was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize.
"Invictus" is the Latin word for "unbeaten," and at the same time is the name of a poem by English author William Ernest Henley published in 1875.
The requirement was how to speak
The film depicts a true event in which Nelson Mandela merged his forces with the captain of South African rugby team, Francois Pienaar, to unite the country. The new president Mandela knows that his people still remain racially and economically divided even after apartheid.
He believes he can unify his people through the sport, that's why he decides to agree the rugby players who are among the outsiders worldwide.
After all, the South African team advances to the 1995 World Cup.
Freeman worked for several years to bring Mandela's story to the cinema screen.
He had no other goal other than to play this role as closely as possible to reality, Freeman said.
The biggest challenge, of course, was to speak like him.
The actor said that if he and the world politician were to be in the same country, he would try to meet Mandela, have dinner with him, and be behind-the-scenes before his speech.
The most important thing for him was to shake Handlanda's hand.
I've realized that when I shake your hand, I'll take over your energy, it's transmitted, and I've had the feeling I know what you're feeling," he said.
To me, it's important to have become a different person.
Matt Damon portrays François Pienaar, captain of the national rugby team in which the white players prevailed.
The actor said he had six months to prepare for the role in the tough rugby world.
It was a big surprise for him when he first met Pienaar at his house.
I remember when I rang the doorbell that he opened and the first thing I said to François Pienaar in my life was, 'In the movie I look much bigger'.
Despite the obvious differences in the stature of the actors compared to their actual role models, under the direction of director Eastwood "Invictus: Unbeaten," he has received positive reviews and one also speaks of prospects for an Oscar.
Daily Variety film critic Todd McCarthy summed up his evaluation in the following words: "That was very good story, very well executed film."
In the film reviews on the web pages rottentomatoes.com, Invictus has received 76 percent positive evaluations.
Light secremy over Norway dissolved: The Russians have tested a missile
A special light effect of unknown origin could be observed over Norway.
The Russian Defense Ministry only yesterday acknowledged that an intercontinental ballistic missile had been tested not far from the Norwegian border.
However, the launch was again unsuccessful and the missile, referred to as type "Bulava," which was originally supposed to be the pride of the Russian army, will slowly become the nightmare of the Russian generality as well as the prime minister, Vladimir Putin.
Also, his repeated attendance on site in the rocket test had no positive effect on the success of the test flights.
Bulava has mostly not been launched at all or damaged in the air.
In the Russian newspapers one already writes about "Bulava" as about a "rocket that does not fly".
A rocket that doesn't fly, but glows
This already 13th attempt, proceeded normally at the beginning.
Only at the end of the flight, technical damage to the rocket occurred again.
Presumably, the engine exploded in the third rocket stage.
This time "Bulava" took off the nuclear submarine Dmitry Donsky, which sailed under the waters of the White Sea.
This type of missile allows firing from the submarine.
That's why it's probable that the mysterious light caused by an indefinite flying object over northern paths was actually the damaged "Bulava."
In addition, the Norwegians have not doubted since the first moment that it was a Russian missile.
This time, the generality defended itself against it, claiming that one cannot clearly speak of a failure.
The first two stages of the rocket did not fail and only the third stage was affected by the accident.
In the previous cases, the engines in the first stage were damaged.
Even if there was no test start without errors, only six out of thirteen consider it unsuccessful.
The almost fifty percent success therefore fills the generality with optimism and so it is still claimed that the "Bulava" will once fly without problems and even carry up to ten hypersonic nuclear warheads with a total weigh of a maximum of 1.15 tons.
The legendary Fetisov has signed an annual contract with CSKA Moscow at the age of 51
The legendary defender Vjaceslav Fetisov introduces himself again in a professional hockey game at the age of 51.
The former world champion, Olympic champion and Stanley Cup owner is helping his team CSKA Moscow in need, and is probably already competing in Friday's duel KHL against Petersburg.
Fetisov, who completed his career at the age of forty, in 1998, is currently president of CSKA.
After Denis Kuljas was injured, we urgently need another defender.
Fetisov has trained on a regular basis and agreed that he will support the team.
We only need to clarify some remaining formalities, the agency AP quoted the coach of Sergei Nemcinov's Moscow club.
Meanwhile, it's not clear whether the comeback of the famous hockey player's comeback will affect only one game.
I mean that Fetisov is one of those who can raise the morale of other players, listed fellow player Nemcinov.
When Fetisov comes back to the ice surface, he will become the oldest Russian professional hockey game according to AP.
In the NHL, legendary striker Gordie Howe has played an entire season for Hartford at the age of fifty-one and was able to take advantage of 15 hits and 26 assistants.
The oldest of the famous Five, Fetisov, Kasatonov - Makarov, Larionov, Krutov, is one of the best and most successful hockey players in history.
In the dress of the Soviet Union, he has won two Olympic gold medals, seven world titles and he has also triumphed in Canada Cup as well as in the Junior World Cup.
In the second part of his career, he also garnered successes in the NHL, where he lifted the Stanley Cup over his head twice in the Detroit dress.
He has been a member of the ice hockey star room since 2001.
Hyundai yielded to unions. All overtimes are lifted
The management of Nosovice-Automobilfabrik Hyundai agreed with the union on the cancellation of all overtime.
Due to frequently ordered overtime, the union announced the willingness to strike on Monday.
This was confirmed by the spokesman for the Hyundai automobile factory, Petr Vanek.
However, the management wants to agree with the trade unionists on a Saturday shift this week.
We must not give up our manufacturing plan, which is a principle, that's whay voluntary overtimony was suggested on Saturday, December 19, for the reason that two working shifts would be worked, Vanek said.
Sedmicka.cz wrote that the company offered a bonus of CZK to employees who would work the Saturday shift.
Employees will also receive this in retrospect for the Saturday shift of 28 November.
According to Vanek, the company has decided to change the motivation of the employees for the proposed overtime.
Either they can pay the overtime surcharge or they can take replacement holidays on December 28 and thus extend the Christmas holidays, Vanek listed.
In addition, all employees of the Saturday shift are paid the fare and lunch.
With regard to the fact that the overtime hours were the most urgent and burning point with regard to demands and complaints of the trade unionists, who had ceased production last week, the management decided yesterday afternoon with immediate effect on the cancellation of all overtime for the month of December, Vaněk pointed out.
The employees of the automobile factory had been forced until last week to work almost every day after the eight-hour shift.
From the signing of the agreement, the willingness to strike by the trade unions is revoked.
Further negotiations between the management of the automobile factory and the trade unionists are due to take place on Friday afternoon.
The union leader, Petr Kuchar, said on Wednesday that if they can agree on meeting the demands and sign a joint document, they would be ready to pick up the willingness to strike.
The situation in the automobile factory worsened last week, on Wednesday, when about 400 employees spontaneously went on strike because of constant overtime.
They also protested against the poor payment conditions and alleged harassment.
The management argued that the employees had to make overtime to comply with the automotive supply requirements.
The unionists also demanded in their Monday strike readiness declaration that the employees, who interrupted production last Wednesday, would not be slapped with sanctions.
In the area of overtime, unions are calling for overtime work to be reduced to a minimum.
In addition, they are demanding this year the remuneration of 5000 kronor to pay out.
Nosovice automobile factory Hyundai now employs 2000 people.
Serial production was started at the company in November last year.
Until this September, about 80,000 vehicles have been manufactured here, the current production capacity is 200,000 vehicles per year.
Czech discovery: a substance that also works in ́thesied ́ HIV viruses
The team of Czech and German scientists is testing new compounds designed to prevent the spread of HIV viruses in the organism.
The main advantage of the improved substance is that it also works on the viruses, which are already resistant to drugs.
This fact outweighs the disadvantage that the compound does not act as strongly on conventional virus variants as one of the already existing drugs.
The disease AIDS cannot be cured yet.
However, the meducation compilation can prolonge the patient’s life and prevent the multiplication of HIV viruses in the body.
However, your intake is accompanied by a whole range of side effects.
Incomplete suppression of virus multiplication also results in the development of so-called resistent viruses, against which no drugs are no longer effective.
The work of the experts of three institutes of the Academy of Sciences CR, of the VSCHT (Chemical-technological university) Prague and the University of Heidelberg opens the way to deal with virus resistance.
They have demonstrated that the substances referred to as metalocarborans act on the protein responsible for the propagation of the HIV virus.
The metalocarborans are compounds of boron, hydrogen, carbon and cobalt.
These compounds block virus multiplication in a different way other way than any medicines used today, so they can overcome the resistance problem.
In their work, the scientists arrive at new ́improved ́ compounds that have been prepared on the basis of the knowledge of the molecular mechanism of their binding to the virus protein.
The metalocarborans have a unique three-dimensional structure: two multiplied cages consisting of boron, hydrogen and carbon atoms and coupled by a metal atom, in this case cobalt.
Insidious and persistent virus
HIV protease is protein of the HIV virus that is essential for the life cycle of the virus.
Without the HIV virus cleavage caused by HIV protease, no ripe infection virus particles would emerge.
If we turn off the HIV protease, we also turn off the multiplication of the virus in the patient's body, the scientists explain in the press report issued by the Institute of Organic Chemistry and Biochemistry of the Academy of Sciences CR.
In the thesis, which was published in the Journal of Medicinal Chemistry, the scientists describe entire series of compounds in which two pairs of the cages (see table) are coupled by a short organic chain, which is then systematically changed.
Slightly weaker, but more reliable
The effect of this range of substances against HIV protease has been tested in the test tube, even against their resistant (resistant) variants obtained from HIV-infected patients.
The effect of metalocarborans on the usual enzyme variant is not as strong as with the clinically used medicinal products, but it does not lose its effect on the resistance variants against which the drugs used are often ineffective.
The unique mechanism of action as well as its further properties, such as the biological and chemical stability, low toxicity and possibility of further chemical formations, makes the metalocarborans interesting compounds for further research directed towards the development of effective drugs against HIV, says Pavlina Rezacova, head of the Laboratory of Structural Biology of the Institutes of Organic Chemistry and Biochemistry and the Institute of Molecular Genetics of the Academy of Sciences.
Dispute over budget. ODS went after Fischer, who fights back
ODS chairman Mirek Topolanek described the approved budget for next year as "cruel" and the government of Jan Fischer's government should, in his opinion, consider its further existence.
The ODS's statement, the prime minister described as "strong pronouncements."
It's supposed to take time for analysis.
Deputy ODS chairman, Petr Necas, said the concept of civil servants' cabinet, with support from the CSSD, ODS and Green Party, obviously stopped working.
According to Necas, the Prime Minister must decide whether he intends to rely on the minority in parliament when it comes to the fight against the rising deficit, or whether he wants to remain in office with the help of the parliamentary left.
The approach of the ODS and the Social Democrats to the problem of public deficits is diametrically deviating, Necas pointed out.
Parliament adopted the budget for 2010 on Wednesday, but with Left Party amendment proposals for more than 12 billion crowns.
The parliament has increased the money for civil servant salaries, for the maintenance of social benefits and for direct payments to farmers.
In doing so, the government has countered the Social Democracy's proposals that they covertly increase the budget deficit.
The Czech Republic was supposed to operate with a backlog of 163 billion crowns.
The ODS left the hall before the vote, so it has complied with the government's wish, because even the modified budget is only a less bad variant of the budget provision.
Disappointment and futility
The finance minister, Eduard Janota, has spoken about his disappointment and a sense of futility, whereas Prime Minister Jan Fischer has argued that the government is still assessing the current situation.
Necas is convinced that if Janota had seriously threatened with his resignation in the event that the amendments would be accepted, the situation would look different.
According to Czech television, Janota has registered with the Premier for Monday after the business of government.
In doing so, he hinted that he is considering his further stay in office.
The reason for this is his disappointment with the handling of the budget.
According to Necas, the nominants of Social Democracy (CSSD), who had enforced the changes in the budget have nothing to do in the government to the extent to which Fischer advocates for the further struggle with the budget.
The Premier should also say clearly that he will resign if parliament continues to adopt deficit-enhancing laws, for example those that affect sick pay and pensions, or the service law amendment.
Topolanek: Ministers nominated by ODS didn't be wrong
This is a matter for Prime Minister Fischer and the whole government.
The ministers nominated by the ODS have made no mistakes, Topolanek has emphasized in a text message from the USA.
It mentioned that in some ministries in a deputy capacity there are people who profess TOP 09, who voted against the budget.
Necas emphasized that if Fischer decides to renounce the fight against the deficit level of public finances, "it is perfectly logical and correct that he relies on the left-wing majority, which is currently in parliament."
Then allegedly he can't reckon with the support and tolerance of citizen Democrats.
I haven't thought about it, and if that's going to be up-to-date then I'm going to deal with that," Defense Minister Martin Bartak, nominated by the Citizen Democrats, told journalists in parliament.
Premier Fischer flew to Brussels in the afternoon at the European Council.
The ODS resigned at the request of the Premier
Necas stressed at the same time that the ODS would probably vote against the amended budget in parliament on Wednesday, should the prime ministers explicitly ask Citizen Democrats to make it possible to adopt the budget.
We don't feel like orphans, we are not young children, so he then responded to the question of whether the ODS was not pushed back at the political negotiation on the budget due to the absence of the party chairman in parliament as well as in the Czech Republic.
I didn't realize my voice was missing.
Even when I renounced the mandate, I knew that in the next few months it would be mainly about Bolshevisation of Parliament and all the positives, Topolanek himself reacted.
According to the new timetable of the CD (Czech Railway), fewer trains will run for about the same fare.
The Czech railway ends or restricts from 13 December some less busy high-speed and local trains, while other connections will be strengthened.
The total scope of traffic will be reduced by two percent from the date of validity of the new timetable compared to today.
Most tariffs, including basic and customer travel fares, will not be changed.
For example, the inhabitants of the Ostrava area receive several connection options to the capital, the railway uses trains of the Pendolino type for this route.
The express train will no longer go to Bratislava (Pressburg).
Fewer express trains will also run on the Prague - Pisek - Budweis route, in principle the direct connections from Prague to Letohrad will be reduced.
The railway plans the largest reduction in the district of Hradec Kralove (Königsgraz) by eight percent, while Prague and the surrounding area can look forward to an increase.
There will also be two night trains on the Prague - Tabor - Budweis route and to a few days a week will also be reduced the operation on the Prague - Pisek - Budweis route.
One of the biggest changes is the introduction of the new direct route from Milovice to Prague, currently the inhabitants of Milovice have to change to Lysa nad Labem.
It is the fifteenth transport route of the Esko suburban transport system, whose opening is possible thanks to railway electrification from Lysa nad Labem to Milovice.
The long-distance routes to the capital will end at the destination station Prague - Hauptbahnhof.
The CD introduces more connecting lines SC Pendolino between Prague and Ostrava and the trains to Sumperk/Jesenik will run at intervals of two hours.
Passengers have not been able to travel to Bratislava with the Pendolino since 13 December.
This year's bill for regional railway: an increase of 200 million
The express trains are ordered and paid for by the Ministry of transport. In totall, the railway will receive four billion crowns to settle the so-called liquidity loss next year, exactly like this year. The local trains supply the districts, in totall, they will pay eight billion crowns next year, with the state shooting three billions.
This year, the regional trains are 200 million Czech crowns more expensive.
For the local trains as well as the express trains, the railway has recently concluded a ten-year contract, until now the contract has only been concluded for one year.
The railway praises the system, but perhaps for this reason it did not resort to increase the price for the vast majority of tickets.
Changes occur only with one-day network tickets CD Net and for low-cost internet tickets eLiska.
For passengers without a customer card, the CD Net price will be increased from 450 crowns to 600 crowns.
The eLiska prize will then depend on the ridden track, until now it was uniform for the journey between any district cities and cost 160 crowns.
Philippine militants arrest more than 50 people, the children were released.
Millitants kidnapped 75 people today in the southern Philippines, including some elementary pupils and their teachers.
This was cited by the agency AFP, which originally informed only about 65 abductees.
All 17 children detained were released after eight hours, including one teacher.
The kidnapping took place in the province of Agusan del Sur.
Approximately 19 militants allegedly used the hostages as a living shield while fleeing from the police.
Local negotiators are now making an effort to release the remaining hostage.
According to the agency AFP, the millitants belong to the New People's Army (NPA), this is the arms faction of the Communist Party of the Philippines (CPP).
According to AP, the police prosecuted the kidnappers because of the violence, which demanded acts of revenge between the two local family clans.
In the south of the Philippines, in Maguindanao province, the right was imposed because of the recent massacre that killed 57 people.
Among the victims of the November 23 massacre related to the election to the governor's office were 30 journalists.
On suspicion of participating in the massacre, the governor of this southern Philippine province and his father Andal Ampatuan, patriarch of the influential Ampatuan clan, were arrested.
Muslim separatists are also active on Mindanao Island.
However, they resumed peace negotiations with the Philippine government on Tuesday.
South Africa's history affects everyone, says Morgan Freeman aka Nelson Mandela
Over the next few days, the new Hollywood film Clint Eastwoods arrives in American theatries "Invictus: the Undefeated," which includes part of the life story of the former President of the Republic of South Africa, Nelsona Mandela.
The role of the world-famous fighter against apartheid has received Morgan Freeman, who had already been allegedly chosen by Mandela himself a few years ago.
The supporting actor role of the legendary South African champion in rugby, Francois Pienaar, has been given to Matt Damon.
With the film's theatrical release, reactions from critics as well as the public are also loud.
Nelson Mandela
His full name is Nelson Rolihlaha Mandela, born on July 18, 1918 in Mvez, South Africa.
In 1988 he received the Sakhar Prize, in 1993 together with de Klerk the Nobel Peace Prize.
He was elected President of the Republic of South Africa in 1994-1999.
Great expectations are placed in the film because it comes to cinemas at a time when preparations for the World Cup culminate in South Africa on the one hand, but at the same time the media shake the world with frightening news about rising racial hatred and the discrimination of residents on the part of the black-ruling majority.
According to the AP news agency, the reactions to the new film in South Africa are consistently positive for the time being, despite numerous reservations about the cast of leading roles with American Hollywood stars.
Morgan Freeman is nevertheless proud of the new film.
I have dreamed about this role for many years and have prepared well for it.
Several times I also met Nelson Mandela personally, so that I can - as they say - receive his energy, he performed in the interview after the festival premiere of the film in Los Angeles.
I think it's good if we can reminiscent of Mandela's personality and his message with this film.
This is a history of South Africa, but it affects every person, he added.
In the dress of the rugby national team
The film is based on the book template by British writer John Carlin, freely translated ́In the role of the enemy ́: Nelson Mandela and the game that united the people, portrays the South African advocate of human rights within his first presidential election period.
In the course of the film, Morgan Freeman strives to bring together two racial and for year-and-day demarcated population groups and, like Nelson Mandela in 1995, he also steps onto the World Cup field in his role in the South African national rugbydress, which South Africa won at the time.
For entering the lawn in the dress, which in South Africa applies almost like an ordinance, Mandela reaped great ovations fourteen years ago, predominantly from the white audience and has thus won the recognition of the white population.
The rugby championship perhaps makes it to unite people into a single people in turn, the rugby player Chester Williams, who in 1995 was the only "black" on the national team.
Whether the tense situation in South Africa improves, that will only become apparent.
However, the white South Africans are sceptical, as revealed, for example, in a recent interview for LN the rugby fan Christopher Dawson, who fled from the country to Britain.
The championship starts on June 11, 2010.
In Louny area, transformers burn for several 10 million crowns
The fire broke out in the morning at the transformer of the company Ceps in the switchgear near the municipality of Vyskov in the Louny area.
In the case of the fire, according to the spokesman of the district fire brigade Usti nad Labem, Lukas Marvan, six units intervened.
Originally, the fire brigade wanted to burn out the transformers, but later it changed the view and now the fire is extinguished with a mixture of water - foam and special extinguishing agents.
The cause was a technical defect, a transformer internal short circuit.
No one was violated, according to the company spokeswoman, Pavla Mandatova, did not jeopardize the safety operation of the pipeline system.
The damage was estimated at several 10 million crowns, more precisely the damage can only be quantified after the fire.
According to Mandatova, the destroyed transformer must be replaced by a new transformer.
For more than 10 years, nothing similar has happened at the company Ceps, mistakes of this species are rare, she added.
According to the spokeswoman of the company CEZ for Northern Bohemia, Sona Holingerova, the power supply was not interrupteded, due to the fire of the transformer station, the functionality of households, authorities and companies was not affected.
A.G. Ceps operates on the Czech territory as the sole operator of the pipeline system, the electrical cables of 400 kV and 220 kV.
It maintains, renovates and develops 39 switchgears with 67 transformers, with which the electrical energy is transferred into the distribution system and wiring section with a length of 4339 kilometers.
Jancura files criminal charges for defamation against three heads of the CSSD.
The owner of the student agency, Radim Jancura, is filing a criminal complaint today for defamation against three socio-democratic leaders.
Against Michal Hasek from the District of Southern Moravia, Radko Martinek from Pardubice district and Olomouc district, Martin Tesarik.
Jancura said this in a twenty-minute interview on the radio journal.
The lawsuit is related to the firm's effort to operate regional train services in some districts.
However, all regions ultimately signed the long-term treaties with the Czech Railway.
All three ladders attack us and say that we take out the raisins, in doing so we have only made the offer on the extent that it was coordinated with them.
They claim that we are expensive as the Czech railway because we would know the prices of the Czech Railway, which were completed last week.
So we have no choice but to defend ourselves.
Because of this slandering of the Society Student Agency, Jancura told the Radiojournal.
At the same time, he argued that he has evidence from all districts that they have taken their steps in accordance with European law.
This is why we are preparing an indictment for the European Commission.
Hasek considers the collection of the criminal complaint for the further continuation of Jancura's media show, which serves him as a free advertisement for his society as well as for his own person.
Of course, I am ready to defend my good name as well as the good reputation of my district, and I also call on other leaders.
I assume that it will also become clear on this matter that it is Jancura who manipulates the public and uses lying and untrue arguments in the matter of regional rail transport, Hasek said.
Jancura already reported last week that he had invested several million crowns in advertising, which will point to the disadvantageous step of the districts.
He told the radio journal that he interrupts the campaign after Christmas and intends to continue it after New Year.
He clarified that he will lead the campaign against corruption as such.
Jancura initiated the campaign after the districts reached an agreement with the Czech Railway at the instigation of regional railway traffic and had not tendered any tenders for the contract in a high by 150 billion crowns.
According to Vancura, such conduct is unlawful and contrary to the requirements of the European Court of Justice.
Because of the criticism of train traffic in the districts, according to Jancura, he was already threatened by a member of the CSSD.
The only threat was if we do not forgo that we can then also lose bus licenses.
He introduced himself as a member of the CSSD, I know who it is, I don't say his name, Jancura told the Radiojournal today.
Vaccination against pneumococcia free and voluntary
Immunization against pneumococci will change the usual from January.
It should be the first for the families to be free and voluntary.
But even now, votes are reporting that compulsory vaccination would be more effective.
The preparations for the introduction of free vaccination against pneumococcus culminate.
The medicine institute is already setting the maximum prices of the vaccines that health insurance companies have to pay.
The experts are also aware of the vaccination procedure.
New Vaccination
Vaccines free of charge for August children
From January, children can be vaccinated free of charge against pneumococci, who were born on August 2 last year and later.
The condition is that they have not yet received a vaccine dose.
If you have already started vaccination last year or earlier, you also have to pay for further repeat vaccination yourself.
The first vaccine dose must be given between the 3rd and 5th months of the child's life.
Last year, a quarter of families had their children vaccinated against pneumococcus.
The parents paid 1600 krowers and more for one dose.
Four doses are needed to vaccinate the youngest children.
On the whole, the experts estimate that the health insurance companies spend 300,000 to 450,000 crowns a year on vaccination doses.
However, they save on the treatment that the children would need without vaccination.
Until now, the state has spended about 500,000 per year on all area vaccinations of the children.
The obligatory vaccination was introduced with the so-called Janota package.
Pneumococci cause middle ear infection, but also serious infections including cerebrinal inflammation and blood poisoning.
Pneumococcal infections kill up to 28 children aged up to 10 every year after examination in hospitals.
Outside the Czech Republic, the compulsory vaccination of children against pneumococcus has been introduced in about 40 countries.
Will vaccination be mandatory?
At the same time, it is said that this vaccination could be mandatory in the future.
From January, this should be paid by the insurance companies and voluntary.
Rudolf Kalovsky, for example, mentioned that the compulsory vaccination would be better.
His association is already promoting pneumococcal vaccination in the long term.
The professionals at the international conference in which our association was involved were not very satisfied that we have voluntary vaccination.
This isn't quite ideal.
On the other hand, we are glad that we managed to enforce at least that.
I think that's the path we have to take now, Kalovsky said.
However, the Ministry of Health does not want to change the vaccination procedure against pneumococcia.
At this moment we are not considering this at all, said his spokesman Vlastimil Sesen.
Voluntary vaccination has its drawbacks, doctors claim.
Hana Cabrnochova, chairwoman of the society of practical pediatricians, admits that voluntary vaccination has its drawbacks.
If all children were vaccinated, one could think about the reduction of the number of repeat vaccinations "In a situation where the vaccine is so expensive, we understand the political decision that this vaccination has been started as a voluntary one," says Cabrnochova.
Experts expect more than 80 percent of parents to vaccinate their infants against pneumococcus from next year.
Vaccination against pneumococci is also groundbreaking in the fact that it is paid for as the first area vaccination by the health insurance companies and not by the state.
The insurance companies should spend the money on prevention, which saves them for further treatment, Cabrnochova explains.
Doctors have to buy the vaccines themselves.
However, this entails a concern of the practical pediatricians, which concerns the purchase of vaccines.
According to the original project, the vaccines should be purchased by the insurance companies and doctors should receive them in the same way as other vaccines issued by the state to them.
However, some MPs feared that the insurance companies "could "carry out the money from health insurance."
That's why doctors will have to buy the vaccines themselves and wait for the insurance companies to pay them.
According to the estimates, the individual practical doctors will have to invest 40,000 crowns per month, which is a significant sum.
This is not going to become easy at all, Cabrnochova says.
U.S. artist's son involved in 'museum theft'
The son of famous American fantasy artist Frank Frazetta has been accused of attempting to steal $20 million worth of paintings from his father's museum.
According to Pennsylvania police, Alfonso Frank Frazetta was captured while inviting 90 paintings into his vehicle with a trailer.
It's said Frank Frazetta junior and another man had used an excavator to enter the museum in the Pocono mountains.
81-year-old Frank Frazetta Senior is famous for his depiction of figures such as Conan the Barbarian and Tarzan.
According to the Associated Press reported, the artist was staying in Florida at the time of the incident.
AP quoted an unnamed police officer who said Frank Frazetta Junior may have been motivated by a family feud.
The agency reported, according to a police affidavit, Frazetta Junior claimed he was instructed by his father "that he should invade the museum by any means to take all the paintings to a camp."
The agency said Frank Frazetta denied Senior having given any permission.
Labour defends budget and tax increases
Ministers have defendeded the tax rises and spending freezes announced in the pre-review report against criticism from the opposition, the economy and trade unions.
Conservatives said Alistair Darling had "elapsed" an opportunity to show that he was serious about reducing the deficit by pushing out the decisions until after the election.
The Chancellor of the Exchequer also came under fire for targeting low and middle-income workers.
His main proposals include a 0.5 per cent increase in social security and a 1 per cent capping limit on public wage settlements for 2011.
social security annoyance
The unions have protested that low-wage earners will be punished for a recession they did not cause and warned of imminent "problems."
Raising social security to about £3 billion a year has angered the private sector, which says this is a tax on jobs even though attention should be on the economic upswing.
The increase, which is limited to those earning more than £20,000 a year, will affect about 10 million workers.
FOCUS OF THE ADVACANT BUDGET REPORT
Increasing social security by a further 0.5% from April 2011
This year's economy shrinking by more than the expected 4.75%
New 50% tax on banking bonuses
1 percent increase in corporation tax discarded for small businesses
Tax rebates for electric vehicles and wind turbines
2.5% increase in state pensions
According to Treasury estimates, someone with an annual income of £30,000 will be £90 a year poorer and someone at £40,000 per year will be £190 poorer, while a person with £10,000 annual incomes will be £110 richer.
Ministers said their goal of mitigating by 2013 meant "difficult decisions," but they also said that 60% of the burden of additional taxes would account for the 5% of top earners.
Treasury Secretary Stephen Timms denied that the tax rises outlined and spending stoppages would be "a drop in the bucket" compared to the necessary measures.
"These are big numbers. They are going to bring us the deficit halving and this is also absolutely necessary over the period of the next four years," he told the BBC.
Timms said he was confident the economy would return to growth by the end of the year.
Predictions of the Labour Party about future economic growth – which will affect the scope of new debt and the rapidity of deficit rebatriation – were questioned after the Chancellor of the Exchequer was forced to revise previous figures.
He said in his opinion that the economy is likely to shrink by 4.75% this year - far more than the 3.5% decline predicted in April - while debt would be €3 billion higher than previously estimated.
'Failed Opportunity'
Conservatives, who want to launch a new advertising campaign on Thursday to warn of Labour's "debt crisis," said the planned £789bn debt was unsustainable over the next six years.
They added that ministers have failed to put forward a credible plan on how they will repay this and "cynically" squeeze before difficult decisions until after the election - which must take place at the latest in June 2010.
"They didn't say anything about what they're going to do," Shadow Finance Minister Philip Hammond said. "They had their opportunity to do so and they've let them pass."
“The significant announcements will not be made until after the election, whoever wins them.”
Roger Bootle, Deloitte
Labour insists that schools, hospitals and the police be saved from future cuts, unlike the Conservatives' proposals, which see deficit capping faster and sooner.
But the Liberal Democrats said that the money gained through the tax increases and spending freezes will be used to cover spending for next year, not to reduce the deficit, and therefore argue that the plans were "built on sand."
The BBC's political editor Nick Robinson said that at the timing of the announcements - such as the above inflation adjustment on some disability pensions next April - the Chancellor of the Exchequal would expose the Chancellor to the accusation of vote-catching, since the money could simply be retrieved next year.
Labour insists to provide the extra aid when people need it most and that the situation will be re-examined in September 2010.
'Hollow budget'
Although ministers haven't announced any budget details beyond 2011, economists say pressure on public spending won't come to fruition until medium-term.
The recognized Institute for Tax Investigations said the Chancellor's figures would mean hefty smears in many areas, possibly in the areas of transportation, higher education, science and defence in the future.
"It's shifting the pain only until later," his leader Robert Chote said.
"The significant announcements won't be made until after the election, whoever it wins them," added Roger Bootle, economic adviser at accounting firm Deloitte.
"This was just the hollow advance budget report. The markets recognize that."
FBI investigates 'arrests of U.S. citizens in Pakistan'
The FBI is investigating the arrest of five reported U.S. citizens on suspicion of extremist links in Pakistan.
The men were arrested in a raid on a home in Sarghoda, in the eastern Panjab province, the American embassy in Pakistan told the BBC.
The FBI said it is investigating whether these are the same men who were reported missing last month in the U.S. state of Virginia.
The U.S. State Department said they were also looking for information about the men.
Three of them are said to be Pakistani descent, one is of Egyptian origin and the other has a Yemeni background.
"If they are American citizens, of course, we are very interested in the charges they are accused of and under what circumstances they are being detained," spokesman Ian Kelly said.
FBI spokeswoman Katherine Schweit said the agency was aware of the arrests and was linked to the missing students' families.
"We are working with the Pakistani authorities to find out their identities and the purpose of their stay there, in case it's actually the missing students," she said.
The Pakistani embassy in Washington said the men were arrested at a house belonging to the uncle of one of the students.
He said local police had been interested in the house earlier and that no charges have yet been filed against the arrested men.
The Reuters news agency reported that Secretary of State Hillary Clinton declined to comment on the arrests, but she said the U.S. must "work more closely with Afghanistan and Pakistan to exterminate the infrastructure of terrorism that continues to recruit and train people."
The five students were reported missing by their families in northern Virginia at the end of November.
Amnesty Condemns 'Abuses' in Iran
According to a report by the human rights group Amnesty International, human rights in Iran is still just as bad as in the past 20 years.
The report detailed "abuse patterns" by the regime, both before and after the contested presidential elections in June.
One man quoted in the report said he was beaten and burned with cigarettes. Another said he was threatened with rape.
Iran dismissed previous criticism of its human rights record.
Official agencies said such criticism was politically motivated.
After the contested election of President Mahmoud Ahmadinejad, thousands of people were arrested and dozens were killed in Iran, leading to the largest street protests since the 1979 Islamic revolution.
Dozens were given imprisonments and prosecutors said at least five people were sentenced to death.
BBC Tehran correspondent Jon Leyne, who is now located in London, said that at the start of the protests, the highest leader, Ayatollah Ali Khamenei, acknowledged some of the abuse claims and ordered the closure of the Kahrizak detention centre.
But since then, there has been no longer any tolerance for criticism from the authorities, our correspondent said.
"False Confession"
Amnesty International quoted 26-year-old computer science student Ebrahim Mehtari, who said he was charged with "working with Facebook networks" and for protesting the election results.
"They slapped me in the face more often," he was quoted.
"I was burned with cigarettes under my eyes, in the neck, on the head ... they threatened me with death and they humiliated me."
After five days, he signed a false confession and, still bleeding and semi-unconscious, was released on the street, Amnesty said.
Beaten presidential candidate Mehdi Karroubi said some protesters who had been arrested after the election were tortured to death in prison in August and others raped.
Iranian authorities denied the rape allegations, but admitted that abuses took place.
Amnesty also cited the case of a former detainee who said he had been held with 75 others more than eight weeks in a container at a detention centre in Kahrizak.
Amnesty acknowledged that Iran's parliament and judicial authorities had set up a committee to investigate the unrest after the election and the government's responses to it, but according to Amnesty, the mandates and power of these bodies would have been unclear and the results that would have been won by the parliamentary committees had not been published.
The group said at least 90 people have been arrested in the past three weeks to prevent further demonstrations.
Ski slope has been set up in the Stadtpark
More than 100 tonnes of artificial snow have been used to turn a landmark in Bristol into a competitive slope.
The ski slope of this parking event attracted 16 semi-professional skiers and snowboarders fighting for cash prizes.
On the top of the slope in Brandon-Hill-Park, there was a short ski jump at the beginning of a 100-meter-long slope.
Student John Hickman, who studied medicine in her fourth year, said there's been a gorgeous view from the top of the slope, but it was also quite intimidating.
It's a great view with all of the city's lights glittering below.
He added: "It's some of the country's best skiers and snowboarders here - some proper talent."
Prizes have been awarded for the best feats performed on the slope - or tricks.
After Thursday night's event, the snow will be left to melting.
Still she and us
Can Isslam French?
pluralism and pragmatism in a secular state.
At a time when Swiss voters have voted in favour of a ban on minarets and concerns about the alleged Islamization of Europe are widespread, John Bowen, an American academic, has written a well-founded and deliberate account of whether Muslims can be integrated into one of Europe's declaredly secularized societies - and integrated.
Some readers will read this new book out of admiration for the author's last work: "Why the French Don't Love Headscarves" (2006), an elegant and densely arguing study of a contentious question that divided and occupied the country over a decade and a half, and whose effects can still be perceived today.
Bowen's latest book offers a broader and bolder spectrum.
As a good anthropologist, he not only wants to know what the politicians and the media are saying about Islam in France, but what is really happening in the field.
He has spent months in mosques, schools and institutes that now offer "Islamic freedoms" to the 5 to 6 million Muslims in France, as Bowen calls it.
He's a good listener, he's reflecting debates between teachers and students about the questions that worry them the most.
Should a Muslim get married (or both) in a mosque or town hall?
Should young Muslims be instructed about the evolution and rights of homosexuals?
Can a Muslim woman marry a non-Muslim man?
Is it permissible for a Muslim to use an interest-based banking system to get a mortgage?
It's these seemingly profane matters, he claims, that make up daily life, quite contrary to the political dramas that preoccupy the media.
The author recognizes a new generation of imams, teachers and intellectuals, none of them with a well-known name, possibly with the exception of Tarik Ramadan, a Swiss-born Arabic-Muslim scholar and academic.
This new generation is trying to open up the debate about how it is possible to be a good Muslim on the one hand and a good citizen in a modern secular society on the other.
They don't have all the arguments on their side.
Conservatives are suspicious of the idea of a French or European Islam.
The thinkers and activists that Bowen surveyed are mostly disagreeing with their Salafi counterparts – advocates of purist Sunni Islam associated with Saudi Arabia – who nowadays show a small but influential presence among Europe’s Muslims.
Bowen is of the opinion that Muslim values and French secularism could be compatible.
But an agreement requires giving and taking on both sides.
He questions the extent to which French political decision-makers (and the intellectual elite, which so passionately guarded the separation of church and state) are truly committed to pluralism.
He suggests that Muslims are possibly undergoing harsher treatment than Catholics, Protestants and Jews, who in turn had to make their historic compromise with secular republicanism.
He recognizes a "tightening of the screws of values" rather than a growing pragmatism.
Can Islam be French? After reading this book, one is inclined to say "Yes, but not yet."
World's bleast outlook
The Arctic is changing faster and more dramatically than any other environmental region on Earth.
The ice that characterizes them melts with alarming speed and takes life with it that can't survive anywhere else.
Oil, gas, shipping and fishing interests have entered the recently open water, with diplomats, lawyers and now also authors in their keel water.
Richard Ellis, an author and illustrator, paints a natural history of the icon of the north, the polar bear in "On Thin Ice" (original title: "On Thin Ice").
Landscaped in the complicated history and politics of whaling, he describes the long tradition of Arctic explorers, who have proved themselves when hunting the polar bears.
Admiral Nelson's encounter with a polar bear as a brave 14-year-old ensign to the sea, who only struggled with the rifle butt of his musket, is certainly a legend, but others are true.
Young bears caught swimming behind the bodies of their recently slain mothers were taken to zoos and circuses.
A circus performance at the beginning of the 20th century showed 75 polar bears at a performance.
Even today, zoo facilities still typically own one-millionth of the habitat of a wild-living old animal. The climate is rarely suitable: the polar bear in Singapore became quite green from the algae that grows in the hollow hair of its fur.
Click here to find out more!
Ellis refers to the accounts of other authors and historians and often returns to the threat that hunters pose to the survival of the species.
Although banned in Norway, America and Russia, succumbing continues in Greenland and Canada, where hunters in helicopters and sleigh-driving Inuit use powerful assault rifles to take down their prey.
Ironically, in the waters in the north of these two hunting nations, the ice will persist for the longest.
In his new book "After the Ice," Alun Anderson, a former editor of the science magazine "New Scientist," offers a clear and disturbing description of the Arctic's science and a gripping insight into how the future might develop.
It’s not that scientists have all the answers.
Neither atmospheric scientists nor oceanographers cannot adequately explain the rapidity of the changes.
However, this is not due to a lack of attempted explanation.
Fridtjof Nansen's pioneering journeys at the end of the 19th and beginning of the 20th centuries suggested for the first time the swirling of the packing.
Submarines in the Cold War measured the shifting ridges when going understaking.
A useful addition are satellite surveys and the large-area application of measuring probes on icebergs, but still much is achieved by intrepid people camping outside in the cold.
Anderson takes a look into the peculiar, tiny world of inflow system within the Arctic ice formed by saltwater troughs that are compressed during freezing.
But, from the bear on the ice to the microscopically small miracles in the ice, everyone is at risk when the summer ice once passes, which is expected between 2013 and 2050.
“In a few months ... this could be us”
Naval infanterists were among the 6,000 wreaths laid in Arlington
Superior De'Angello Robinson traveled for seven hours by bus to place a single wreath on the tomb of a soldier he had never met.
For him and the other navals who undertaked Camp Johnson’s journey in Jacksonville, North Carolina to National Cemetery in Arlington to decorate graves, the day was a tribute to the men and women in whose footsteps they followed.
In a few months, from wherever we're going to be brought back from, we could be, Robinson said of the fallen soldiers.
If we were, I'd wish someone would do the same for me, so I'm trying to show my respect.
He was one of more than 6,000 volunteers who had gathered on Saturday morning to put wreaths on the graves of veterans in several sections of Arlington Cemetery.
Morrill Worcester, owner of Worcester Wreath Companioner Worcester Wreath Company in Harrington, Maine, established this tradition in 1992, when he and some others decided to decorate several hundred tombs in the cemetery in Arlington.
It's now an 18-year-old tradition and Morrill and his wife Karen taking this trip every year, stopping in different cities along the way to hold events dedicated to people in the military and victims of terrorism.
The Worcesters also founded a non-profit organization, Wreath's Across America (wreaths throughout America), which also extended the event to other states.
This year, volunteers placed more than 16,000 wreaths on the graves at Arlington Cemetery, in areas at the Pentagon, on tombs of Fayetteville National Cemetery in Arkansas, Battery-Park in New York, and at the memorial site for United Airlines Flight 93 in Shanksville, Pennsylvania.
The Walmart Foundation donated more than $150,000 for the second time to purchase and transport the wreath.
Charleen Hunt, 70, from Westminster, said she took the trip to Arlington in memory of her husband, a deceased professional soldier.
Although her husband isn't buried in Arlington, Hunt said it's her way to give something back to those who brought victims.
She said that's a small contribution to how we civilians can support our soldiers.
It's just like visiting a family burial site.
Wrapped in layers of warm clothes, the volunteers wandered across the cemetery's extensive lawns and hills.
The volunteers had older tombs from World War 1 and 2, and the Vietnam War as a target, as these are not visited as often as the newer graves, according to the organizers.
The mood was cheerful, children were playing and couples held their hands as they carried the fresh pine branches to the greying tombstones.
Each round wreath was dark green with a small red bow attached to the upper part.
Section 60, which is located nearby and where mainly veterans of the wars are buried in Iraq and Afghanistan, remained nearly quiet.
The United Service Organizations donated 1,000 wreaths to decorate this section, but the bleak mood of the current wars filled this area.
Individuals cried openly, others prayed and families and friends stuck to each other in the icy air.
Some read loudly the names carved in stone, while others bowed down to small black-and-whise square maps that had been poured into the damp mud for those whose tombstones had not yet been completed.
Sandra Lockwood was one of the many mothers shed bitter tears in front of her son's tomb.
She drove 8 hours from Zanesfield, Ohio, to visit the burial site of naval infrantery sergeant David Shane Spicer, who fell in battle in July.
No one should ever forget why we're free ..., my son paid for it, she said.
“When I haven’t been for a long time, I want someone to remember him.”
U.S. government's contractor arrested by Cuban authorities
The Cuban government has arrested a U.S. government contractor that sold mobile phones and notebook computers in the country, State Department officials said Saturday.
The contractor, who has not yet been identified, works for Development Alternatives Inc. based in Bethesda.
The company is working on projects for clients, such as USAID, the U.S. agency for international development and for the World Bank.
Consular officials from U.S. advocacy in Havana are trying to gain access to the detainee, who was arrested on December 5.
The special charges have not yet been published, although under Cuban law a Cuban citizen or a foreign visitor can be arrested for almost anything under the allegation of "endangeration."
All so-called counter-revolutionary activities, which include light protests and critical writings, pose a risk of arrest.
Anti-government graffiti and statements are seen as a serious crime.
Cuba has a growing community of bloggers led by popular reporter Yoani Sánchez, who often describes how she and her husband are persecuted and assaulted by government officials.
Sánchez has repeatedly requested permission to leave the country to receive prizes, but this has always been rejected.
The arrest of an American contractor is likely to boost tensions between the Castro brother's communist government in Cuba and the Obama administration, which had sought a "slow" rapprochement to improve relations with the island.
The news of arrest was first reported Friday night by the New York Times.
The new U.S. policy attaches importance that the United States will provide in return if the Cuban government takes concrete steps in that direction, such as releasing political prisoners and creating more space for opposition.
Mobile phones and notebooks are legal in Cuba, although they are new and sought-after products in a country where a government employee's average earnings are $15 a month.
The Cuban government has only been allowing ordinary citizens to buy a mobile phone since this year, they are mainly used for sending text messages, as a 15-minute phone call would cost a day's wages.
Internet use on the island is extremely restricted.
It is accessible in expensive hotels and there to foreign visitors and in some government sites, such as universities.
Cubans who wish to log in frequently have to report their names to the government.
To many websites, access is restricted.
In Cuba, the arrest of an American is rare.
Most of the few U.S. citizens who are in prison in Cuba are behind bars because of crimes such as drug smuggling, said Gloria Berbena, the press spokeswoman for the U.S. advocacy agency in Havana.
Berbena said she couldn't give any further information on the arrest.
The arrest and detention are clearly wrong.
An activity that would be legal in any other vacant society - the delivery of free mobile phones - is a crime in Cuba, Jose Miguel Vivanco, head of the American section of the "Human Rights Watch" group, which recently brought out a harsh account of freedom in Cuba titled "New Castro, Same Cuba," an allusion to Raul Castro's inauguration as the country's leader, who replaced the sick older brother Fidel.
Vivanco said the defendants were very often arrested, convicted and imprisoned within a day.
He said that probably any solution would be political and that the Cuban government often provokes a negative reaction in the United States, just at the time when both countries are moving towards each other for more willingness to dialogue.
Pakistani government officials uncover plan to send men to Afghanistan
Pakistani authorities on Saturday discovered an allegedly leading head of a conspiracy aimed at sending five men from northern Virginia to Afghanistan to kill U.S. soldiers, saying they hoped this case could help uncover an extensive network of terrorist recruiters searching the internet for radicalized young men.
Investigators said they had been seeking an opaque insurgent, known as Saifullah, who invited the men to Pakistan after he had tracked them down because one of them had made agreeable comments on terrorist attacks on the internet video site YouTube.
Saifullah became the leader of these men after their arrival in Pakistan and attempted to help them reach the remote area in Pakistan's tribal belt, which is home to Al Qaeda and its terrorist training camp.
But a representative of Pakistan's intelligence service who was briefed on the matter said Saifullah failed to convince al-Qaeda leaders that the men aren't part of a CIA conspiracy to subdue the terrorist network.
As a result, they have been held overnight in the eastern city of Sargodha, far from the inhospitable mountains in the northwest, which have become a terrorist refuge.
They were regarded as part of a coverted operation.
That's why they were rejected, said the government official, who wished to remain anonymous because of the sensitivity of the matter.
The government official said the men were by no means discouraged and continued trying to get the right recommendations to gain access to the Al Qaeda camps when they were arrested by the Pakistani penitentiary authorities.
The case of the five men - who continue to be in Pakistan and are questioned by the FBI - underscores the crucial role of recruiters in identifying future terrorists, and possibly more importantly, in deciding who is trustworthy.
Since the attacks of September 11, 2001, the U.S. secret service has made it a matter of the utmost urgency to accommodate human assets within Al Qaeda.
The recruiters of the organizers act as gatekeepers who do not let in those whose commitment to the Holy War is not serious enough and those who could be spies.
American would-be recruits are undergoing special scrutiny by Qaeda, analysts said.
However, due to their potential access to U.S. goals and propaganda value, they are also considered immensely attractive to the group.
Evan Kohlmann, the highest-ranking analyst of the NEFA Foundation based in the U.S., said terrorist groups have also become much more cautious in recent years with regard to who they allow access to, because U.S. intelligence agencies have become experts in their recruitment methods.
Trying to infiltrating someone into these groups, what better path could you go than to follow the recruitment model that so many have already followed?
The model is now much more based on the internet.
Tens of thousands of protesters demand climate "action" in Copenhagen
Tens of thousands of protesters marched through the streets here on Saturday and demanded bolder action on climate protection from negotiators at the city's Bella congress centre.
Protesters said 100,000 people had joined the event, while police estimates were more likely to expect about 25,000.
The event proceeded relatively peacefully, although a handful of masked activists detonated small explosive devices near some government buildings in the city centre.
On a day when little happened at the UN-sponsored climate negotiations, thousands of activists wandered through the city with banners that read in English "There is no Planet B" and one in Spanish that declared "The earth says "Enough."
Some stars joined the protest, for example the Danish model, the photographer Helena Christensen, who said that the trips to Peru, her mother's country of birth, make her aware of the heartbreaking problems the country is struggling with due to the already noticeable effects of climate change.
That's part of the motives why I decided to take part in the large-scale demonstration - to pass on the word and appeal to the world leaders to bring about a fair, ambitious and binding agreement, she said.
It's not an easy task, but it has to be done now and there's no longer a path that leads around it.
Police stopped protesters from approachinging too close to the Bella centre and said they had arrested 19 people, mainly due to the fact that they were either masked or carrying pocket knives.
Under Danish law, these actions are prohibited during demonstrations.
According to a spectator who did not want to be named as he is involved in the climate negotiations, masked protesters in black clothing detonated several explosive devices near Copenhagen's main canal, near which several ministries are located.
They threw them near the buildings, he said, adding that they started as light rockets, but were then followed by "a few major explosions."
Inside the convention center, people gathered in front of television screens to watch the demonstration throughout the afternoon.
But the protest has apparently failed to achieve the awareness of the main officials, such as Su Wie, China's chief climate negotiator.
When he was asked if he thinks that the demonstration would have a constructive effect on international considerations, he replied in English: "That's something I wasn't even aware of".
He then proceeded in Mandarin and said, "Because the venue is large, I can't hear what's happening outside".
He noted that it depended on the individual's perspective whether the demonstration would hurt or help.
It demonstrates the concentration of the general public and various sectors on the issue of climate change, he said.
On the other hand, "you can also say that they interfere with the negotiations or the freedom of other people".
Examination data shows stubborn racial divide
Testimonies on mathematics performance last week were welcome good news for the public schools in Washington D.C.
Although the district of Washington is still far behind the country's schools that produce top performances, testimonies show that fourth- and eighth-graders have made faster progress over the past two years than cities that include Atlanta, Chicago and New York.
However, what is included in the latest figures from the National Assessment of Educational Progress (NAEPs) is the stubborn performance gap between African-American and white pupils, both at the local and national level.
The average score of white fourth-graders from Washington D. C. in the last two years rose from 262 to 270 (on a scale of 500), that of their African-American peers but only increased by three points from 209 to 212.
In reality, the performance gap rose from 53 to 58 points between 2007 and 2009.
The progress of the African-American eighth graders basically remained the same, falling by a statistically negligiblable point from 245 to 244.
The average score of white students was not included in the test data because the sample size wasn’t large enough.
The picture over a six-year period isn't more encouraging either.
The chasm separating white and black fourth-graders in 2003, when the first NAEP was detected in this district, was 60 scale points (262 to 202).
And although the score that children of both groups have reached has grown in this period, the difference has hardly diminished with 58 points now.
Some of the district's education advocates last week expressed their concern that the results, celebrated by school chancellor Michelle A. Rhee and Democratic mayor Adrian M. Fenty, for the most part, were driven by white pupils who are already high-performing children anyway.
This suggests we've raised the overall ranking by treating those at the top end of the scale, which is problematic and difficult, said Jeff Smith, board member of D.C. Voice, a non-profit group that advocates educational equality in Washington D.C.
I don't jump up and down for a span of two or three points, said Democratic Washington City Council member Kwame R. Brown, who has a fourth-grade Eaton elementary school daughter.
Brown, a common critic of Rhee's management style, said the message of examination scores is that the city's middle schools urgently need attention.
Of course you always want to see the positive signs, I respect that.
What frightens me, however, is that we don’t expend nearly the time and energy we would have to devote to our middle school.
Rhee said the district must continue to find better ways to meet the needs of low-performing students.
This fall, for example, some teachers are trained to use a new reading curricular, the Wilson reading system, aimed at pupils in the upper classes of elementary school and middle school, who have not mastered basic skills at the beginning of their school career and who lag significantly behind their classmates of the same age.
Everyday maths, a curriculum that comes to the fore from kindergarten to sixth graduation games and lifelike experiences, has been taken to Washington under former senior school inspector Clifford Janey and this curriculum is attributed some of the NAEP's advances reported last week.
I mean that we must continue to work to let the best measures benefit students who are below grade levels, Rhee said.
Others say the NAEP results highlight the question of whether Rhee can continue to improve the overall performance of the system and, in the process, can continue to provide the extra money and resources for the low-performing schools that are needed to reduce the divide.
This is a serious dilemma for Michelle Rhee, Bruce Fuller, professor of education at the University of California at Berkeley, who examines urban exam results.
The conventional remedy is to direct its resources and management reforms to the schools located in the poorest areas of Washington D.C.
The objective could cause political aftermath that is working against this important item on the agenda.
The weak point, of course, is, as with other municipal school inspectors, that she is trying to hold the white and black middle class.
Our focus is to make sure that we build a system of great schools.
We need to strengthen our schools in the neighbourhoods so that all families, regardless of where they live, can be sure that their children can receive an excellent public education.
Janey, who is now school inspector in Newark, said in an interview this week that one of the most significant elements for closing the benefit divide is to extend the traditional public school year, which is currently 180 days in Washington, to compete with the charter schools.
One has to raise the school year to nearly 200 days in order to have the strength to change, said Janey, who came to an agreement last year with his teachers' union on a 185-day a year, which he considers "a milestone" on the way to a bigger increase in the next contract.
Higher education for more people as a prospect for the future
The coach arrives at Gettysburg-College with a loud whistle.
Graciela Rodriguez, 12, gets out and blinks for a moment at the sight of the white columns, the brick facades and the emerald turf.
Graciela's parents barely graduated high school in El Salvador.
Until recently Graciela, who was recovered at Silver Spring and now lives in Riverdale, had never set foot on a college campus.
Their attitude and that of the group's other eighth-graders exploring Gettysburg, where teaching costs $38,690 per year, was that of an awe-inspiring visitor rather than that of an enthusiastic future student.
"Yes! I will be here soon!" cried Graciela, who would like to study medicine when the guide announced that they had now entered the building for natural sciences.
"Wow, really?" she said thoughtfully when she was told the school's low professor-to-student ratio.
College in Pennsylvania was the 7th that the children attended on their three-day tour and now they've fully taken up the intended message: The question isn't whether to go to college.
The question is where.
On the one hand, there is a growing concern about the large number of U.S.-born children of Hispanic immigrants who don't finish high school or become pregnant as teenagers, but on the other hand, there are also hundreds of thousands who receive college education needed to ascend into the middle class.
In truth, one in five of those "second generations" of Hispano-Americans graduate from college -- which is a notable feat as many of their immigrated parents, mostly from Mexico or Central America, came to the U.S. without graduating high school.
Their success stories are important, researchers say, because for a generation that will play a huge role in the country's workforce, they point the way forward.
Those examining powerful children say these often have a natural affinity for school and an inherent impetus for success.
Many of them also have parents who set high goals for their children and find ways to compensate for their lack of familiarity with American schools.
But counseling programs also play a big role by helping Graciela and millions of children like her get to college, especially if those efforts persist for an extended period.
Considering low-income children whose parents don't have the experience and skills to help them find their way through the system, then a single measure at some point won't solve the problem either, said Patricia Gándara, a researcher at the University of California at Davis who studied hispanic students.
We need to think about providing a supportive network for these children from preschool to through high school.
The federal program that had funded Graciela's college tour is a useful example.
It's known as GEAR UP - shifting up a gear - and provides more than $300 million a year for local school systems to launch college preparatory programs that begin when low-income students are in middle school and keep running until they graduated high school.
According to the U.S. Department of Education, the program has helped more than 10 million students since 1999, more than 60% of whom have gone to college.
Iraqi Oil Minimish contracts with 10 foreign oil companies
Despite concerns about violence and political instability, the Iraqi government has been able to get larger oil companies to rebuild its damaged infrastructure at two auctions concluded on Saturday.
Iraq's Oil Ministry's 10 contracts concluded with foreign oil companies suggest that China, Russia and European oil companies are willing to play an important role in renovating Iraq's oil industry, which has been crippled by war and economic sanctions for decades.
U.S. corporations were only able to record shares in two of the 10 fields auctioneed.
Seven American companies had paided for participation in the second auction, which began on Friday.
The only company that made a bid has pulled the shorter.
Two American companies were able to conclude contracts for fields auctioneed in June.
Thinly sown representatives of American oil multinationals in opening Iraq's oil industry surprised analysts.
Iraq has finally opened its doors after 6 years of war and instead of U.S. corporations are pretending Asian and European the way, said Ruba Husari, the editor of Iraq Oil Forum, an online news service.
There will be no further offers in Iraq for a long time.
Security concerns underscored by massively coordinated bombardments on Tuesday and political instability caused by the withdrawal of the U.S. military have likely stopped American oil companies, so analysts said to become more involved in Iraq, which owns the world's third-largest proven crude oil reserves.
U.S. corporations have been disadvantaged in some cases because competitors, especially the Chinese and other state-controlled energy companies, have significantly lower wage costs and are more likely to take risks because they don't have to account to shareholders.
Exxon-Mobil and Occidental Petroleum Inc. were the only American companies that were able to enter into contracts with the ministry.
Larger U.S. companies, such as Chevron and ConocoPhillips, which maintain close ties with Iraq's oil ministry and have stood with technical advice in recent years, have gone empty-handed.
The Russian companies Lukoil and Gazprom were the main players in two of the contracts awarded this weekend.
The state-owned China National Petroleum Corporation has bided for more contracts than any other company and has been able to go home with large packages of contracts for two larger fields.
We all know, oil ministry spokesman Assam Jihad said, that China is on its way to becoming one of the greats in the global economy as well as a technology power.
We are convinced that the Chinese company can compete with its fellow bidders and will fulfill its commitments to Iraq.
Preselected companies submitted bids, which were then compared with the fee per barrel of oil that the ministry was willing to pay in order to increase the output of each field beyond the current level.
Al Qaeda's top planner apparently killed in Pakistan
In an apparent U.S. missile strike along the Pakistani border with Afghanistan, a top Al Qaeda deployment planner is said to have been killed this week, U.S. counter-terrorism officials reported Friday.
This would be, if it were confirmed, the second deadly attack on a high leader of the terrorist organization this autumn.
Saleh al-Somali was one of the two Arabs alleged to have been killed, according to U.S. sources and Pakistani government officials of the region, by a number of missiles that hit their vehicle near the city of Miran Shah in North Waziristan province on Tuesday.
Local authorities reported that the missiles were fired by a type of drone used by the CIA in Pakistan's lawless tribal belt.
They were driving in a white vehicle towards the Afghan border when the car was hit, an authority representative from a Pakistani civilian intelligence agency from Miran Shah said by phone.
Local residents, suspected of being among the militants, ran at the spot and quickly took everything left of the "completely destroyed bodies."
The local authorities could not confirm the identity of the victims, but two representatives of U.S. counter-terrorism cited indefinite evidence that Somali belonged among the dead.
Somali has been described as an Al Qaeda's leading military planner leading operations by terrorist groups outside the Afghanistan-Pakistan region.
He was involved in conspiracies around the world, noted a senior official wishing to remain anonymous, pointing to the delicate nature of U.S. airstrikes on Pakistani soil.
Assuming one's central role as a given, it also included planning attacks against the U.S. and Europe.
He took strategic instructions from Al Qaeda's top leaders and implemented them in operational planning for possible terrorist attacks.
The second American official said that Somali had risen rapidly in the al Qaeda hierarchy and maintained best ties to other extremist groups in the region.
He may not be known to some Americans, the second official said, but this by no means diminishes the danger he posed to us and our allies.
If his death were confirmed, Somali would be the second leading Al Qaeda or Taliban leader to be killed since September when Najmuddin Jalolov, the leader of a militant faction in the tribal area and three other top activists were killed.
The pace of attacks by CIA drone drones has declined since the summer, according to a summary by the Long War Journal, a website maintained by a non-profit organization, from an average of six operations per month to two.
The decline can be based on improved tactics of the terrorist groups that have taken steps to reduce their vulnerability while brutally killing suspected informants, the site reported.
Leading U.N. envoy in Kabul will resign
U.N. leading envoy to Afghanistan, Kai Eide, said on Friday that he would resignate from office in March, ending a stormy term clouded by allegations of widespread corruption in the UN-backed Afghan presidential election.
Oath's departure occurs when the Obama administration decided to send another 30,000 U.S. soldiers to Afghanistan.
The U.N. envoy said he supports the troop increase, but he also expressed his concerns that the U.S. timetable for the military withdrawal, which is scheduled to begin in 18 months, could prompt other NATO administrations to withdraw their forces.
We need to accelerate the build-up of Afghan security forces and thereby send the right signal to Afghans that they can trust the international community, Eide said in a telephone interview from Kabul.
Commitment must be long-term.
The Norwegian diplomat also urged the United States and other military powers to increase the number of international civilian aid workers who are supposed to help in Afghanistan's political conversion.
The troop increase on the military side must correspond to an increase on the civilian side, he said.
Eide said that he did not resignate his office, but only fulfilled an agreement reached against his family in March 2008 to stay in Kabul for only two years.
He said he wanted the notification to U.N.
Secretary Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon is now forwarding so that he has time to provide for a replacement.
What I've said is that he's better starting to look for a successor, Eide said.
When I came here there was a two-month vacuum between the departure of my predecessor and my arrival.
Ban has begun, according to U.N. representatives, with the search for a replacement.
The U.N. officials say he has considered the Swedish-Italian-born Staffan di Mistura, who recently led the U.N. mission in Baghdad and the Frenchman Jean-Marie Guéhenno, who previously led U.N. peace operations.
Oath's position in Afghanistan was put to the test after his former deputy, Peter W. Galbraith, instructed him in September of favoring President Hamid Karzai in the country's presidential election and covering up evidence of massive electoral fraud.
Eide has denied all the accusations, but he also said the accusations of Galbraith - who was fired - "have undoubtedly harmed the mission because there has already been a huge level of skepticism about international interference in the election."
Eide said that he has recommended the appointment of a leading civilian representative to coordinating the relief efforts of the American-led armed forces in Afghanistan.
He also urged the U.N. leadership to allow his successor to hire more personnel from the United States and other Western countries donating for the Afghan mission, and said that this would boost their confidence in having their money deployed properly.
Eide expressed his disappointment at restrictinginging his forces in Afghanistan and said the cumbersome U.N. recruitment arrangements have undermined its ability to bring in capable people.
The U.N. rules are designed in such a way that since May, he said, I have only been able to hire one person.
This is catastrophic and must not continue like this.
U.S. Defense Secretary Gates: Iran must expect additional sanctions
Defense Minister Robert M Gates said Friday that world powers will soon impose "significant additional sanctions" on Iran for failing to participate in talks on its nuclear plans.
Gates spoke during a 7-day trip through the war zones in Afghanistan and Iraq to a group of about 300 U.S. soldiers in northern Iraq and downplayed the prospect of military action against the Islamic Republic of Iran.
There aren's no good opportunities to vote in Iran, he said in response to a soldier's question about the likelihood of such development.
“One of things that are on my mind, if we have learned anything from Iraq over the last six years, is its own unpredictablemence to war.”
The Obama administration is considering a sanctions package targeting Iran's military and political elite, but Gates has announced that some sanctions could also affect the average Iranian.
He said a "package of positive and negative incentives" is needed "to convince the Iranian government that in reality it is less protected with nuclear weapons" because "its people will suffer tremendously" from the sanctions.
On Friday's statement by White House spokesman Robert Gibbs, the U.S. government joined European leaders in warning that Iran must face "credible" consequences if it fails to comply with the demands of the U.N. Security Council and its Atomic Energy Agency, the International Atomic Energy Agency.
Iran continues to claim that the knowledge of nuclear technology should only be gained for peaceful purposes.
At talks in Geneva on October 1, Iran said it would resume talks on restricting its nuclear program and would forego a substantial portion of its stockpile of enriched uranium in exchange for much-needed nuclear fuel for its medical research reactor.
The U.S. government has urged this kind of agreement to build trust between the two parties and gain time for negotiations.
Iran, however, appears to have been abandoning negotiations on the provisional agreements since that time -- in part, experts say, because the Iranian leadership disagrees over whether they should commit to the United States.
That Iran simply left the international community standing in terms of some proposals they had already agreed to in early October has, frankly, brought together the international community, including the Russians and Chinese, in a way they have never been in conjunction with major additional sanctions for Iran, Gates said.
President Obama has set Dec. 31 as a deadline for Iran to respond to these proposals before pursuing other options, including those that Secretary of State Hillary Rodham Clinton once called "suffocating sanctions."
In a statement issued in New York, Iran's representation to the United Nations has condemned what it on Thursday called "foundless and unfounded claims" by some Security Council members about Iran's nuclear activities and said they are ready to continue talks with the United States and five other world powers "to achieve a suitable long-term solution."
Gates, who was scheduled to return to Washington last Friday, met with Iraqi Prime Minister Nuri al-Maliki in the morning before flying to the oil-rich Kurdish region in Iraq for meetings with the soldiers in Kirkuk and with Kurdish officials in Irbil.
Tensions between the Kurds and the Arab majority in Iraq continue to be high, in particular regarding borders, property rights, and because of the division of public revenue.
Gates urged both sides to lower the potential for conflict to prevent delays in U.S. plans to lower American troop strength from 115,000 to 50,000 by the end of August.
Gates also sought to dispel Kurdish concern about the impending troop withdrawal. U.S. representatives quoted Gates, who told Masud Barzani, president of the Kurdish regional government, said: "We will preserve your security, your prosperity and your autonomy within a united Iraq.
We won't leave you."
A change of course in aging China
Wang Weijia and her husband grew up surrounded by propaganda posters that gave them lectures that "Mother Earth is too tired to entertain more children" and "A baby more means a tomb more."
These lessons they have learned so well that when government officials in Shanghai, who suddenly began to change course through the low birth rate and the aging population this summer and encouraged young couples to have more than one child, their answer was immediate and definitive: "No way."
We've already invested all our time and energy in a child.
There's nothing left for a second child, said Wang, 31, a human resources manager with an 8-month-old son.
More than 30 years after the introduction of the one-child policy in China that has produced two generations of notoriously thick, warped single children affectionately called "little emperors," a population crisis in the country is drawing up.
The average birth rate has fallen to 1.8 children per couple compared to 6 at the time when this policy came into effect, the U.N.
Department of population issues firmly, while the number of populations of 60 years and older is expected to increase explosively from 16.7 percent of the population by 2050 to 31.1 per cent. This is well above the global average of about 20 percent.
The imbalance is stronger in rich coastal cities with an educated population, such as Shanghai.
Last year, 60-year-olds and the elderly accounted for nearly 22 percent of Shanghai's reported residents, while the birth rate has been below one child per couple.
Xie Lingli, head of Shanghai's Urban Population and Familiary Planning Commission, has said couples of procreative age need to have babies to "help reduce the ratio of aging populations and alleviate the labour shortage of the future."
Shanghai will soon be "as old -- but not as rich -- as the industrialized countries, such as Japan and Sweden," she said.
Gradual relaxation
Enshrined in the country's constitution in 1978, China's one-child policy arguably is arguably the most controversial injunction introduced by the ruling Communist Party to date.
Married couples who violate this policy have to expect huge fines -- up to their triple annual salary in some regions - and with discrimination at their workplace.
Chinese state representatives attributed it to this policy that the critical use of natural country resources could be avoided, whereas human rights representatives have condemned abuses in enforcing politics.
In rural regions, some officials have forced pregnant women who have already had a child to abort.
Many couples also had gender-selective abortions, creating an unnatrually high ratio of men to women.
In recent years, government officials for the population have progressively mitigated their stance on one-child politics.
In 2004, they allowed more exceptions to the rule -- including city dwellers, members of ethnic minorities, and cases where both husband and wife have been single children --- and in 2007 the tone of many hard slogans was tempered.
Qiao Xiaochun, a professor at the University of Beijing's Institute for Population Research, said government officials have recently even discussed more radical changes, for example, allowing couples two children where a partner is an only child.
In July, Shanghai became the first Chinese city to launch an aggressive campaign to promote more births.
Posters that instructed families to have only one child were replaced almost overnight by guidelines detailing who is eligible for a second child and how to apply for approval.
The city administration has sent out municipal employees and volunteers for family planning to visit couples at home and to push information sheets under the doors.
She also asked to provide psychological and financial advice for those who have more than a child.
The reaction was not exactly encouraging, family planning officials said.
Disappointing reaction
Although officials from a small rural town in Shanghai's outdoor areas said that an upswing can be seen in the applications of couples who want a second child after the launch of the campaign, the urban districts do not report any change.
For the municipality of Huinan with a population of 115,000, only four to five applications are still received as an example per month.
Disappointed Shanghai officials say the number of births expected in the city for 2010 will still be around 165,000 despite the campaign -- slightly higher than in 2009, but lower than in 2008.
Feng Juying, head of the family planning committee of the Shanghai community of Caolu, said that financial considerations were probably the main reason why many people don't want to have more children.
They want to do the best to their first, she said.
Yang Jiawei, 27, and his wife, Liu Juanjuan, 26, said that they would like to have two children and this was also legally permissible.
But they, like many Chineses, have only minor health and life insurance from the government.
Without a social network, they say, this decision would be irresponsible.
People in the West mistakenly rate one-child policy as a human rights issue, said Yang, a civil engineer whose wife is seven months pregnant with her first child.
Yes, we are being robbed of the opportunity to have more than one child.
However, the problem does not simply lie in politics.
The problem is money.
Other couples give psychological reasons for their hesitation.
Wang, the human resources manager, said she only wants one child because she was also an only child. "We were the focal point of the family and used that everyone took care of us.
We'ren't used to caring about others and we don't really want that either."
Chen Zijian, a 42-year-old who owns a translation company, said it more bluntly.
For parents from the middle class with a double-career, through which the birth rate drops, he said, it's about being successful and for that you have to be selfish.
The age group of today's 20- to 39-year-olds grew up by seeing their parents struggling during the early days of China's capitalism experiment and they don't want such a life for themselves, he said.
Even a single child, he said, places huge demands on parents' time.
A mother has to give up her social life for at least two years.
Then there's the space question -- "You have to remodel your apartment" -- and the life planning -- "If the child is 9 months old, you already have to have a resume ready for the best preschools."
Most of his friends are willing to go through this once, Chen said, but not twice.
Our generation is the first to achieve a higher standard of living, he said.
We don't want to make too many sacrifices.
U.N. group devises plan to reduce emissions
The U.N.-sponsored climate conference -- which until now has been characterized by wild poses and mutual accusations -- re-established an ambitious reduction in greenhouse gases for the next 40 years, with industrialized nations having to shoulder the greatest burdens in the near future.
The text, which could provide the basis for a definitive political agreement regulating greenhouse gases, showed both the remaining obstacles, as well as the way forward.
But he has been seen as an important advance in a negotiation that runs out time as more than 100 top politicians from around the world will arrive in Copenhagen next week.
The text, which was formulated by a specially created UN working group for this purpose, is silent about the amount of money that rich countries should give to the poor countries so that they can master global warming in the short and long term.
It also offers a range of options for the key issues, including how industrialised and large emerging economies can limit their carbon emissions and what the upper limit of the global temperature rise that policymakers would support.
The process will be given great flexibility, according to John Coequyt, senior representative of the Sierra Club in Washington.
Michael Zammit Cutajar, who drafted the six-page document, has summarized a 180-page negotiating text to highlight what the UN's leading climate protection representative, Yvo de Boer, has described as "the big picture."
It shows the outline of a possible agreement in which industrialized nations would collectively reduce their emissions by 25 to 45 percent by 2020, compared with the 1990 limits, while larger developing countries would reduce their emissions by 15 to 30 percent over the same period.
Together, countries could cut emissions by 50 to 95 percent by 2050.
The European Community has also boosted Friday's talks through its assurance to allocate €3.6 billion per year over the next three years to help poorer countries adapt to the impacts of climate change - from mastering floods and droughts to preventing deforestation.
Friday nevertheless brought the same verbal fireworks that had determined the talks in the last week.
U.S. Climate Special Representative Todd Stern has rejected formulations calling for binding reductions in greenhouse gas emissions for industrialized countries, compared with voluntary reductions for larger emerging economies if they are not funded by developed nations.
That move announced that the Obama administration is running a tougher line with China than representatives of the Bush administration not even two years ago.
The United States will not sign a deal without larger emerging economies standing up and taking action, according to Stern, who also complained that the text did not go far enough to ensure that the reductions could also be confirmed by outsiders.
Stern issued its opinion an hour after Chinese Vice Foreign Minister He Yafei's statement, which stated that the leading American climate negotiator was either lacking of "sanity" or it was "highly irresponsible" from him saying earlier this week that the United States wouldn't financially help China cope with global warming.
Due to the fact that the future economic direction of the major world powers is at stake, fracture lines have opened up both within the industrialized nations and the emerging countries.
The current struggle relates equally to the rescue of individual economies, as well as the rescue of the planet, with China and the United States committing because of their corresponding commitments, while poorer countries claim that the two dozen most influential countries would ignore the scientific need to take bolder action.
Ricardo Ulate, a delegate from Costa Rica, said that it is not surprising that the major powers are fighting over who should bear the costs of curbing greenhouse gas emissions, as even affected countries have moved to more aggressively in bringing countries accountable for their actions.
This is quite clearly a game from which a new economic supremacy will develop, according to Ulate, who is also working as a regional climate change adviser for Conservation International in Mexico and Central America.
Some of the countries most exposed to the impacts of climate change said they would continue to fight for a legally binding treaty in Copenhagen, although the leading participants say the talks will lead to a political agreement at best.
The alliance of small island states with 43 members presented a 24-page draft treaty early on Friday morning.
Artur Runge-Butcher, who chairs the international climate negotiations for the European Commission, said the offensive of the small island states "exerts political pressure on the entire political process," partly because they are now united and are demanding action from emerging countries, such as China and India.
The talks got a new urgency when the delegates focused on the fact that most open issues have yet to be clarified before the arrival of government leaders in order to reach an agreement.
High-ranking representatives, such as Indian Environment Minister Jairam Ramesh and China's Vice-Minister got out of their planes and raced through the aisles of the Bella Center to meet behind-closed-door meetings and press conferences to stake out claims that will be negotiated during next week.
The pure extent of the gathering -- 13,000 people go in and out daily at the convention center, guitar-playing activists are hosting night-time shows in which they make fun of the countries, which in their opinion reveal and sell, and contract drafts are passed from hand to hand rather than by e-mail -- is already a challenge.
Intensity is just building up: almost all the key ministers are here now and from Wednesday there will be 60 government leaders in Copenhagen.
We're coming to the upper league, said Carlos Manuel Rodriguez, vice president for global politics of the organization Conservation International.
The heavyweights are coming.
Britain's first 225 km/h fast train redefines Monday's feeling
Monday morning at 5 a.m. 13, a whistle will permeate the darkness to announcing a train departure that represents a revolution in traffic in Britain.
The first high-speed commuter train in the country will reach 225 km/h during its lightning journey from Ashfort, Kent, to London St Pancras and travel 93 km in 38 minutes.
To Lord Adonis, the transport minister, Dame Kelly Holmes will then embark aboard the first Javelin commuter service from London.
He hopes the UK's new service will ultimately connect to an international rail network of faster, reliable trains and will connect the major metropolitan areas of the Midlands, the north and Scotland to London.
The neglected province could become vibrant commuter cities, working hours would be radically reduced and switching from plane and car to train would reduce national carbon emissions and congestions on the roads.
In theory, the three major parties believe in this vision.
But the many billions of British pounds needed to build a new national rail network, the unavoidable planning battle and fears about environmental damage could still put the brakes on Britain's high-speed tracks.
High Speed Two (HS2), a company established in January that is to submit a feasibility study to the government, will present Lord Adonis with the report on December 30, according to the Times.
As a result, it receives a detailed route map of the next planned stage of the high-speed rail network.
The new railway line to connect London with the West Midlands will lead past urban areas with a five-metre leeway and places where the impact on the environment is controversial.
In the open terrain, the plan will show the concept within a distance of 25 meters from the final route.
HS2 is also to create three options for a larger, north-facing high-speed rail network.
Lord Adonis will respond in the spring.
The preferred proposed option is a Y-shaped routing in which a single high-speed line would lead into the West Midlands.
The route would split in or near Birmingham and one turn-off would lead west the Pennines to Manchester and Scotland and another heading north-east to Sheffield, Leeds and Newcastle upon Tyne.
A single line would lead to Scotland.
"It seems to be the best-functioning option," one railway insider said.
Upon completion, the travel time between London and Edinburgh would decrease to 2 hours 40 minutes.
The first section, which would not open before 2025, would allow travellers to cover the London-Birmingham route in 49 minutes, compared with just over an hour today.
The designs offered trains the opportunity to travel at a speed of 400 km/h, making the British rail network the fastest in Europe.
The first route was planned in such a way that 400-meter-long trains with a maximum capacity of 1,100 people can run on it.
Up to 18 trains could be in operation per hour on the London-Birmingham route.
This in turn would mean that a terminus would be needed in London where 20,000 travellers per hour could be handled.
Given the spatial restrictions in the capital, an existing station is expected to be expanded to accommodate the high-speed network.
Railway experts say that only St. Pancras International or Euston have this potential.
Lord Adonis will announce whether or not this plan should be continued in his opinion, but the lengthy public advisory and planning measures mean that a final decision will not be made until after the parliamentary elections.
But even if it sets the wheels in motion, the rail network must be built in sections.
Trains would first have to switch from the high-speed lines to existing rail lines in the north of Birmingham, making the journey time between London and Scotland continuing to be over three hours.
This is considered to be a decisive sticking point, so that a change from the aircraft to the railway can be ensured, a benchmark for the rail revolution.
Taxpayers come up for mortgage for €75,000 Orangery of a Conservative
Alan Duncan, the Conservatives' leading lower-house member, has billed thousands of pounds a year for the cost of an orangery that he had built in his apartment building in his constituency.
The party's prison spokesman, degraded by David Cameron for complaining that lower-house members have to live "from meagre rations," has increased his mortgage by £75,000 to build an oak-wood half-timbered annex to his second home in Rutland.
He was allowed to charge the taxpayer the extra interest, a sum of hundreds of pounds per month.
Those in charge of the House of Commons approved the motions at the time and they were also not questioned by Sir Thomas Legg, who is conducting a review of the expenses of the lower house members. Last nightuous not have been "even cleaner or more understandable."
Duncan, one of the richest lower house members, owns a two-storey house in a village in his constituency of Rutland and Melton.
Nearby homes have yielded a purchase price of almost a million pounds (1.1 million euros).
The ground floor includes kitchen, living and dining room, but the Fees Office, an Expenditure Checkpoint of the House of Commons, has agreed that Duncan needs more space.
Last year, he grew a conservatory called "orangery" in the plans.
Neighbors described him as a "glass house for entertainment."
Duncan has also not been urged during a recent investigation of his previous mortgage contracts by the Committee on Standards and Privileges to comment on the increased borrowing.
The committee acquitted him last month after an investigation into his spending from the breach of regulations.
In 2004, Duncan transferred collateral for £271,406 (€300,000) from his London home to his constituency house, which he had bought in 1991, a year before becoming a member of parliament.
Documents released this week show Duncan was refunded about £1,400 (1,500 euros) per month as a mortgage interest by March last year.
In April, his claims rose to more than £1,800 (€2,000) per month.
Duncan's RBS mortgage's interest rate has not changed during this period, suggesting that the entire increase served to finance the £75,000 (€85,000).
Normal stepless RBS mortgage rate fell from 7.94 percent in December 2007 to 4 percent in March this year, where it still stands.
The latest documents released by the House of Commons show Duncan continued to assert his right to £1,250 (1,400 euros) per month this May.
Duncan lost his post as opposition leader of the House of Commons in September after an undercover reporter filmed him in the process as he said after the spending scandal that members of parliament would live "from meagre rations."
“Essentially, if you work for the state, it means that you have to live from meager rations and be treated like s*****e.”
He said: "I spent my money on my garden and are claiming a vanishingly small part, measured by what belongs.
And I could've demanded the whole damn sum, but I don't."
The member of parliament, millionaire from his previous career as an oil wholesaler, was attacked for the first time in May because of his spending, when it became clear that he had claimed thousands of pounds for his garden before clarifying with the Fees Office that this "could be seen as an exaggerated demand."
An activist has dug a hole in the form of a pound sign as a protest in Duncan's turf after it became known that he had demanded £4,000 (4,500 euros) over three years.
When he was questioned about the increased mortgage last night, Duncan said: "This had been approved both by the Fees Office and subsequently by Legg to increase value."
He added: "There's no debate about dodging and meandering or bending the rules or anything like that.
Everything is completely transparent and moves strictly within the rules, is approved and everything.
It couldn’t be cleaner and clearer.”
Come all of you believers to admire Brighton's beach hut advent calendar
Beach huts in Brighton have found a new content of life as an interactive advent calendar for the winter months.
"Beyond," an alternative church group spurring to spiritual discoveries through creativity, has invited 24 owners to decorate their huts for every December day with Christmas carols as a theme.
The event kicked off on December 1 and visitors were allowed to watch the latest hut every day from 5:30 p.m. to 6:30 p.m. with free mulled wine and traditional mince pies.
The previously selected Christmas songs are "I saw three ships", "O come, o come, Emmanuel" and "The first Noël," where the cottage decoration is the work of Janette Tozer, a local artist.
Martin Poole, 50, a TV marketing assistant from Hove, is an honorary cleric of the Chichester diocese and head of Beyond.
He said: "We want to make religion more important again for people in a post-Christian society.
Why do we, as a church, expect people to come into a strange old building? I think the church should go to the people and host thrilling celebrations.
The idea for the beach hut advent calendar began as a conversation with some friends at dinner.
Brighton is such a fantastically creative and lively place and we are trying to express this through our spirituality.”
Strong mobilisation in Copenhagen and in the world for the climate.
Tens of thousands of people demonstrated worldwide and in Copenhagen on Saturday to call for an ambitious and obligatory agreement on the fight against global warming, where the police have made several hundreds of arrests.
At least thirty thousand people, according to the police, a hundred thousand according to organizers, have marched in the cold of the Danish capital, on the sidelines of the United Nations climate conference, which was supposed to lead to a conclusion by Friday, which could come into force from 2013.
For Connie Hedegaard, Danish chairwoman of the climate conference, the increasing mobilization in the world for the climate contributes to what is illustrated by these demonstrations that the "political price" of a failure in Copenhagen would be very high.
