The majority of the protesters have arrived by fines and trains from the major European cities, but also many Asians, including some Chinese and Koreans were presented, as well as some Africans.
Approximately 3,000 people, most of them with sky-blue raincoats, made a first casserole in the morning in Copenhagen as you have followed the call of the Friends of the Earth "blue tides" to form "climate justice."
Today we are taking to the streets to demand reparation of ecological guilt in favor of the South, explained Lidy Nacpil, Filipino activist of the Jubilee South Coalition in Copenhagen.
We can't carry on like this and say to ourselves: we have time, said Angelique Kidjo, singer from Benin.
There are rivers in Africa that dry out, watercourses where you can walk, just as it has never been in the past.
For the first time in the history of climatic diplomacy, founded in 1992 with the adoption of the UN agreement, the movement of globalization critics has approached environmental organizations.
MEP José Bové, figure of globalisation critics, announced that he came to Copenhagen to link "climate justice and social justice": "Today there is no difference between the fight against global warming and the struggle for another world."
The Asia-Pacific region, where there are many islands that are particularly endangered as a result of warming, has fired the launch for the demonstrations.
About 50,000 people have taken to the streets in Australia, according to organisers.
There have been a few hundreds of people in Manila, mostly students who have marched in red and hoisted bandanas in which you have praised solar energy.
In Hong-Kong or Djakarta, but also in Canada, marches of several hundred protesters took place to demand a vigorous approach against climate change.
In France, the demonstrations organized by the "350" network have gathered a few hundred people, namely in Paris, in Marseille (south), in Lille (north), in Bordeaux (west) and in Lyon (east).
Some people have gathered in Geneva.
Separatist Abkhazia has elected its president, denouncing Georgia that this is a "prank."
Abkhazia elected its president on Saturday, this happened a bit more than a year after Moscow recognized this pro-Russian separatist Georgian territory as self-sufficient, Tbilissi denounced the "prank."
Some 131,000 people have been calleded to participate in the first election after the Russian-Georgian war of August 2008 for control over the South-Ossetian territory, another Georgian secessionist area recognized by Russia.
Polling stations closed at 5 p.m. GMT.
Voter turnout amounted to approximately 58% a few hours before the polling station closure, according to the Abkhasian electoral commission officials.
The first preliminary results should be announced in the night from Saturday to Sunday.
The five candidates, including the previous president, Serguei Bagapch, categorically reject the idea of a reunification with Georgia condemning the election.
These elections are a farce.
For his part, the Georgian president, Mikheil Saakachvili, regrets that Abkhazia is now under a total scrutiny, his press spokeswoman, Manana Manjgaladze.
In addition to Russia, only Nicaragua and Venezuela have recognized the independence of this territory with 216,000 inhabitants, while the rest of the world perceives it as a part of Georgia, which is illegally occupied by Russia.
After separating from Georgia during the 1992-1993 conflict with several thousand people dying, Abkhazia wants to prove through this election that it is ready to join the international community.
This process is a stage in our new life, a new era, as an independent state, explained Svetlana Kvartchia, a 54-year-old historian who claims to have chosen Mr Bagapch.
She was one of the voters who went to a polling station, which was in a school in the capital Soukhoumi, a beautiful white building with a few palm trees in front of the entrance.
The question of Russian dominance is at the heart of this presidential election, in a region with beautiful landscapes on the black sea.
Large signs on which Bagach can be seen on the side of the Russian president and head of government, Dmitry Medvedev and Vladimir Putin, are everywhere in this small area that has 216,000 inhabitants.
In Gali, which is located in the east of the country, Beslan Archba, has prepared a warm-hearted welcome to the Russian representatives who have come to oversee the election.
We, the Abkhazian people, are grateful, he has said at a small celebration, as an allusion to Moscow's support, in particular with regard to recognising Abkhazia's independence.
I hope that your country will soon be recognized by the United Nations, answered Alexei Ostrovsky, a member of the Russian parliament, raising a glass of vodka in the process.
The opposition for its part, represented by the former vice-president and two Russian businessmen, Zaous Ardzinba and Beslan Boutba, has criticised the agreements made with Moscow in the previous year, such as the one in which Abkhazia leaves Russia control of the railway for 10 years.
The fifth candidate, Vitali Banba, claims to support neither the current government nor the opposition.
A majority of 50% plus one vote is needed to win the election.
If none of the candidates reach this limit, a second round of voting must be organised within two weeks.
