According to Chatel, the subjects geography and history emerge strengthened from the school reform of the Lycées.
National Education Minister Luc Chatel explained in an interview that appeared in the Sunday newspaper "Journal du dimanche" that geography and history emerge strengthened from the school reform of the Lycées, even if they are not taught obligatively in the terminals (12th school year).
"Première's pupils (11th school year) now have all four hours per week and the same programme," confirms Mr Chatel.
For the minister, "this hypocrisy must be overcome," because according to him, "the scientific profile is no longer a scientific profile," but on the whole a concentration "of the best pupils."
"The economic-social and literary profile (ES and L) must not become a sub-profile of the scientific profile," he continued, underlining: "I have not heard many historians and intellectuals who were outraged by the lack of historical-geographical education in the terminals on technical lycees."
On Saturday, an IFOP survey published in the Humanité revealed that almost seven out of ten French (69%) criticize the state project for the deletion of compulsory teaching in history and geography in Terminal S (12th school year with a scientific profile). Many teachers and intellectuals also criticized this project, as well as the left and some representatives of the right.
The minister replied that there was no question about limiting the philosophy of the Terminale S programme.
Luc Chatel also defended educational reform for teachers, while 16 organizations (students and trade unions) had called for Tuesday to protest against the project, which in their like to fall victim to the pedagogical training of future teachers.
The minister argued that they undergo a subject-related aptitude test in Master 2 (M2) at the beginning of the year at the beginning of the year and an admissions examination at the end of the year, during which their teaching skills will be tested.
In addition, they carry out internships during the year in the M2 and then teach one year in front of the students as teachers in the internship.
“If you add all the internships offered prior to the performance test, you’ll achieve even more hours you’ve spent in front of the students, i.e. more than 300 hours.”
The minister eventually declared that he wants to offer "the individual right to education" - a "thing that doesn't exist in people's education."
He again confirmed that he wants to abolish the school card "by 2012".
15 minutes for the climate at the "Ecole Normale Supérieure"
Fifteen minutes to talk about the deployment at the Copenhagen summit: this is the challenge Jean Jouzel, vice-president of the "Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change", had to deal with on Saturday at the Ecole Normale Supérieure at Rue d’Ulm in Paris.
According to an AFP journalist, Nobel Peace laureate Jean Jouzel opened the first of the "Ernest" conferences, during which experts turn to the audience for fifteen minutes each with filmed contributions.
One would like to do something to reach people with and awaken in them the feeling of wanting to learn more, Edouard Hannezo, 22 years and one of the organizers of that day, explained on the sidelines of the confession. The designation of this event goes back to the name that the students have given to the fishes in the bash of school.
"Among the experts, around twenty participants were selected, even after their charisma," he clarifies.
"You can talk about global warming for hours, but the core goals can also be outlined in a few minutes," Jean Jouzel told AFP, who spoke in front of about 50 people only with the help of foils.
"This is part of our role as scientists," he underlined in the hope that listeners may learn that "the Copenhagen Conference relies on an absolutely serious scientific dossier."
The videos filmed by these conferences will be found online from December 15 on the school's website.
The next "Ernest" day is scheduled for February and will take place every two months thereafter.
Payment delay : angry prison guards, actions planned
Penitentiaries' unions called for a general day of action on Friday to ensure that premiums and overtimony, whose pay has been deferred for budget reasons until January, will be transferred ahead of Christmas holidays.
The 2nd union of correctional officers ("FO-Pénitentiaire"), urged the employees, who have no service to demonstrate on Tuesday morning in front of the prison and to block it in order to draw attention to the "theft of what is due to them".
The 3rd union of correctional officers ("CGT-Pénitentiaire") called for "uniting for a strong mobilization in the various prisons", on which the non-paying of overtime, overnight surcharges incurred in October, as well as the Sunday and holiday surcharges in December will market as "disgraceful and unacceptable".
The first union of correctional officers, the Autonomous Federal Penitentiary Union (UFAP/UNSA), called for "general mobilization (...) to show dissatisfaction and anger."
The management of the penitentiary and the Ministry of Judiciary stated that overtime hours increased sharply in 2009 and not all were provided for in the budget. However, they guarantee that the remaining demands will be paid in January.
Michèle Alliot-Marie sent a letter to the penitentiary officers’ unions, guaranteeing that the payment will be made next month.
"I know very well about the efforts of the staff to keep our prison system functional and modernise," she said.
"I have instructed the management of the correctional facility to take immediate action so that such a situation, which has arisen due to a lack of assessment of the overtime incurred, does not repeat itself in the future," Ms Alliot-Marie added.
The December wage is therefore meager, except for the management staff, who enjoy high service and target premiums, explains UFAP/UNSA, for which the "budget constraints" have to endure.
"This is an absolute scandal," outraged the inter-regional trade union FO in Marseille, announcing to boycott "all assemblies and convocations" pending new instructions.
"The staff doesn't go to work on Sundays and public holidays, when then the hours from October in December are still not paid. This also applies to their overtime," the FO Rhône-Alpes-Auvergne also threatens.
"At a time as more and more tasks are being burdened on us without hiring more staff, the attempt to enrich oneself's resources is more than is more than unreasonable, according to Bordeaux's CGT union.
According to the trade unions, the situation is not the same in all regions.
In any case, however, it is injustice, states the Secretary-General of UFAP, Jean-François Forget.
Obama believes he's always distributing Christmas better gifts than he's getting himself.
The American president, Barack Obama, who will spend Christmas for the first time with his family in the White House, has chatted to the TV talkmaster Oprah Winfrey that he always distributes better gifts than he gets himself.
At a special broadcast that aired on Sunday night via the ABC channel, the president and his wife Michelle gave insight into how they're going to spend the holidays in their new illustrious home.
Even Bo, the small four-legged companion of the family, will be involved.
"Santa Claus also loves Bo," Michelle Obama affirms, adding that of course the dog also gets a gift.
Michelle Obama strongly dismissed her husband's claims about the quality of the gifts. "I've given you beautiful gifts over the past year," she teases the president.
"So, please," he summoned her mischievously, pointing to the first lady's pearl necklace: "And who gave it to you there?"
During this one-hour broadcast in a casual atmosphere with the well-known television presenter who had so vehemently supported Barmamba during his election campaign, the president and his wife also talked about gifts they loved most as a child.
Barack Obama remembered a bicycle with ten gears and also a basketball that his father, whom he very rarely seed, had brought him from Kenya for Christmas.
"I remember when my dad visited us once Christmas and brought me a basketball," the president said.
“It wasn’t until much later in my live that I understood I had this ball from it.”
For her part, the first lady remembered her dollhouse.
"I didn't know at all how to arrange the furniture in a house, so I put them all on the wall instead of grouping it around the fireplace. But I loved this little doll's house," Michelle Obama recounted.
Johnny Hallyday re-operated, RTL confirms he's been put into artificial coma
Johnny Hallyday resumed surgery in Los Angeles because of significant "damages" following surgery in France. According to the people surrounding him, the situation was "under scrutiny," although RTL confirms that the singer has been put into an artificial coma to avoid pain and complications.
The 66-year-old rock musician underwent surgery again on the night from Wednesday to Thursday at Cedars-Sinaï hospital in Los Angeles after American doctors found "damage as a result of surgery of a herniated disc" he suffered on November 26 in Paris, according to his production company in Paris, adding that this damage would have required renewed surgery.
Nevertheless, the singer's production company wanted to make it clear that the situation was "under scrutiny" and announced that a new medical opinion would be available in 48 hours at the latest.
However, RTL, the partner during the singer's final tour, Johnny Hallyday was put into an artificial coma to avoid "pain and complications."
However, it was only after 48 hours that one had a little more certainty about the patient's condition when he opened his eyes, RTL added.
At the request of the AFP, there was neither confirmation nor a comment from the production company.
RTL also confirms that "emerging from certain sources" that "no drining whatsoever" was laid after its first operation in Paris.
The rocker had undergone surgery at Parc Monceau's international clinic because of a herniated disc.
The procedure planned for several days was carried out by neurosurgeon Stéphane Delajoux, a well-known doctor who had already treated many stars.
This was not available on Thursday evening for comments to RTL.
Following Johnny Hallyday's surgery, the singer was admitted Monday at Cedars-Sinaï hospital in Los Angeles due to a follow-up infection following the procedure.
The singer Eddy Mitchell, who is very close to Johnny Hallyday, told RTL on Thursday that his friend was going through a "deep valley". However, he assured that you "don't have to worry" about the rock musician, as he was "a good fighter."
"He had called me even before his departure for Los Angeles. I told him it was bullshit to sit on a plane for twelve hours after such an operation, that's not possible," Eddy Mitchell said.
On Wednesday evening, the singer's producer, Jean-Claude Camus, confirmed that Johnny Hallyday was "responding well to antibiotics" and that "the infection was contained."
Mr Camus also stated that he had spoken to Laeticia, the singer's wife, and that he felt no particular concern.
Nevertheless, concern for the singer's health had become ever greater since he spent nine days at a hospital in July after falling on his yacht.
Two months later there was general astonishment that Johnny Hallyday had a "little bowel cancer surgery," resulting in a mild infection.
At the end of September, the singer resumed his long "Tour 66" as a farewell tour.
Since then, however, every health problem has been extensively commented. Fans and journalists have not escaped that the singer struggled to move on stage.
Only in mid-November, the people surrounding him had to deny a re-admission to the hospital.
Euro-2009/Kurzbahn: First place for Bousquet over his favourite distance
The Frenchman Frédérick Bousquet, a three-time medalist at the 2009 Swimming World Championships, clinched an international title over his favourite distance, 50m freestyle, at the Euro-2009 on the short track in Istanbul on Thursday.
On the first day of this competition, on which the polyurethane suits were allowed for the last time, there were only three world records, while one had expected a real record flood.
One record has been improved by the Hungarian Evelyn Verraszto over 4 x 200 m layers and the other two by Russia at 4 x 50m layers of the men.
It's a pity for Bousquet, who wanted to reach for the world record at the 50m freestyle, whose record he holds in the big cymbal (20.94).
At one of the two royal distances in swimming, 50m freestyle, the 28-year-old Frenchman prevailed in 20.53-sec, in a final with ten swimmers at the start (usually there are eight) - a premiere at an international level.
Bousquet defeated the Croatian Duje Draganja (20.70) and the Russian Sergey Fesikov (20.84), but he failed to break the world record (20.30) as planned.
“You can always believe in bigger things, and in the present case I have said that if the world record is within my reach, I’ll try.”
Like Bousquet, almost all swimmers started in polyurethane suits, which are banned from 1 January 2010.
With 238 world records since February 2008, these combinations end their epic in Istanbul, which began for the first time at the same competition in 1999.
The use of the combination did not pay off this time for the German Paul Biedermann, two-time world champion in the basin this summer in Rome and world record, in the end.
The young swimmer has not managed to improve his best time over 400m freestyle (3:32.77), yet he prevailed with the second-best time in his career (3:34.55), ahead of Russian Nikita Lobintsev (3:35.75) and Dane Mads Glaesner (3:36.82).
“I haven’t given everything this time.
“I didn’t know I couldn’t win a world record today, but I defended my title and fulfilled my job.”
"Lobintsev will be a serious opponent in the future," Biedermann explained.
U.S.: Tomb slabe at Zola estimated at $1.5 million
A Roman tomb that appeared with French writer Emile Zola was estimated at $1.5 million above its value on Thursday, according to art retailer Sotheby's.
This plate is two meters long and 63 cm high. It dates from the third century AD and has always been valued at 150,000 to 250,000 dollars.
The marble depicting "four scenes of Dionysus in a sophistic architectural décor, surrounded by satyrs and Bacchus figures, is a rare piece. There are only four or five of them in the world," Florent Heintz, vice-presidence for auctioning Roman and Egyptian antiquities at Sotheby's, told AFP on Tuesday.
It has been sold under hundreds of pieces.
"Sales are still ongoing, but this part is almost certainly what buyers have offered the most for so far," Sotheby's spokesperson Dan Abernethy told AFP.
The discovery of this purchase of the marble part by Emile Zola goes far behind.
The plate was at home for almost 300 years in Rome's famous Borghese family's collection, after which she appeared piece by piece with French actress Cécile Sorel, who was used in her bathroom of her Parisian hotel and with Paul Reynaud, minister in the Third French Republic and president of the council, before Marshal Pétain came to power.
The next fifty years are in the dark.
While searching the Louvre database, Florent Heintz discovered that this record was part of an "inheritance by Emile Zola," which opened in 1903, a year after the death of the author of the "Thérèse Raquin" and the fresco "Rougon-Macquart".
Zelaya's exit attempt from Honduras towards Mexico has been thwarted
On Thursday, Mexico had declared the conditions for the overthrown Honduran President Manuel Zelaya's overthrow on domestic soil as incomprehensible after this escort by the cougnancy by the putsch government as unacceptable.
After a telephone conversation with President Zelaya that took place during the night, everything seems to indicate that one disagrees about the possibilities of the execution of this transfer, explained the Mexican Minister for Foreign Affairs, Patricia Espinosa.
According to the Immigration Department, the plane provided by the Mexican government, which had arrived in Salvador on Wednesday for the purpose of M. Zelaya's transfer, had returned to its starting point.
The coup government wanted M. Zelaya to "abdicate" and asked for political asylum for him abroad under guidance.
The ousted president declined, however, as he wished to return to Mexico as the most welcomed, incumbent head of state.
The coup regime "has suffered renewed failure by trying to persuade me to give me at my expense (...) they wanted me to abdicate," M. Zelaya told Radio Globo on Thursday, in which he has frequently spoken out since the beginning of the political crisis, which lasts for five months.
I can stay here well and gladly ten years, here I have my guitar, added the ousted president, who had fled two and a half months ago to the Brazilian Embassy of Tegucigalpa, in striking some chords.
The Mexican embassy has presented us with a request for guidance, which we unfortunately cannot accept, as it does not include a request for asylum, Oscar Raul Matute, interior minister of the coup government, told Honduran Radio HRN on Wednesday.
Manuel Zelaya replied in an interview with Telesur, whose headquarters are located in Caracas in no country.
A possible departure from Honduras, he had underlined, he would only undertake as president of the Honduran people.
Rumours of M. Zelaya's departure had mobilized on Wednesday the supporters of the ousted president, who had set out for the Brazilian embassy and gathered around it, whereupon the local military had intensified its deployment.
Brazil's Foreign Minister Celso Amorim criticised the coup government's position.
This, according to his assessment, reveals the marginal character of this government in the face of international norms.
Zelaya, who had approached Socialist Venezuelan President Hugo Chavez, was overthrown on June 28, on the day of the referendum he organized to prepare his re-election, against the advice of the Supreme Court, the army and the Congress.
From his exile in Costa Rica, he had surprisingly returned on September 21 and had fleded to the Brazilian embassy.
If he leaves these, he will be imprisoned for treason, which he is accused by the judiciary, according to the coup government.
Putsch President Roberto Micheletti has managed to organize a presidential election on 29 November to replace M. Zelaya.
Porfirio Lobo was recognized by the United States in the course of the disputed election, but was rejected by the Latin American countries such as Venezuela, Argeninia or Brazil.
Three days later, the Congress had voted massively against M. Zelaya's return to office until the expiration of his mandate on January 27.
Wall Street on upswing, U.S. trade balance feeds optimism
The New York Stock Exchange closed with a high on Thursday, the figures in the American trade balance testify to a price recovery of the shares of the United States as well as its partners: the Dow Jones increased by 0.67%, the Nasdaq by 0.33%.
According to figures at the close of the stock exchange, the Dow Jones Industrial Average has risen from 68.78 points to 10,405.83 points, the Nasdaq in turn, which is dominated by technology from 7.13 points to 2,190.06 points.
The Extended Standard & Poor ́s 500 indepent index was posted at 0.58% (6.40 points) to 1,102.35 points.
There is good news from various sectors of the economy, judged Lindsey Piegza of FTN Financial.
However, this remains anecdotal below the premise that the market focuses only on "half-full glasses," she moderated.
According to Avalon Partners Peter Cardillo, investors are mainly fixated on figures of the U.S. trade balance, "which exhibits an improvement in activity in the fourth quarter."
The trade deficit of the United States fell by 7.6% to $32.9 billion in October relative to September.
Analysts, by contrast, had anticipated deficit growth.
Both imports and exports have increased.
The big news is the increase in the total volume of trading, especially in goods export, reflectinging the economic growth of the United States' main trading partners, commented Christopher Cornell of Business Research Economy.com.
In return, unemployment reports in the United States have risen against expectations in the last week and have now reached 474,000 requests.
However, the average of new job seekers had "fallen to the lowest point of the year during the past four weeks," emphasized Scott Marcouiller of Wells Fargo Advisors.
The bond market is down.
The profitability of government bondies over 10 years has risen to 3.482% from the 3.432% from Tuesday evening, the profitability of bonds to 30 years to 4.492% compared to the 4.408% from the previous evening.
Cuba: Hundreds partisans in power whistle "ladies in white"
On Thursday, a number of partisans in Cuban powered the wives of political detainees who came together in Havana for the purpose of World Human Rights Day and also thwarted another rally, on the basis of which a good dozen dissidents were temporarily arrested.
Around the forty "ladies in Weiss", wives or people close to political prisoners were whistled on the open street by partisans of the government under Raul Castro, just as they were on the foot march to the center of the capital to demand the liberation of political detainees, according to a journalist of the AFP confirmed.
Down with the Yankies!, "Mercenaries!", "The street belongs to the revolutionaries!", "Living Fidel and Raul!" the empowered partisans chanted the dissidents with a raised fist.
The latter were escorted by civilian agents who had appeared to "ensure their protection."
A similar demonstration against the "Ladies in Weiss" had taken place the evening in Havana, which did not lead to any injuries or arrests.
In the Havan district of Vedado, on the other hand, a dozen dissidents, who also set out for the demonstration held on Thursday in view of the International Human Rights Day, were temporarily arrested by plainclothes police, a reporter of the AFP confirmed.
According to this journalist, the police forced the dissidents booed by the partisans of the government under the use of force in civilian vehicles.
Temporary imprisonment of dissidents under such circumstances are usually only limited to a few hours.
As contenders for the Sakharov Prize awarded by the European Parliament in 2005, the "Ladies in Weiss" have organized an unapproved march for the purpose of "please for the release of all political prisoners", the number of which according to the dissident list has around 200 persons, explained Laura Pollan, one of the leaders, whose husband Hector Maceda is serving a prison sentence of 20 years.
M. Maceda belongs to the group of the 75 Cuban dissidents imprisoned in March 2003, 53 of whom are behind bars to date.
The Cuban authorities perceive the regime opponents as "mercenaries" or "agents" in the service of the declared arch-enemy, the United States.
A rarity in itself is that Cuban television reported about Thursday's demonstration events between militant pro-government fighters and "countra-revolutionaries."
Cuba, led by a Unity Party, the Communist Party, is regularly blamed by European or American NGOs of denigrating rights and freedoms, in particular that of freedom of expression.
Insane man kills eighty-year-old with knife and leaves 5 injured in Clichy-la-Garenne
A madman armed with two knives killed an eighty-year-old and injured five other people on Friday around late afternoon at the main business hour in downtown Clichy-la-Garenne (Hauts-de-Seine).
According to the first information of the questioning, the 28-year-old attacker had injured the eighty-year-old from previously unexplained cause in or in front of a pharmacy on the Boulevard Jean-Jaures with two knives and this, according to police information, in the presence of witnesses.
The old man, born in 1929, succumbed to the consequences of his injuries, according to a person in charge of the police syndicate Alliance in the hospital.
In or in front of the same pharmacist, he pre-buttoned a couple with a pregnant woman.
The man, about in the threesomes, intervened and was injured.
The first two victims were hospitalized seriously injured, where the eighty-year-old died.
The other injured person is in danger of death, as he, according to police sources, apparently vital organs were affected.
The pregnant woman has become ill as a result of the events and is said to have been hospitalized according to a police spout in "shocking".
The armed man, according to police, appeared to be under the influence of alcohol or drug, is said to have immediately escaped into the street, Jean Jaure's equalitary.
He would have injured two other passers-by, apparently by chance: a thirty-year-old and a forty-year-old, the latter of both severely.
The scenario remained in the dark in the evening.
He had been armed with two butcher's knives, which he wore crossed behind his back, explained Arnaud Pressé, a person in charge of the Syndicates Alliance of the Hauts-de-Seine area.
Several passers-by, including juveniles, are said to have tried to tame the madman, according to witness reports determined by the AFP.
I've seen a guy in the thirties with a green vest.
Three teenagers attempted to intervene.
They didn't make it because the guy was well-built, Alberto, a waiter of a nearby bar, reported.
A horde has fallen over him, he added, including a city police officer who broke his wrist in the process.
Still on Boulevard Jean-Jaures, at the height of house number 61, two city police officers, including their supervisor, have tried to arrest the armed man and were injured with stabbings, according to police information.
The actions have taken place in one of Clichy-la-Garenne's main arteries, where a number of shops are located.
Passers-by approached me to tell me that there is an injured person outside.
I've gone out and seen a 70-year-old person lying on the floor, I've put her in first aid position and noticed a gaping wound in the stomach area, Frédéric Allione, a 35-year-old pharmacist, told AFP.
Five minutes later, a second, about thirty-year-old person has come, injured by cuts.
Officials of the state police have finally arrested the man, who did not carry identification papers.
The prosecutor's office of Nanterre has commissioned the district department of the criminal police of Hauts-de Seine (SDPJ 92) with the case investigation.
The insane was hospitalized on Thursday night.
Considering his condition, he has not yet been heard in court.
A police source doesn't rule out him being admitted to a mental hospital.
According to another police source, it smelled six police officers to tame him at the police department in Clichy, where he had been taken.
He was "in a state of overagence" like "all the spiritually confused of this kind, in which the forces are released during the course of action".
U.S.: Polanski's lawyer calls for cessation of prosecution again
The lawyer of the French-Polish filmmaker Roman Polanski once again demanded on Thursday in Los Angeles (California, Western United States) to drop the prosecution against his client, who is currently on trial at his place of residence in Switzerland due to a moral violation of more than 30 years ago.
Chad Hummel, a lawyer for the director of the "pianist," denounced gross litigative errors before three judges of the Court of Appeals in the second district of California at the time of the trial in 1977.
On the spot, I demand punishment, M. Hummel explained, before he added that the conduct of Judge Laurence Rittenband, who was tasked at the time of the trial with the case, "let it run coldly down your back."
According to him, the - now deceased - Judge Rittenband, before he dropped his verdict, had discussed with a prosecutor, who is said to have told him that Roman Polanski deserved penitentiation - which was a serious procedural error.
This discussion had been raised in the documentary "Roman Polanski: wanted and desired" (Roman Polanski: persecuted and desired), especially by the questioning prosecutor David Wells.
At the end of September, M. Wells declared that they had "lied" to the authors of the documentary.
Should the Court refuse to close the criminal proceedings, Mr Hummel would require holding a trial that would allow him to provide the evidence for his reasoning.
For its part, the indictment has repeated the evidence already explained in the first instance, i.e. that a discussion on the annulment of the facts charged to the defendant cannot be seriously considered as long as Roman Polanski does not appear in court.
The 76-year-old director had fleded from the U.S. in 1978, even before promulgating the sentence for "illegal sexual relations" with a 13-year-old minor.
He has not traveled to the United States since that time.
Los Angeles County deputy prosecutor Phyllis Asayama had stated that Roman Polanski "must be there to attend the trial."
Do you want to spread the embassy in this matter not only (Roman Polanski) but also to other defendants, the escape is a way out, asks Ms Asayma.
In her opinion, the filmmaker "asks the Tribunal for a favor and despises his authority."
One of the three judges – who now have 90 days to announce their decision – even emphasized “that there are other flight alternatives”.
Hummel wasn't the only one who had requested the question of prosecution against Roman Polanski.
Samantha Geimer's lawyer, the victim, also demanded the lifting of the criminal proceedings once again.
She recalled that her client would no longer bear to be bothered by any resurgence of the Affaire Polanski and added: "No one in this room can say that this procedure was fair.
Thirty-two years is enough.”
Roman Polanski was arrested on September 26) in Switzerland at the request of an American arrest warrant.
After spending two months behind bars, he was then convicted in anticipation of an eventual extradition to house arrest at his chalet in Gstaad.
Wall Street is being curbed by the dollar's rise and closes without a price.
The New York Stock Exchange closed without a price on Friday in a market that was torn between better indicators envisaged in the U.S. and an increase in the dollar: the Dow Jones gained 0.63%, while the Nasdaq lost 0.03%.
Based on definitive figures available at closure, the Dow Jones Industrial Average has risen 65.67 points to 10,471.50 points, while the technologically-dominated Nasdaq has yielded 0.55 points to 2,190.31 points.
The Extended Index Standard & Poor's 500 is up 0.37% (4.06 points) to 1,106.41 points.
"Honestly, the market is quite calm," Mace Blicksilver of Marblehead Asset Management noted.
"It remains narrowly limited, the trading volume has become very low and I think this will remain so until the end of the year."
The tendency was supported by the publication of better economic indicators than envisaged, which "helped to improve the market view on the solidity of the resurgence of the economy," Charles Schwab's analysts summarized.
