Election of the Miss World again in South Africa
After a month round trip through South Africa, the 112 female candidates for Miss World's title on Saturday will pass the last hurdle to realize their dream: to be voted the most beautiful woman in the world and this in front of a billion TV viewers.
During this great beauty fair, which will be held for the second time in a row in the surroundings of Johannesburg, five of them have already snatched their ticket to the semi-finals.
Miss Japan Eruza Sasaki, Perla Beltran Acosta from Mexico, Mariatu Kargbo from Sierra Leone, Kaiane Aldorino from Gilbratar and Yanbing Ma from Canada have been named Miss Sportive or Miss Topmodel in the course of their African stay.
This was a true whirlpool of pleasures.
Nice towns to visit, like Port Elizabeth, Durban and Cape Hoorn, was simply exceptional.
I've seen a lot of things and had a lot of experiences!, said Miss Sierra Leone with full enthusiasm.
This competition will take place for the ninth time in South Africa and will be broadcast live on Saturday, starting at 5pm (15pm Central European time) from Midrand, north of Johannesburg.
I couldn't think of a more beautiful country to hand over my crown.
This experience was simply intriguing.
I am happy to have had this experience twice, underlined Miss World Ksenia Sukhinova in the heat of the South African summer.
For the young Russian, her second stay in South Africa was characterized by numerous exchanges of experience with candidates and the rediscovery of "breathing landscapes".
After a month of galas, rehearsals, encounters with the press and safari, the Miss are now focusing on the competition.
Indian Pooja Chopra hopes to follow in the footsteps of Priyanka Chopra, who was elected in 2000 and will also be present in this election, as well as emulate Aishwarya Rai, who was elected in 1994 and both became Bollywood stars.
It is, of course, every woman’s dream of being crowned as the most beautiful woman in the world, but it’s not just about appearance.
We are ambassadors of our country and I would like to come home with the crown, throws Miss India with a broad smile.
According to the organizers, this 59th edition is underlined with music and dance.
The candidates defile in traditional costumes and not to forget, in a swimsuit.
The performance is presented by a television presenter, the Chinese Angela Chow, the ex-beauty queen from South Africa, Michelle McLean, and by a leader of the Miss World committee, Steve Douglas.
South Africa has shown to welcome the world with a hard-to-beaten warmth and pride, assures Julia Morley, chairwoman of Miss World.
The contestants, like last year, did a safari, visited the famous township in Soweto, but this time tasted by bicycle and local cuisine.
Quite extraordinary, however, was the meeting with South African President Jacob Zuma, who gave them his hand.
Not usual for a head of state, Miss Sierra Leone noted.
Their finest memory, however now, remains the draw in Cape Town (south-west) for the soccer World Cup in June 2010, a kind of country to advertise for this event.
I'm dealing with people I'd probably never be able to approach.
I will certainly come back to the 2010 World Cup, enthusiastically promised Miss Italy, Alice Taticchi.
Johnny Hallyday's news is "very positive," according to his son David
The news on Johnny Hallyday is "very positive," his son David assured on his arrival at Los Angeles airport on Friday, where he visited his father, who has been in the hospital there for several days.
The news is very positive.
He's very strong, it's all going very well, David Hallyday explained to the press when getting out of the plane where he arrived shortly after 1 p.m. local time (9 p.m.
