I spoke personally with this doctor, who assured me that he considers this trip as completely unproblematic, which had surprised me very much, since I already have two herniated disc operations behind me, he added.
Shortly before, he announced at RTL that the singer had apparently left the hospital "without drainage, without everything."
Dr Stéphane Delajoux is very well-known in the artist milieu.
He had the actress Marie Trintignant , operated a few days before her death in Vilnius, at a clinic in Neuilly-sur-Seine, where he practiced at that time.
In 2007, he had also operated on actress Charlotte Gainsbourg in order to resorb a hematoma after a brain hemorrhage.
However, he is also a doctor with a dubious reputation who has already been convicted several times due to unsuccessful operations and not to be forgotten, for tax evasion and fraud.
Me Olivier Metzner, lawyer for the Medical Association, has described him as "a man without scruples," who "regularly" has to answer before the board of directors and is "anything but recommended."
After a few days of silence, Dr Stéphane Delajoux issued a statement on Friday about his lawyer, Me David Koubbi.
He explained that the intervention would have "gone perfectly" and the post-operative examinations would have been "normal".
The surgery "didn't evoke any bleeding and therefore doesn't require drainage," he added.
In any case, the tour of the singer, whose health problems have been picking up since last summer, seems in question.
It's quite obvious that it's a bit too scarce for a continuation of the January 8 (in Amiens), Mr Camus explained.
The UDF (Union for French democracy) is becoming energetic and urges the Nouveau Centre party to stop using its seal
The party alliance, which is supervised by François Bayrou’s MoDem party, reacted violently on Friday to a takeover attempt of the seal, once founded by Valéry Giscard d’Estaing, on the part, on the partisan of the Nouveau Centre party, Hervé Morin, and urged him to no longer use this seal on a document.
Hervé Morin publicly announces his intention to adopt this UDF seal because he believes François Bayrou would not be worthy of defending this political heritage after he had handed his hand to the left.
By the way, the defense minister, whose party partner is the UMP, wanted to reaffirm this strategy on Saturday before the National Council of the NC.
To reprint his efforts, he had received the support of Mr Hervé de Charette (former UMP, now at NC), who lays a claim on the property of the UDF brand, since he was the first to apply for this mark in 2004 at the "Institut national de la propriété Industrie".
However, the office of the party alliance UDF supervised by François Bayrous's friends broke the silencement and made it clear that they could not be expropriated and senteded for the minister as well as under the threat of judicial prosecution a deadline to no longer use this seal and confiscated the property rights of the brand.
You have made numerous statements in recent days that you would propose to your political movement to +acquire the UDF-Seal+, the party alliance wrote in a letter signed by 19 of the 24 office members, including François Bayrou and Marielle de Sarnez, the MoDem party, as well as Senator Jean Arthuis of the Centrist Alliance.
You probably also believed that you had the right to have the name of this movement +Nouveau centre+, the slogan + of today's UDF +, printed on its political material as well as on your website's homepage.
As you know, you aren't entitled to the use of this seal, the UDF's officie, and urges the party to "immediately remove the mention of the name UDF from all material as well as from (its) website."
In addition, Hervé de Charette "demanded to transfer the property rights of the trademark to its legal entitlement".
If you deny this, we will bring an action against you in order to give respect to the law and to defend the legitimate rights of UDF members.
In their letter, they stress that "UDF "has been a full-fledged political movement since 1978 and are reminiscent that Morin and his friends "made the choice" to leave these in 2007 to create a competing movement, as others had already done in 2002, to found the "UMP" party.
The Nouveau centre party, according to the undersigners, has no rights whatsoever to use the UDF seal (Union pour la démocratie française).
They further recall that the last Congress of the UDF in 2007 adopted the three-year accession to MoDem party, as well as the establishment of an office to defend "the material and moral interests" of the movement founded by Valéry Giscard d'Estaing.
As for Hervé de Charette, the office continues, "at the time he applied for the brand name, he was no longer a member of UDF for three years" but of the UMP.
He benefits in a scandalous and fraudulent way, from the lack of deposit of the acronym UDF in order to carry this out for himself.
Furthermore, you consider that this recklessness is well on its way to turn into a crime, and ironize the strategy of the NC: "It seems more dignified to us in politics as well as in life to make a name for ourselves, instead of trying to come out of anonymity by appropriating a name in a fraudulent way on which you have no legal claims."
Affaire Liliane Bettencourt: François-Marie Banier is convicted of exploitation of weakness
Nanterre's court on Friday decided to judge artist François-Marie Banier to determine if, as the daughter of billionaire Liliane Bettencourt claims, he took advantage of a weak moment of the eighty-year-old to sneak up part of her fortune.
The 62-year-old photographer, who is known for his star photos, will have to answer on April 15 and 16, 2010 for exploitation of weakness in court, an offense that is punished with a custodial sentence of a maximum of three years and a fine of 375,000 euros.
This decision means that, in fact, there will be a process in this affaire worthy of the saga of one of France's richest families, in which mother and daughter shred and at the same time have the reins of the cosmetic empire L'Oréal in their hands.
Likewise, however, the 15th Criminal Chamber ordered a medical examination of the 87-year-old Liliane Bettencourt, which is carried out jointly by three doctors.
L'Oréal's heiress and main shareholder has so far refused to undergo such an investigation, especially under the conditions put forward by the Nanterre Prosecutor's Office during the investigation.
Doctors appointed on Friday are due to submit a report before March 10, 2010, in which they clearly announce whether and at what times Liliane Bettencourt may have been in a state of weakness.
I think she's going to under these investigations.
I'm going to talk to her, Ms. Bettencourt's lawyer, Me Georges Kiejman, explained after the trial.
As for Françoise Bettencourt-Meyer's lawyer, Me Olivier Metzner, he is very satisfied with the court's decision.
This is excellent news (...) The report will prove that he (Banier) manipulated this old lady to protect, he affirmed.
For two years, Liliane Bettencourt's only daughter has launched a criminal lawsuit against François-Marie Banier, photographer and all-round artist, accusing him of taking advantage of her mother's weakness for years to steal donations of a total of one billion euros in the form of bank cheques, insurance policies or paintings.
For her part, Liliane Bettencourt had always refuted having been manipulated by the photographer, whom she considers a long-time friend.
Although the court would have to consider the admissibility of the lawsuit initiated by Ms Bettencourt-Meyers, the plaidoyers of lawyers led to a major family debacle on Friday.
Liliane Bettencourt's lawyer, for example, has invoked three letters sent by his client to her notary (1999, 2003 and 2005) in which she wrote that she distrusts Françoise Bettencourt-Meyer's husband.
According to her lawyer, Liliane Bettencourt added the following: "You could please explain to my daughter that for fifteen years, once or twice a day, I have been writing to Banier.
Françoise I have never been able to speak since her marriage.”
Nanterre's public prosecutor's office once again considers that the plaintiff had no right to react in this way, mainly because a guardianship judge in recent days has declined her request to initiate judicial protective proceedings against her mother.
Liliane Bettencourt "is fully legally capable, is not under protection and has no guardian.
No one has the right to act in their place," the prosecutor's representative, Marie-Christine Daubigney, affirmed.
The court has now decided to give a verdict after the trial on the admissibility of Mme Bettencourt-Meyers took place.
managerial revolution or the end of Strategic Early Enlightenment?
If nothing changes in the leadership of the great French companies, our struggle for the development of economic intelligence has been in vain ...
For more than a decade, the attitude of managing large corporations in terms of its employees, the position of the companies vis-à-vis the consumers before the marketing revolution said: it is intended for the staff "values" intended the best, as it used to be on the consumer products designed by the technicians at the plant.
By placing the customer at the heart of the companies, the marketing revolution has given the strategies of conquering the markets in the place ... although the authoritarian tempty to separately impose factors preferences of the customers again regularly!
Demobilization of employees can lead to deadly demoralization.
The suicides at France Telecom, PSA or Renault are just the tip of a much larger phenomenon that touches the employees in particular.
This demobilization negatively impacts the development of all the intellectual functions of the company, especially those of R&D, which is even more important, on those of economic intelligence.
In fact, the IE ( Intelligence Economique = economic intelligence) demands "from all parties involved (...) a proactive approach and a future-oriented point of view"; it claims from the employees a commitment to which the instructions of authoritarian management completely contradict.
Therefore, we demand an operational revolution that would consist of placing employees at the center of the company's concerns on an equal footing alongside customers.
The very conditional benefits of internal opinion polls
Will the internal opinion polls, such as the questionnaire that Orange submitted to its wage earners, stimulate this revolution in terms of management practice?
I'm afraid, sadly not.
First of all, because I was able to realize that, in the face of a new situation after a lot of detoions, the whole structure finally arrives at doing what it has always done.
Also because authoritarianism - one of the pathologies of power - increases proportionally to the importance of its own failures.
And finally, because most executives have a greatly simplified idea of human behavior.
Emerging from the major engineering schools their interpretation of psychology is mechanistic.
Through intellectual comfort, they adopt the old behaviorist theory that has recently been reactivated by U.S. "neurosciences" and unknowingly spread extensively by research institutes and through the media.
Thus, and by suggesting that it is possible to operate the "levers" (sic) on the commitment of employees, a recent Le Figaro.fr survey by Ipsos encourages executives in an attitude towards a mechanistic control system that is unsuitable for today's world.
Identifying and measuring mobilization factors
Instead, an operational revolution necessary for the proper functioning of modern companies requires the adoption of a definitive view of human behavior.
It consists of integrating a basic protocol into every consideration or practice of governance: for wage earners, commitment to the company is only possible if it clearly appears as a means of realizing part of their dreams, their ambition and their projects.
At the moment, only the studies from the multi-functional analysis take over this conception of the motivation of the employees.
These studies have shown that they judge their company on the basis of a predetermined doxa, which, through their perceived image of the company, allows them to assess what it offers them to achieve their life goals.
Thanks to the identification and measurement of these mobilization factors, the executives of the companies can control their "social performance", predict and anticipate their "social performance" and place their human resource management back in the right place.
Thus, they create fertile ground that is favorable for the coordinated development of robust and efficient economic intelligence.
Oslo's speech: the "just war" earns Obama praise from the right
U.S. rights on Friday welcomed the speech delivered by Barack Obama in Oslo the night before and was satisfied that the 2009 Nobel Peace Prize defended the notion of "just war" against the enemies of the United States.
Conservatives, who had mocked the allocation of the prize to Obama on October 9, applauded this time to the president's speech, following the former vice presidential nominee in the 2008 election Sarah Palin.
I liked what he was saying, Palin, the face of the right wing of the Republican Party in the daily USA Today, adding that she herself had raised the issue of a "sometimes necessary" war in her newly published memoirs.
The war, of course, is the last thing I think Americans want to do, but it is necessary.
We have to stop the terrorists down there, she says.
Obama, who inherited two wars from his predecessor George W. Bush, the wars in Iraq and Afghanistan, made an effort Thursday in Oslo to justify the use of force; a way of declaring his decision of nine days ago to dispatch 30,000 additional troops to Afghanistan.
Another Obama opponent, Newt Gingrich, argued that the president had done "very good work" in Oslo.
I found that the speech was really very good, the former president of the House of Representatives rejoiced, who particularly acknowledged the reference to "the existence of evil in the words of Obama."
In some parts, I found this speech to be entirely historic, explained Ginrich, who was questioned on the radio show "The Takeaway".
The previous lawmaker, who is seen by some as a possible nominee for the Republican nomination for the 2012 presidential election, said he was pleased to have seen a "president of the left" who reminded members of the Nobel committee that they "weren't free and couldn't hand out a prize for peace if the use of violence didn't exist."
For Michael Gerson, former writer of George W. Bush and meanwhile editor at the Washington Post, Obama has given "a very American speech."
In addition to portraying himself as a "cosmopolitan," as "he always does," he "spokes like an American president" when he pointed out that Europeans "live like in a security bubble they didn't create themselves and for whose protection they don't do much," according to Gerson.
Among the few, unconvinced right-wingers, former U.N. Ambassador John Bolton, a hawk of the Bush era, described the speech on the National Review Online's website as "boring, high-spirited and inspiration-free."
On the left of the president, Democratic Representative Dennis Kucinich expresses concern about how Barack Obama is defending from resorting to the armed forces.
If the war is packaged under the guise of the judiciary, the massacre of the innocuous can easily be justified, he warns.
However, the press of the moderate left remains praising alongside the president.
The Los Angeles Times, which is still of the view that the Nobel Prize was prematurely awarded to Obama, referred to the address itself for a speaker like him, who is known for his talent, a "monumental work."
The speech in Oslo "provides a schedule to govern international decisions against the conflicts, poverty and oppression," according to the California newspaper enthusiastically.
The New York Times believes that Obama "proclaimed the necessary discourse" and describes his speech as "gloomy and exemplary at the same time."
Long-distance drivers: Three marathons for a deal that secures supply at Christmas
After a three-week negotiating marathon, the unions of the street association and the TLF - the only employers' organisation that remained until the end - came to an agreement on Friday evening that puts an end to the strike order and the looming severe supply shortage at Christmas.
At Friday night's signature meeting "welcome" Secretary of State Dominique Bussereau in charge of transport "welcomed" the agreement, which was initiated on Wednesday morning after several weeks of negotiations.
After a night of negotiations, negotiators parted ways early Friday morning in hopes of quickly signing the agreement in the late morning.
And this despite the fact that the three employers’ associations (FNTR, Unostra and OTRE) had left the meeting.
However, more than six hours from the start of the meeting at 11:00 a.m. to the conclusion of the agreement and it was not until 18:00 that the agreement was signed.
The conflict "would have done a great deal of damage to businesses" and "it was to be feared that it would affect the holidays at the end of the year," according to Bussereau.
The agreement envisages a wage increase of 4% for the lowest wages (local and regional road transport) and 2.9% for the highest wage groups (international transports).
Thus, the hourly rate for employees with the lowest wages rises from 8.72 euros to 9.06 euros.
"That's not very much, but we'll start small," says Patrice Clos (FO).
Activists are now instructed to stop blocking supply centers.
"However, there will probably always be some recalcities who aren't satisfied with the settlement," he adds.
TLF chairman Philippe Grillot spoke of a "good settlement," although it was "difficult to concede these wage increases in the face of international competition."
According to him, the increase is 170 euros per month for the low and 100 to 120 euros for the high wage groups.
"This agreement is based on common sense and respect for employees," Maxime Dumont (CFDT), whereas Thierry Douine (CFTC) spoke of "a historic settlement."
Richard Jaubert (CGT) commented on "an acceptable compromise" in this regard.
The agreement also envisages increasing travel costs by 3% as well as maintaining the overall road transport budget in the first half of 2010, which in particular will make it possible to address the issue of 13th monthly salary and the modernisation of collective agreements.
M. Bussereau recalled that "the sinking of levies for employers amounting to €100 million" was the subject of an amendment tabled by the government's bill for corrected finances for 2009, which was passed by the National Assembly on the night of Thursday to Friday.
The government is calling for "an identical" vote from the Senate.
The signature of the TLF allows that the agreement initially applies to the undertakings affiliated to that organisation and that it subsequently applies to the entire profession in the event of an extension by the Minister of Labour.
This will be the case "in a very short time," Bussereau assures.
The opposition of employers' associations can only delay the proceedings.
The FNTR denounced a "fraudulent trade," because the €100 million "had already been promised to the sector to compensate part of the carbon tax."
The Secretary of State forced us to negotiate as soon as possible, but not on the basis of a joint analysis, but in order not to grieve the goose eaters on the approaching Christmas days, the Unostra laments.
For the Otre association, the agreement threatens "to dig a large grave for the small and medium-sized enterprises of the industry in the coming 12 months."
Line RER A: disruptions this weekend, unions unhappy with RATP's proposals
The disruptions on line RER A, France's largest high-speed rail network for the Greater Paris area and the Île-de-France, continued over the weekend, while a gathering on Friday, the 2nd day of strikes between the unions and the management of RATP (Independent Paris Passenger Transport Administration), continues to await strikers with their demands for wage surcharges.
On this important shopping weekend before Christmas, there were no trains between 10:00 and 20:00. On the central route (between La Défense and the nation), only one of two trains and only one of four trains outside the central line was ready for use.
The connection to SNCF's trains were disrupted at Nanterre Préfecture, forcing travellers to change trains.
Department store chain Galeries Lafayette, which counts 200,000 customers every day, welcomed the fact that "RATP strives to connect the big stores in terms of transport technology."
The RER A line runs through the Paris metropolitan area from west to east.
The RATP section connects Saint-Germain-en-Laye and Nanterre as well as Boissy-Saint-Léger and Marne-La-Vallée.
On Friday, about one of two trains was on schedule at rush hour, and almost none during the low-traffic period.
As on Thursday, there was no crowd on the platforms, as the residents of the Île-de-France had adjusted to the situation.
So early in the morning, Saint-Germain-en-Laye (Yvelines) station was far less crowded than usual, largely because of the lack of high school students.
In Boissy-Saint-Léger (Val-de-Marne), Jack Nbakina, an engineer (29), explained that he would have got up extra "one-and-a-half hours earlier to get to work on time."
The SNCF, which had planned to take care of the passengers by staff and law enforcement personnel, pointed out that there had been no bottlenecks at Saint-Lazare station.
The trains operated by the SNCF on the RER A line or in the direction of Cergy-Le-Haut arrived or departed via Saint-Lazare station to ensure the connection to the metros.
On Saturday and Sunday, meetings of the strikers took place again, during which the results that the associations had classified as "poor" after the meeting with the senior leadership on Friday afternoon were reviewed.
The RER Managing Director proposed the specific drafting of a protocol for the RER A line with regard to all relevant aspects, working hours, professional careers, etc.
However, that'sn't what the drivers want. According to Thierry Garon (Sud), they're asking for a long-due wage surcharge.
The CGT was outraged that the RATP itself puts its wage surcharge proposals in brackets and emphasized that the leadership "is not able to solve a problem, while this was also possible for long-distance drivers and SNCF."
RATP clarified that a re-meeting was scheduled for "comprehensive examination of working conditions" for Monday.
This also demonstrates to the associations that the company "ignores the weekend drivers."
In the morning, about 150 drivers visited a committee branch in Paris.
Once again, it was the senior leadership that has so far rejected any discussion during the strike, suggesting a gathering after calming the situation.
On Tuesday, the conflict threatened to extend to the RER B line following an advance announcement by Unsa and Indépendants.
The associations demand a wage surcharge of 120 (Unsa) to 150 euros, of which 30 euros are variable (CGT, FO, CFDT, Sud, Indépendants). In addition, they lament the deterioration of working conditions on this route, on which millions of travelers travel every day.
The drivers of the RER line, who have many years of professional experience, receive a gross wage of 2,200 to 2,700 euros as well as a wage surcharge of 600 to 650 euros for shift services as well as Sundays and public holidays.
Turkey's Constitutional Court dissolves pro-Kurdish party. Decision with serious consequences
Turkey's Constitutional Court on Friday announced the dissolution of the country's pro-Kurdish main party; a decision that immediately followed furious demonstrations in the Kurdish community in the country's south-east and Istanbul.
This decision threatens to complicate the work of the government enormously complicating, which strives to shake hands with the Kurdish community in order to put an end to the conflicts that have lasted for 25 years.
The "Party for a democratic society" (DTP) has become a "place for activities that are detrimental to the independence of the state and its indivisible unity," the President of the Constitutional Court Hasim Kiliç told the press four days after the decision.
According to Kiliç, the decision would have been taken unanimously by 11 judges, with a majority of seven votes needed to impose the dissolution. In addition, he pointed out that 37 of the party's leaders, including chairman Ahmet Türk and parliamentarian Aysel Tugluk, were excluded from political events for a period of five years.
In addition, the judge promulgated the waiver of parliamentary immunity enjoyed by Türk and Tugluk, as well as confiscation of the party's goods.
The DTP now has 21 seats in Parliament (out of 550).
The party leaders expressed themselves before the verdict that the parliamentarians would rather leave parliament than meet like non-partisan parliamentarians.
The decision followed proceedings initiated in 2007 by the Prosecutor of Turkey's Abdurrahman Yalçinkaya Prosecutor's Prosecutor's Prosecutor's Counter-Bracket-By-Abdurrahman Yalçinkaya. He accused the DTP of following the "instructions" of Kurdistan's Worker's Party (PKK), considered a terrorist organization by Ankara and numerous other countries.
Numerous observers are of the view that the DTP is the legal political showcase of the PKK rebels.
The party assures to have "no connection" to the PKK, but refuses to describe it as a terrorist organization and calls on the government to negotiate.
The verdict of the Constitutional Court radios the government in between, which strives to accommodate the Kurdish community and propose a series of measures to strengthen the rights of the Kurds in order to allow the support of the PKK by the Kurds to dry up and put an end to the conflicts.
The leaders of the DTP reaffirm that a dissolution could lead to a resurgence of tensions in south-eastern Anatolia, where numerous demonstrations against PKK chief Abdullah Öcalan's detention conditions had led to clashes with the police in recent weeks.
Since the court's announcement, thousands of protesters have gathered outside the DTP's premises in the million-dollar city of Diyarbakir in the south-east, which is predominantly populated by Kurds.
Law enforcement deployed tear gas grenades and water cannons as protesters began to throw Molotov cocktails and stones.
Hundreds of people also demonstrated in Istanbul.
According to the verdict, Türk assumed that the court's decision will "deepen the desperation even further."
"Turkey cannot clarify this (Kurdish, editor's note) question on the basis of a party's prohibition," he explained to the press.
The government, made up of a party emerging from the Islamist movement, also regretted the decision.
“We oppose on principle against the closure of the parties.
That doesn't solve the problems," Energy Minister Taner Yildiz said.
"This decision is a torpedo of the democratic open-up promoted by the government," according to political market observer Ahmet Insel.
"One can obviously be prepared for a reaction from the PKK," he adds.
On Thursday, the PKK confessed to an attack in which seven soldiers died three days earlier in northern Turkey, one of the most brutal attacks in recent months.
The snew slump can come
It’s a really crisp winter, that’s what Wolfgang Brauburger wants.
Not because he wants to harass the citizens, says the winter service manager of the FES.
But so that its employees can try out and master the entire vehicle technology.
At the weekend, according to meteorologists, it could be this far for the first time.
More than 330 employees and 120 vehicles are ready to take care of snow and ice.
In doing so, the FES proceeds according to an exact plan.
As soon as it snows nationwide in Frankfurt and the temperatures are below zero degrees, 20 so-called A-routes with large-scattering vehicles will be driven off, explains Brauburger.
These include, for example, main and thoroughfare roads such as the Neue Weg and the Hanauer Landstraße.
But also firefighting access roads, public transport routes, pedestrian zones and routes with gradients, such as the Atzelbergstraße.
The service of the FES begins at three o'clock in the morning
If necessary, the approximately 1040 kilometers would have to be sprinkled in a maximum of three hours with wet salt, which adheres better.
That's why the FES starts at three o'clock at night, "so that the routes are free until the professional traffic starts".
It will be difficult if it doesn't snow until 7.30 a.m.
Then we have a problem, says the winter service manager, "because then no one is willing to make way for the scattered vehicles".
Only after that, if it does not snow further, it is the turn to feeder and access roads, which also include Rat-Beil-Straße.
The last priority is side and side streets.
There will be complaints there, Brauburger knows.
At five o'clock in the morning, the foot people of the FES make their way, danger points and crossings are primarily cleared.
The FES usually cannot take care of all cycle paths.
There is a special service in Frankfurt that keeps an eye on the bridges from 22 to 4.30 p.m., says Brauburger.
Since there is smoothness on this quickly, as soon as the temperature drops below two degrees, the spreader is on the way.
If necessary, it will also be called by the police.
Cradle of Dinosaurs
Tawa hallae looked like many other carnivorous dinosaurs: the two-meter-long and 1.50-meter-tall animal ran on two powerful hind legs, had an elongated skull with sharp, curved teeth, short arms with sickle claws and a long tail.
Had the species lived a few million years later, it wouldn't be special.
Yet Tawa hallae was one of the first dinosaurs with these body features, report Sterling Nesbitt of the University of Texas at Austin and colleagues in Thursday's journal Science.
The animal lived about 215 million years ago in present-day New Mexico, which at that time lay in a dry zone of the Pangea supercontinent on the equator.
Tawa opens a new window on the early evolution of dinosaurs, Nesbitt says.
He shows us a lot about the kinship ratios, the propagation and features of early dinosaurs.
In 2006, Nesbitt and other paleontologists began an excavation at the Ghost Ranch in northern Mexico.
We've sometimes found a dozen dinosaur bones in one day, that's unparalleled for debris from the Triassic, reports team member Randall Irmis of the Utah Museum of Natural History.
Some of the bones the researchers quickly identified as a new species.
In the course of the excavation, they collected the remains of a total of five individuals.
Fund confirms theories about dino-early history
The name "Tawa" comes from the sun god of the Hopi Indians.
For Sterling Nesbitt, it's an extraordinary find: "Dinosaur fossils from the Triassic are extremely rare, usually you only find individual fragments."
Other dinosaur experts don't necessarily share the enthusiasm.
This find is not particularly spectacular, but it confirms some theories about the early history of dinosaurs, says Martin Sander from the University of Bonn.
According to the current doctrine, dinosaurs first appeared about 230 million years ago.
However, many of the oldest fossils are incomplete, which is why the assignment of these primordial dinosaurs is usually controversial.
For example, the experts debate when the lizards split into their three most important lines of development: the carnivorous theropods, which later included the Tyrannosaurus rex, the Velociraptor and also the birds, the four-legged sauropods known as "long necks" and their relatives, and the plant-eating Ornithishia (Bird Basin dinosaurs), for example.
According to Nesbitt's analysis and his colleagues, Tawa hallae is an original theropod.
He has an interesting combination of primitive and advanced properties, Nesbitt says.
On Tawa hallae's skull, for example, the researchers found clues to aerial sacs, such as the descendants of the theropods, the birds.
Thanks to aerial sacks, the breathing of birds is more efficient than that of mammals.
At Tawa hallae, the bones of the spine were apparently partially filled with air.
Tawa is the oldest and most primitive theropod with aerial sacks, says Randall Irmis.
Since the related line of the giant sauropods later also possessed aerial bags and lightweight bones, the common ancestor of both groups had probably already produced this useful innovation.
At least three waves of migration to North America
Because the most primitive dinosaurs so far lived in South America, the researchers conclude that the lizards appeared there for the first time.
Accordingly, the three lines of development separated early on and spread from South America over all continents, which at that time still formed a single land mass as supercontinent Pangea.
This is evidenced by the fact that the team, together with Tawa hallae, found the remains of two other, not particularly closely related theropods.
The three can't be descended from a common, immigrated ancestor, the researchers argue.
We believe there were at least three waves of migration to North America, says Alan Turner of the American Museum of Natural History in New York, a co-author of the Science study.
Of course, there are also contradictions.
An even older species, Silesaurus, which belongs to a sister group of dinosaurs, lived in today's Poland, says Bonn paleontologist Martin Sander.
In his opinion, a distorted picture could arise, because in South America especially many primitive dino fossils have simply been preserved, while there is hardly anything to be found on other continents.
How the dinosaurs managed to develop from a relatively rare sideline of the reptile stem to the dominant land vertebrates of the Earth's Middle Ages is still puzzling.
During the Triasis, the dinosaurs shared their habitat with many other reptiles, for example with the ancestors of today's crocodiles and flying dinosaurs and with numerous other lizards.
Towards the end of the Triassic, the dinosaurs spread around the world and developed many new species, while kinship on the border from the Triassic to the Jurassic died out.
Success story didn't last forever
There are two hypotheses for explaining this change, says Martin Sander.
There may have been a mass extinction that the dinosaurs survived by chance, but the other reptiles did not.
Or the dinosaurs were better adapted, so they could beat the competition out of the field.
In any case, the success story did not last forever.
150 million years later, a meteorite impact of a group of animals helped to flower, which had also already developed in the Triassic and since then a shadowy existence: the mammals.
Solidarity from Vienna to Sao Paulo
The list is long and the signatories are international.
Professors from the University of Toronto stand on it, the University of Cambridge, the University of Vienna or from Berkeley in California.
Lecturers from Naples are there, from Quebec, Edinburgh, New York, Sao Paulo, Berlin or Bremen.
The police eviction of the occupied casino of Frankfurt's Goethe University is making waves worldwide.
278 professors and lecturers from Goethe University, but also from universities throughout Germany, Europe and North and South America, stand in solidarity with the protesting students on the Westend campus.
In a statement written in German and English, they "disapprove" of the police operation initiated by the Bureau.
University President Werner Müller-Estler had cleared the building occupied by students and lecturers on an educational strike in the past week because walls and artworks had been smeared.
Five students were injured.
The material damages, the signatories judge, did not justify "the violent dissolution of self-organised classes of students and teachers by a police squad."
Eviction constitutes an “unacceptable intervention” in the freedom from research and teaching.
The exclusive focus on vandalism serves "the deligitimization and criminalization of the protest" and distracts from the contents.
The Presidium had evaded the necessary discourse.
Lecturers are calling for the immediate cessation of criminal prosecution.
The majority of occupiers did not support the damage to property, it said.
Protest legitimized
The youth of the Verdi trade union and the German Trade Union Confederation are also in solidarity with the students.
The protest is a legitimate expression of a growing concern for one's own future, says Verdi State Youth Secretary Alexander Klein.
Hesse’s Minister of Science Eva Kühne-Hörmann (CDU), meanwhile, defended the actions of university president and the police.
We are grateful to the university president.
Destruction and occupation are completely unacceptable means, she said in a recent hour on Thursday in the Wiesbaden state parliament.
We understand the protest, but no understanding of vandalism, according to CDU member of parliament Peter Beuth.
The opposition factions SPD, Greens and Linke saw it differently.
The CDU takes painted walls as a pretext to criminalize the protest, said Janine Wissler (left).
Instead of dealing with the arguments and demands of the students, they have been cleared.
The left has tabled a motion calling on the interior minister to give report on the police operation.
New Silicon Valley
For many years it was considered a secret city, inaccessible to Western visitors.
Selenograd, one of ten districts of the capital Moscow, was a closely guarded location in the Soviet Union for secret arms research.
Today, the commune with its 216 000 inhabitants, 37 kilometers from the center of Moscow, is considered "Russian Silicon Valley".
Significant research and production facilities for micro- and nanoelectronics are located here.
Since September 2008, representatives of Selenograd had established contacts with Riedberg from Frankfurt – with the university institutes and the companies of the Frankfurt Innovation Center (FIZ).
There were several mutual visits and on Wednesday Selenograd and Riedberg signed a protocol for closer cooperation in Frankfurt.
Moscow's deputy mayor Yuri Rosljak and Prefect Anatoly Smirnov of Selenograd led the Russian delegation, while planning secretary Edwin Schwarz signed for Frankfurt.
In 2010, representatives of the FIZ and small and medium-sized enterprises of the scientific research focus will travel to Selenograd for a seminar.
Scientific exchange
It is about a continuous scientific exchange.
But it's also about economic relations.
FIZ companies are made possible to gain a foothold in the growing Russian market.
Conversely, Russian science companies want to snatch orders in Germany.
Selenograd is the headquarters of major Russian research institutes and companies.
These include, for example, the Moscow Institute of Electron Technology, the large corporations Sitronics and Rosssijskaya Elektronika.
The commune was founded in 1958 as a so-called "socialist planned city" for arms research.
Selenograd (literally translated "green city") got its name from the wooded hilly landscape in which the research facilities are embedded.
Stuttgart braking block
The dimension of Stuttgart 21 cannot be set high enough.
For the proponents of the project, it is simply about "the new heart of Europe".
S21 is much more than lowering a station, more than a monstrous construction project that will plunge the city of the Baden-Württemberg state capital for a decade into wholesome construction site chaos.
Stuttgart 21 emerged from the spirit of the 1980s.
Head stations with tracks that end in the middle of the city at that time were considered 19th-century traffic-technical fossils that acted like brake pads of modern rail traffic, as it once took an enormous amount of time to encompass locomotives from the back to the front.
Drive in, hold for a second, keep driving - that's how it's supposed to go.
And that's why the head stations should disappear: in Frankfurt and Munich.
And in Stuttgart: a drive-through station, which is about halfway along the trans-European rail magistrale between Paris and Budapest/Bratislava - hence the slogan of the "Heart of Europe".
S21 is now actually being tackled, even though the visions of the 1980s are no longer compatible with reality.
Even very ambitious planners have recognized that it is almost unaffordable in the Hessian and Bavarian cities to implement the lowering.
However, head stations no longer have an entry and exit problem, since there are modern reversal trains with traction vehicles and control cars at both ends.
Nevertheless: Stuttgart 21 next to the ICE new construction line to Ulm is now being tackled.
State and local politicians enforced it.
The railway participates, also because the risks of the largest individual infrastructure project of the republic of taxpayers and not the state-owned corporation.
In terms of urban planning, the project can still be reasonably justified.
New urban space is created, which - according to the computer-generated graphics of the planners - is equipped with a lot of greenery.
S21 is harmful to the rail transport system.
The transit station and the extension of the route are intended for passenger traffic, to which - typical 80s - huge growth was attributed.
At that time, there was not yet the competition of low-cost airlines.
Modern transport policy, however, must first be about strengthening freight transport, as transport by rail is considerably more environmentally friendly than by truck on the road.
But the new line is not suitable for freight trains because of heavy gradients.
It is even more serious that S21 indirectly affects many other railway projects.
A good six billion euros are to be builded in Swabian.
That's the official figure.
Experts consider ten to $15 billion to be realistic.
It is certain that in the next decay the money will be missing from other hubs and bottlenecks on the network.
A total of sixty larger projects are on the list of the Ministry of Transport.
One of the most important is the expansion of the 180-kilometer route through the Rhine Valley.
Since 1987, it has been built on the route at a slow-motion pace.
Urgently necessary to press correctly on the tube here.
However, S21 will act as a brake block.
The Rhine valley line becomes a bottleneck for international freight transport: right in the heart of Europe.
Government examines verdict on airport
The state government has left open in the Wiesbaden state parliament whether it will go into appeal against the ruling of the Hessian Administrative Court (VGH) on the night flight ban.
The CDU/FDP coalition has commissioned experts to legally examine the more than 400-page VGH verdict, said Economy Minister Dieter Posch (FDP).
The VGH in Kassel had reasoned in writing on these days that in the event of the construction of the planned north-whelving runway in Frankfurt Airport, the country had to re-regulate the night flight ban.
The approval of 17 flights between 23 and 5 o'clock, as stipulated by the state government, is not to be reconciled with the legally required protection of the population against nocturnal aircraft noise.
The Coalition must decide by January 4 whether to appeal against the verdict.
The opposition criticized this postponement and insisted on the representatives of the government to waive a revision at the Federal Administrative Court in Leipzig.
SPD parliamentary group leader Torsten Schäfer-Gümbel called on CDU and FDP to return to their promises made before the election on a night flight ban. Otherwise, this should be seen as a "wordbreak".
It's damn hard if you're allowed to keep your word, Greens leader Tarek Al-Wazir said in reference to the FDP election slogan "We keep word."
Minister Posch dismissured Schäfer-Gümbel's accusation that the state government is trying to reach its night flights with "right-waning and trickery".
A close legal examination needs their time.
The Coalition does not want to operate on the basis of a "quick first impression."
We need a legally compliant decision, said Stefan Müller (FDP).
Just as portrayed by the opposition, his faction's statements on the night flight ban had never been.
U.S. banks pay back aid
The crisis-ridden U.S. major banks are stripping off their state shackles.
On Wednesday, the Bank of America repaided aid from the bank rescue fund Tarp totaling $45 billion (a good €30 billion) in one fell swoop.
Citigroup is also planning to reimburse state aid, according to a report by CNBC.
The institutes want to get rid of the restrictions associated with the acceptance of the money – for example, the limitation in the payment of manager bonuses as well as the payment of high fees.
Bank of America had raised a good $19 billion of fresh money from investors via convertible bonds.
The rest of the sum she took from her own cash register, which, among other things, was well filled by divisional sales.
On Wednesday evening, bank chief Kenneth Lewis thanked the American taxpayer: The cash injection had helped over a very difficult time.
At the height of the financial crisis, the money housing with the takeover of investment bank Merrill Lynch.
Citigroup also wants to procure money on the capital market in order to free its liabilities to the state.
As early as Thursday, the U.S. bank could announce a roughly $20 billion increase in its capital, CNBC reported, citing circles.
A Citigroung spokesperson declined to comment.
Goldman Sachs, JP Morgan Chase and Morgan Stanley had already repaided their aid in June.
Fast choice
It all went faster than I thought.
Barely two weeks after the ZDF board of directors had forced the incumbent editor-in-chief Nikolaus Brender out of office, the broadcaster can present a successor.
Last night, the panel wanted to vote on staffing.
Peter Frey, head of the ZDF-Hauptstadtstudio in Berlin, was considered the favorite.
However, there was no confirmation from the broadcaster for this - officially, in order not to prejudge the proceedings.
However, it should also have played a role not to further damage ZDF director Markus Schächter if this proposal does not come through with the board of directors.
Frey, Schächter's candidate, needs the support of nine of the 14 committee members.
The result of the vote had not yet been determined at the end of this issue.
Two weeks agove, Schächter failed with his proposal to extend the contract with Nikolaus Brender for another five years.
Only seven members spoke in favour of Brender, seven voting against him. Brender's deselection was a bitter defeat for Schächter, but an expectable one.
After all, the Union majority in the Board of Directors had already signaled weeks before the election that it did not want to confirm Brender in office.
Hessian Prime Minister Roland Koch had organized the resistance against the editor-in-chief.
With the argument that under Brender the ZDF's news broadcasts had lost ratings, he declined another tenure of the highly respected editor-in-chief.
Shortly before the election, therefore, there were protests in which political influence on the ZDF was scourged.
Most recently, even 35 state lawyers had interfered in the debate, assessing Koch's actions as unconstitutional.
It's been of no use.
To the person
Peter Frey was born on August 4, 1957 in Bingen.
He studied politics, pedagogics and Romance studies.
After that, he worked at Südwestfunk and Frankfurter Rundschau.
In 1983 Frey moved to ZDF, initially to "heute-journal".
Subsequent stations: 1988 to 1990 personal speaker of the editor-in-chief; 1991/92 correspondent in Washington; until 1998 director and presenter of the "morning magazine"; until 2001 head of ZDF foreign policy; since then head of the ZDF-Hauptstadtstudio.
The Greens announced a constitutional lawsuit after Brender's deselection, which on Wednesday was still elected journalist of the year by the industry newspaper Medium Magazin.
Koch's counterpart on the board of directors, Rhineland-Palatinate Prime Minister Kurt Beck, declared last week that countries should amend the ZDF state treaty in such a way that personnel proposals of the director cannot be blocked by a politically motivated majority.
An excerpt of party politicians from the supervisory bodies of the ZDF, however Beck ruled out.
Brender's anticipated successor Peter Frey had recommended himself to the board of directors with a smart move - by fiercely criticising the panel for it immediately after the Brender deselection.
Thanks to this evidence of journalistic independence, Schächter could nominate him for the post of editor-in-chief and the Board of Directors could nominate him without a loss of face and the Board of Director to elect him, without suspecting that a party-goer would be held.
In party arithmetic, Frey is attributed to the left-liberals anyway.
Studies are decluttered
Partial success for the students: After a year of the educational strike, the culture ministers of the countries finally intervened on Thursday: At a meeting of ministers' ministerial and university rectors' conference, both sides agreed to want to improve the study conditions for bachelor's and master's degrees.
In the future, students should also be able to complete their studies.
In detail, they agreed to reduce the exam loads: In principle, each study module should only be completed with a single examination.
In addition, the workload of the students is to be checked and "a realistic and justifiable measure" is to be ensured.
Politicians and universities also want to simplify the recognition of examination achievements between universities within and outside Germany.
The countries pledged to make common structural requirements more flexible for bachelor’s and master’s degree programmes.
In addition, there should be no deviating country regulations.
Fewer tests
Since the beginning of the student protests in June, ministers of culture and university rectors have been wearing the winged word of better studyability in their mouths.
Now, for the first time, it has been filled with concrete content.
The so-called accreditation council decided to drastically reduce the number of examinations: "Each module usually concludes with only one exam," according to a 30-page paper, which is intended to partially re-regulate the admission of degree programs.
With the "significant reduction of the burden of the examination burden," said Reinhold Grimm, chairman of the accreditation council, a "misdevelopment that significantly compromised studyability."
In addition, "studiability" should be "the decisive criterion" in the design in the future.
Modules such as content should be "strictly checked whether "unnecessary limitations of the students in the choice of
events" would be avoided.
The students were "entitled to maintain scope, enable and promote initiative."
The rules should apply when admitting new, but also in the review of existing degree programs.
These must be "re-accredited" every five years – but this means that it will take until 2015 for all degree programmes to be reviewed.
Ten accreditation agencies throughout Germany are responsible for this, which are under the accreditation council established by the countries.
The ministers of culture had urged the countries "emphatically" in October to act on the agencies in such a way that the substance is not too large and the examination regime is not too strict.
Universities and universities of applied sciences in individual federal states have already agreed with the state ministries on appropriate measures.
Rhineland-Palatinate was the only state to date pledge additional resources for the further development of the Bologna process.
From the money, the universities are primarily intended to establish tutor programs and to strengthen study counseling as well as examination and foreign offices.
State and universities want to spend tens of millions of euros on this.
In early 2010, the government intends to introduce an amendment to the Higher Education Act in Parliament.
According to this, only a single examination should be necessary for the completion of a study model.
The design of studies should become more flexible, in addition, the recognition of certificates at other universities should be ensured and access to the master's degree should be facilitated.
By the end of next year, all the country's degree programmes are to be checked for their studyability.
The government and the opposition had argued again on Thursday about the study conditions in the country.
In addition to praise, however, there was also criticism of the decisions.
The Federal Executive Board of the Juso University Groups demanded that the design of the education policy should no longer take place "behind closed doors" of ministers and university rectors in the future.
At the latest after the educational strikes, the students as those affected should "have a say".
The future academics received support from the president of the Higher Education Rectors' Conference (HRK), Margret Wintermantel: "We need the students' experiences," she said on Thursday at a meeting with the ministers of culture with which the HRK advised on a joint reform paper.
As a whole, the Bonn decisions did not go far enough for the Education and Science (GEW) trade union: it calls for a legal claim that would allow every graduate with a bachelor’s degree to access a secondary master’s degree.
On the other hand, the countries must ensure that today's generation of bachelor's students there are sufficient master's degree places available tomorrow with the best possible study conditions, according to GEW board member Andreas Keller.
Countries need to hire significantly more lecturers.
However, the ministers of culture did not comment on a more free access to the master.
More than a footnote to Herta Müller
Going into Germany and there is almost no scrutiny..." is the opening line of a poem by the lyricist Werner Söllner, in which he in disturbing shreds of language addresses the arrival experience of a dissident who has gained freedom, but at the same time lost his language space.
Werner Söllner, born in Banat, Romania in 1951, had first worked as a German and English teacher in Bucharest and later as a lecturer in a children's book publisher before he moved to Frankfurt am Main in 1982.
Söllner has been awarded various literary prizes, in 1993 he held the legendary Frankfurt Poetics lectures at Goethe University, and since 2002 he has been head of the Hessian Literature Forum at the Mousonturm in Frankfurt.
Last Tuesday he caused a considerable extra-literary sensation during a conference in Munich, which dealt with "German literature in Romania in the mirror and distortion of its securitat acts".
Visibly unsettled and embarrassed, Söllner reased a statement in front of Romanian-German writer colleagues, including Richard Wagner, William Totok, Franz Hodjak, and Helmuth Frauendorfer, his cooperation as an IM for the Romanian secret service Securitate.
Together with some of the attendees, Söllner had received the German Language Prize in 1989.
The recruitment attempts would have begun creepingly, first in 1971.
I'm, Söllner concluded, "so someone who hasn't defended himself enough against intimidation attempts by the Securitate."
The lyricist Werner Söllner is IM Walter.
Almost at the same time as the Nobel Prize ceremony for Herta Müller and its unprecedented literary engagement with the mechanisms of dictatorial domination, the literary scene, and in particular the community of German authors from Romania, is shaken by a case that identifies the abysses and loathing of a political system based on surveillance and splying.
Werner Söllner is not only perpetrators, but also the victim, as far as one can say according to previous knowledge.
In his Securitate file, IM Walter played a role, said the literary scholar Michael Markel after Söllner’s confession and referred to the positive influence of the IM Walter for him.
He "browed him out" in all points that were difficult for him at the time, according to Markel.
It is a moral need for him to make this remark.
Söllner's explanation was unexpected in the form and in front of this audience, but the confession of a former dissident and his cooperation with the Romanian secret service is not completely surprising.
Germany had been a cozy reserve for Securitate-Spitzel, Herta Müller had already written in July, after reading her own file, 914 pages.
Those affected, it may be assumed, are speculated and in some cases probably also knew which clear name was hidden behind the alias Sorin, Voicu and Walter.
In contrast to the now very procedural inspection of the Stasi files in this country, the handling of the legacy of the Securitate surveillance is still very diffuse.
Systematic enlightenment has been undermined and hindered in recent years.
A National Council for the Reappraisal of the Securitate Files (CNSAS) in accordance with the Birthler Authority (CNSAS) was only established in 1999, but since then the files have been rather sluggish.
Only since 2005 there has been a law that regulates the management of the files.
The fact that the influence of former Securitate members in Romania is still very large was evidenced by an interview after the announcement of the Nobel Prize for Herta Müller, in which a former Securitate boss from Temesvar expressed derogatory comments about the laureate.
She has a psychosis, after all, she has only been interrogated once.
The infamy of power meanted to be able to express itself blatantly in the form of freedom of expression.
Certainly, as so often in connection with concealed guilt, there are inevitable questions attached to the Söllner case.
Why now? Whom was harmed? The prudent reaction of the listeners to Söllner's confession gives hope that the continuation of this chapter will be determined by the urge for knowledge rather than blame and shame.
To the genesis of Herta Müller's work, which received the Nobel Prize yesterday, it is more than a footnote.
Saturation supplement Champions League
It seemed as if there was just coming an ardressed field worker from a very nice Christmas party, at which a sporting goods manufacturer had distributed goodies, when the new VfB trainer Christian Gross climbed into a summoned C-Class a quarter of an hour after midnight accompanied by an orange plastic bag. ̈
The man with the striking baldness was chauffeured because of the city, which was still foreign to him.
Otherwise he could have driven himself - alcohol was not in the game after all, and the 55-year-old was not drunk with happiness.
Had been a pleasant evening, but in the morning the work just continues.
Or really get going first.
In the words of the Swiss committed only three days earlier: "While we have reached the quarter-finals", namely in the Champions League thanks to the 3:1 success against Unirea Urziceni from Romania, "but we must not be dazzled by the success: it will be a brutally tough fight against relegation."
“The much more important task is imminent on Sunday.
Then it is necessary to repeat the premier class performance in everyday league life in Mainz.
And put off the weaknesses revealed again after the quick three goals by Marica (5th), Träsch (8th) and Pogrebnyak (11th).
Draw
The quarter-finals of the Champions League (first leg 16/17. + 23/24 February / second rematches 9./10. + 16/17 March) will be drawn in Nyon on 18 December.
Thus, the joy of the fullious debut of the Babbel successor remained subdued.
Not only with the supposed bringer of salvation, who had conveyed self-confidence, readiness for action and joy of playing the unsettled Stuttgart professionals in the short time.
I approached the players and told them to remember their strengths and encouraged them to be more courageous, explained the simple-sounding recipe for success, which had no effect under therapist Babbel.
He's sparked some magic, Babbel supporter Jens Lehmann admitted, "when a new impulse comes up in football, sometimes things happen that you wouldn't have thought possible."
And Sami Khedira, who became the best player on the pitch thanks to his momentum thanks to his momentum, tried to describe the identified "phenomenon" with the proverbial "fresh wind" and the workings of the new supervisor: "He's a man who reaches through and demands discipline.
This is what we need in the current situation.”
Under Babbel, there had obviously been a lack of it.
The VfB Stuttgart is not yet over the mountain, warns sports board member Horst Heldt
VfB Stuttgart, however, especially needs consistency.
Like Gross, players and managers were wary of overvaluing the victory against a substitute-weakened and completely overwhelmed defense of the Romanian guests.
They had also won in Glasgow, before four days later in Leverkusen at 0:4 the bad awakening had come, Khedira said.
And sporting director Horst Heldt warned that although one season goal had been achieved, the other was still a long way off.
We'ren't over the mountain yet.
The fact that Heldt was on his 40th birthday even after the first home win in three months was due to the events of the weekend, which revived in the form of a television interview broadcast immediately before the start of the game by goalkeeper Jens Lehmann.
There are decisions that others make and not the Jens, Heldt countered Lehmann's harsh criticism.
He had accused the board of leadership weakness and had decided to change the coach on the pressure of pubescent fans.
Lehmann also considered that his day off had been canceleded after games as a sign of a lack of sovereignty and disfavor.
Lehmann's statements were "shaped by pure selfishness," Heldt wedged back and announced consequences for the closing man.
They're not going to be friends for life anymore, the two forty-year-olds.
After all, however, they agreed with everyone else that without the preparatory work by the detached coaching team, the playfully and combatively strong performance would not have been possible.
Babbel and his assistants had "a big share in the success," Heldt and Lehmann said in unison - and the man with the plastic bag also agreed.
Heavyweight World Cup fight
Heated duel in Bern: Challenger Kevin Johnson repeatedly provoked world champion Vitali Klitschko with words and gestures.
However, after twelve hard rounds, the Ukrainian defended his heavyweight title according to the WBC.
The verdict of the point judges was unanimous.
Vitali Klitschko will remain boxing world heavyweight champion.
The 38-year-old champion according to version of the association WBC defeated on the night on Saturday in Bern the American Kevin Johnson unanimously after points (120:108, 120:108, 119:109).
For Klitschko, it was the third successful title defense after his comeback in October 2008.
Previously, the Ukrainian has twice been world champion of WBO and WBC associations.
Unfortunately it didn't work out with the knockout that everyone was expecting.
Klitschko extended his record to 39 wins in 41 professional fights, twice he had to concede defeat due to injuries.
Challenger Johnson had to accept the first defeat in the 24th fight.
In front of around 17,000 spectators at the Berner Arena, the 2.02-metre Klitschko wanted to dictate the fight, especially with his left-wing leadership.
The eleven-centimeter-smaller Johnson, who had announced the end of the Klitschko era before the duel, but repeatedly evaded the beatings of the world champion, so that Klitschko could not decisively bring his dangerous rights to the finish.
Overall, the passive Johnson did too little for a challenger and only tried to make ends meet.
Although he provoked Klitschko with words and gestures, his strokes rarely hit the world champion.
After all, he is only the second boxer after Timo Hoffmann in 2000, who went with Klitschko over the full distance of twelve laps.
The challenger had caused some swirl before the fight in the Swiss capital.
He insulted Klitschko as an "ugly zombie" who "can't do anything right."
While weighining, the champion then let himself be provoked on Friday to push the sunglasses off Johnson's nose "to be able to see his eyes."
Fourth victory after Klitschko's comeback
Klitschko also remained victorious in his fourth fight after his comeback on 11 October 2008.
After almost four years off, he had beaten the Nigerian Samuel Peter in Berlin and retrieved "his" WBC belt, which he previously had to surrender without a fight.
This year he defeated Juan Carlos Gomez from Cuba in his title defenses and Chris Arreola from the USA in September.
I've only dealt one stroke in this fight and I'm in very good shape physically, Klitschko had justified the quick return to the ring.
However, his dream remains the unification of all four important titles in the family.
Brother Wladimir is world champion of WBO and IBF associations.
The belt of the WBA, however, is weared by the Briton David Haye after he was able to dethrone the 2.13-meter-tall Nikolai Valuyev in November.
Haye had earlier canceled in the summer a fight against Vitali Klitschko scheduled for September.
His promotional company is already planning the fight against Klitschko at London's Wembley Stadium.
Kunduz case
Frontal attack from the opposition: Green Group leader Jürgen Trittin has described Defense Minister Guttenberg as a liar because of false information on the Kunduz affair.
SPD and leftists call for clarification from the chancellor.
Hamburg/Berlin - According to the latest findings on Kunduz's air raid, the opposition Defense Minister Karl-Theodor zu Guttenberg (CSU) has subordinated a deliberate deception.
The minister "knowingly said the untruth" to the deadly bombardment on two tanker trucks from September, said Greens parliamentary leader Jürgen Trittin on Saturday in the ARD news program "Tagesschau".
It’s commonly called it: he lied.
The order to kill had been given in defiance of the rules of the International Protection Force for Afghanistan (ISAF).
This is the substance of that NATO had that Mr. Guttenberg has read, Trittin said.
On Friday, it was revealed that the attack was not only about destroying the kidnapped trucks, but also about deliberately killing two Taliban leaders.
In the US airstrike requested by the German Kunduz commander Colonel Klein, up to 142 people had been killed or injured, according to the NATO investigation report.
Greens, SPD and Left on Saturday called on German Chancellor Angela Merkel (CDU) to speak immediately in front of the Bundestag and to clarify what Guttenberg really knew about the incident.
Mrs Merkel must clarify whether a strategy of targeted killing is part of the federal government's Afghanistan policy - and whether chancellery, Bundeswehr and intelligence have endorsed this new strategy, demanded Jürgen Trittin and Green Party colleague Renate Künast.
Government spokesman Ulrich Wilhelm strongly rejected the criticism.
The Chancellery did not influence concrete deployments of the Bundeswehr in Afghanistan, he said.
On Saturday, doubts had also been raised about the ministry's portrayal about the background to the dismissal of Inspector-General Wolfgang Schneiderhan and State Secretary Peter Wichert.
According to the SPIEGEL, the two Guttenberg have correctly and completely informed about the backgrounds of the tanker truck attack.
Officially, it had said they had denied the existence of Nato reports and deprived the minister of sensitive details.
However, according to the two top officials, it said that they had even pointed out several reports to the Ministry of Defense, including a two-page document by commanding Colonel Klein and a field-hunter report.
Wichert is said to have now asked for clarification of the facts in a letter, reports the "Frankfurter Allgemeiner Sonntagszeitung".
He has not yet received an answer, he said.
The ministry did not comment on the allegations on Saturday and pointed out that this would be clarified in the inquiry committee of the Bundestag, which intends to be constituted next Wednesday.
Tream Worlds
Some see pictures in an awake state and hear voices that others don't see or hear.
Not only in the case of mentally ill people and in the drug frenzy, the boundaries between imagination and reality are blurred.
As long as it happens during sleep, everything's fine.
If we lie in our dark rooms with our eyes closed, we are allowed to give ourselves uninhibited to the wildest ideas and imaginatives.
However, anyone who experiences dreamlike states in wakiness, sees and hears things that others do not see or hear, is usually considered crazy or drug-addicted in the modern Western world.
But such images and voices arise not only in the mentally ill or in the drug frenzy.
Our imagination is to blame: the same neural processes that allow us to design houses, paint pictures or write novels sometimes ensure that the boundaries between dream, delusion and reality are blurred.
Dream-like altered waking consciousness states can occur in a variety of forms, at best we call them visions, in the worst case delusion.
Even though triggers and effects may differ, the visions of the mystic Hildegard von Bingen, the drug experiences of the new-age prophet Carlos Castaneda, shamanic rapture states, psychosis and hallucinations can still be attributed to similar processes.
Hallucinations or delusions arise when the balance of neural messengers of the brain - especially dopamine, norepinephrine, serotonin and acetylcholine - is derailed and the interaction of different parts of the brain is disturbed.
Complex hallucinations and delusional thoughts occur very frequently during schizophrenic psychosis.
Gravity has no more power over him
The connection between delusion and dream has occupied philosophers and physicians since antiquity.
Aristotle understood hallucinations as a form of dream in the waking state, Wilhelm Griesinger, one of the founders of modern psychiatry, stated in 1861 a "great similarities of madness to dream states", for Sigmund Freud the dream was even "a psychosis, with all inconsistencies, delusions, delusions of such."
It's the fall of 2001, when Henning T. turns into a comics character.
His body is two-dimensional and squeaky.
Gravity no longer has power over him, he floats on the ceiling.
He senses a kick.
Behind him winged devil figures with blood-reded, distorted faces, teeth-flattering.
They chase him, kick him with heavy, flaming boots, throw him against the walls, he senses the pain all over his body.
Loud, vicious voices roar out of walls and radio.
He can no longer endure them, the voices that insult and insult him.
Henning T. tears two cables out of the stereo system, knots them into a sling and attaches them to a hook to the ceiling.
He climbs a loudspeaker and puts the noose around his neck.
Jumping.
The snarl loosens.
That's supposed to be a lesson for you, screaming the voices in drum-shredding choir, "go to the clinic and let yourself heal!"
Today Henning T. is 40 years old, he has experienced six acute psychosises, the last is 2 years ago, the first almost 20 years.
was triggered by LSD.
His diagnosis is schizoaffective-paranoid psychosis, in addition, he is manic-depressive, he takes medication for depression, mania, schizophrenia.
He has learned to live with his illness and has been married for three years.
In psychosis I've witnessed worst fear and deepest desperation, he says.
The deceital images are cruel reality
About one percent of the population suffers from schizophrenia at least once in a lifetime, in psychiatric clinics she is treated as the second most common condition after depression.
In acute schizophrenic psychosis, sufferers can usually no longer distinguish between the inside and the outside world, they fall into an exaggerated state that can lead to hallucinations, size, relationship or persecution.
Particularly common is listening to voices - voices that command, comment or dialogue.
Optical hallucinations are not uncommon either.
The sufferer is no longer able to separate the important from the unimportant, the brain can no longer filter out disturbing signals, the consciousness is flooded with impressions.
Like the dream, psychosis also opens the floodgates for a flood of ideas and fantasies that come from deeper layers of consciousness.
The perception of reality in psychosis is subject-centered, says Professor Thomas Fuchs, senior physician at the Department of General Psychiatry at Heidelberg University Hospital.
The schizophrenic is located at the center of the action, everything has a meaning directed towards it.
At the same time, he finds himself in a passive role, like in a dream, overwhelmed by the events and cannot actively shape them.
Schizophrenics, therefore, often feel threatened, controlled and manipulated.
A reality comparison is usually not possible for them, the illusions are cruel reality, waking up excluded.
having experienced a hallucination once in a life
Unlike in the dream, Fuchs says, "the sensual and spatial structure of reality perceived in psychosis is fundamentally preserved."
The delusional thoughts and hallucinations are based in reality, the alienation of the experience takes place in the real framework.
Why exactly our brain beats capriciousness, researchers are stirrudging.
States of exceptions can cause everyone to lose the reality reference briefly, says Fuchs.
However, not everyone will develop psychosis.
There appears to be a genetic predisposition to schizophrenia, but twin studies have shown that the genes aren’t the sole determining factors.
Researchers assume that there is minimal brain damage in those affected.
We suspect that neural maturation of the brain is disrupted at an early stage of development, for example by a mother's viral disease during pregnancy, Fuchs explains.
But it remains a mystery how the disturbance of the neural system comes about, which does not manifest itself at first for many years.
Environmental influences also play a role - stress or traumatic experiences can be triggers for psychosis when disposition is present.
Hallucinogenic drugs can also trigger psychosis as they interfere with the transmitter system.
When our brain runs hot
The crazy thing is, psychotics suffer from their hallucinations and swallow expensive medications to get rid of them, others spend money on drugs to get some, says Lübeck psychology professor Erich Kasten.
Whether images are perceived as pleasurable or unpleasant is often only a matter of point of view.
Kasten has been collecting reports of hallucinations and deceivities for ten years, last year he evaluated his findings in the book "The unreal world in our mind".
Hallucinations are one of the typical side effects of psychosis, but also of surprisingly many other diseases.
In addition to mental disorders such as borderline or depression, this often includes diseases such as migraine, epilepsy, multiple sclerosis or Alzheimer's disease and traumatic brain trauma.
Surprisingly, under certain conditions, even the brains of healthy people hallucinate.
Hallucinations, according to Kasten, are composed of memory contents that are usually withheld.
They arise when brain areas in which sensory perceptions are processed, overactive or inhibited; when our brain runs hot or even when stimulation is lacking.
Drugs can trigger this condition in the same way as brain damage or psychosis.
But also stimulus deprivation, stress, heartbreak or other mental states of emergency.
In more recent studies, up to one-third of respondents said they had experienced hallucination at least once in a lifetime.
In the summer of 2005 Sarah K. is 16 years old, she lives in a small town in Mecklenburg-Vorpommern and attends the Gymnasium.
It's a normal girl, actually.
If it weren't for the pictures and voices.
During the break, it can happen that a sloppy boy with long, dark hair addresses it.
When it responds to him, classmates look at it surprised - no one else sees or hears this boy.
They consider the shy girl to be a spun daydreamer, but Sarah K. doesn't dream, she knows that.
She's awake, she hears and sees this boy, just as she hears and sees the girl in the bank beside herself.
She can even sense his presence, at least for moments.
It's a pleasant feeling, she likes the boy.
Am I crazy? she sometimes wonders.
But she knows that this boy and the other illusions aren't real and are disappearing.
At night, appearances are mostly frightening
Often the illusions are also a burden.
When sitting in the classroom, she sometimes can’t follow the teaching material, because the teacher’s eyes and ears is introducing a new classmate in her eyes and ears, while explaining mathematical equations for everyone else on the board.
At night, appearances are mostly frightening.
When she waits in her dark room for sleep, which comes far too rarely and far too late, she often can't distinguish whether she's awaking or already dreaming, whether the pictures she sees the faces or the girl with the bleeding cuts on her arms and legs, in front of her open or closed eyes emerge.
Leave me alone, she yells at the figures.
Parents and friends don't tell them about it, they would think they're crazy.
Autumn 2009. Sarah K. is 20 years old today.
She graduated from high school last year and lives with her boyfriend.
A year ago, the hallucinations stopped.
Since she contacted the psychologist box on the internet, she knows she'sn't crazy.
Delusion is also a work of art of desperation
If someone is socially isolated or suffers from stimulus deprivation, they are most likely to have hallucinations after a while, Kasten says.
I suspect that the brain is then undemployed and takes care of its own entertainment.
Sarah K., an only child, was afraid of other children as a little girl, often she played alone.
Her hallucinations began when she learned at 13 that the man she's been calling dad since she can't think isn't her biological father.
Since in visual and acoustic hallucinations, the same areas of the brain are active, which also process real impressions and noises, it is difficult to distinguish people like Sarah K. to distinguish illusions and reality. "Every experience," says Kasten, "takes place in the brain, the reality takes place there.
Hallucinations are also images that create the brain, that’s why they seem so real.”
Even nocturnal dream images value Kasten as a form of hallucination.
The hallucinations of the healthy do not differ fundamentally from those of acute psychosis.
Unlike schizophrenics, psychically healthy people are able to recognize their deceptions as unreal.
Kasten therefore advises his patients to value the hallucinations as a special ability.
Boundary state between dream and hallucination
Unfortunately, imaginary experience is mostly pathologized in our too much mind-centered world, says Michael Schmidt-Degenhard, professor in Heidelberg and chief physician of the Department of Psychiatry and Psychotherapy at the Florence-Nightingale-Hospital in Düsseldorf.
A psychosis is a serious, sorrowful illness, but it also harbors positives, creative - delusion is also a work of art out of desperation.
Schmidt-Degenhard has explored the so-called oneiroid form of experience, a special form of the traumatically altered waking consciousness.
Oneiroids are highly complex, detailed quasi-dreams, which the experiencer considers to be real and that he cannot distinguish from the waking state even in hindsight.
The oneiroid state of consciousness arises in trauma- or disease-related extreme situations, for example in polyradiculitis patients who experience motor paralysis with full consciousness.
When a person is threatened by self- and world loss, for example by persistent, complete inability to move, he replaces the life-threatening real situation with his own, imaginary world that is decoupled from reality, says Schmidt-Degenhard.
a kind of boundary state between dream and hallucination.
Since it is not possible to cope with a situation in reality, a change of the world takes place, but the real situation is presented in the imagination.
An intense form of sensibility and meaning in an unbearable situation that allows us people their own ability not to perish in a nonsense of the loss of consciousness.
Everything is real, even later in the memory
Zurich-based art historian Peter Cornelius Claussen also describes his oneiroid experiences in his book "Heart Change" as a rescue operation of the soul.
He is 49 years old when his consciousness embarks on trips for days after undergoing heart valve surgery with subsequent heart transplantation, while his body is condemned to exerciselessness in the intensive care unit.
Claussen immerses himself in a self-created world, visits strange times and places: he experiences himself as a bedridden, old man, aboard a train sanatorium, a robot watches over his health.
In the early Middle Ages, he lives among nobles who make intoxicants from blood.
Is abducted by a Greek on a motorcycle in his home country, surfs with his hospital bed in the firn snow of the Alps and meets a Korean mafioso.
He is completely absorbed in the imagined event, everything is real, even later in memory.
These inner journeys are fundamentally different for Claussen from any dream.
Dream is a different state, he describes his experience.
In contrast, the memories from mental journeys are still clear to me after years and in incidental details.
Significant and more intense even more than what is truly experienced.
The intensity of this experience breaks the horizon of our other life-worldly experiences, says the psychiatrist Schmidt-Degenhard.
In the end, oneiroids, psychosis, hallucinations and drug frenzy show how brittle our inner images of external reality are in waking.
Any change in the state of consciousness, sums uprising the psychologist Caste, "can lead to a deformation of what we call reality."
An experience that can also be enriching.
