Burda
Children are to take over the publisher at the age of 27
The new head of the publisher Kallen is an interim solution: In the long term, Hubert Burda wants to put his company in the hands of his two children, he told SPIEGEL.
The holding company will continue to belong to 100 percent of the family in the future.
The publisher Hubert Burda sees his company in the hands of his children in the long term.
Both children inherit at the age of 27 and have the firm will to enter the company, the 69-year-old told SPIEGEL.
We have regulated the details of this as a precautionary measure.
The question of whether they are also active in the company arises only afterwards.
The question of whether they are also able to do so.
You can't preside 7500 employees with your children if they're not sufficiently empowered.
This kills the children and the company as well.
Burda had announced on Thursday that he was withdrawing from the board of directors from January and Appointed Paul-Bernhard Kallen as his successor.
The 52-year-old Kallen has been a board member of Burda since 1999.
Initially, he was in charge of technology.
Subsequently, treasury accounted for corporate assets and, after the departure of Jürgen Todenhöfer, the responsibility for direct marketing, abroades, printing and finances.
For the first time, there is no family member at the head of the publisher.
Burda's children are 19 and 17 years old, his son Felix, who would be 42 years old today, died a few years ago.
