Promises of FonPeek
Peek is extremely limited, a device with which you can only read your e-mails, without a way to see certain attachments or to be able to surf the Internet.
It enjoyed its five minutes of fame when it chose Time magazine one of the products of the year.
Seeing it on the road is rather unlikely, but the idea in itself is still of interest, especially for young people.
In contrast to the smartphones, Peek costs very little and has a very cheap flat rate, for example, only half of the price of the flat rate of an iPhone.
After seeing the success with it, it has launched two new devices: one that is intended only for Twitter, the other with a life-to-life data service, quite unlike Peek, where you have to pay a six times higher price.
Fonpeek, which was unveiled at the Le Web event yesterday, is the European version of this invention and thus its third version.
It has the same limits as its American counterpart, but it has a surprise.
The monthly maintenance price for the phone, about 12 euros a month, includes roaming for the whole of Europe.
Those traveling around the world with a smartphone already know what it means to have to forego all the data to be able to read your e-mails as soon as you've crossed the country's border or when you're desperately looking for a cafe with WIFI (or a connection with Fon).
The idea roaming with flat-rate is incredibly seductive and should actually be possible for a long time.
It’s true that some telephone providers already offer special contracts and prices for stays abroad, but in my experience you already need a doctorate in physics and good glasses to be able to read all the fine print.
The European Commission's repeated criticism of affordable roaming tariffs for the whole of Europe has almost always gone unnoticed.
Outside of one’s own country, you are always afraid of reading your e-mails.
The fact that a small device like FonPeek (although it’s always amazing that it’s named Fon, even though it doesn’t own Wifi/Wi-Fi) can offer these kinds of tariffs means that phone providers have to slowly but surely rethink and start considering different ways they can use their data network.
Kindle is another good example of this, its pricing includes the downloading of books and further inquiries on the Internet.
Without contract and without hooks.
This is an internet-enabled device and you only pay for it once, namely when buying, not month after month.
And that's exactly what I'm expecting from FonPeek one day, a buying opportunity with lifelong internet access like Peek already offers in the U.S.
And that's not because I want to buy it.
I'm reading my emails by phone, thank you.
Instead, I expect this because I believe that it would be a more suitable model for the consumer.
It’d be interesting to see a computer with the same system.
The fetish color of Michelle
Yellow is without a doubt the American first lady's favorite color.
Already when Barack Obama took office as president of the USA, she appeared in a dress and a coat of this color and re-elected the same color to accompany him at his, no doubt second most important moment in his life: the awarding of the Nobel Peace Prize in Oslo.
Michelle Obama wore very similar models on both occasions: except that it was almost the same color - a glossy green-yellow - the clothes consisted of three parts - dress, jacket and coat made of the same fabric, and in addition she wore something large-shaped embroidered or a necklace.
It even goes so far that at the first sight it had the appearance that the first lady of the United States wanted to follow the example of the Princess of Asturias and had decided to wear the same dress at more than just an occasion.
However, the two dresses differ greatly in the type of fabric from which they are made.
At the inauguration she wore a lace dress and, in Oslo, one made of patterned velvet.
The accessories were also different: the first time green shoes with half-height heels and the second time a similar model, but golden.
The hairstyle was also different every time: the hair was stuck up on this occasion as a hair wreath, in the previous one she wore her hair open.
75% of working mothers have had problems at work because of their maternity
Discrimination at work has increased in recent years, especially among the younger women who want to have children.
This is the result of CSIC’s “Fertility and Careers of Women in Spain” in collaboration with the Institute for Women.
The survey of 9,737 women between the ages of 15 and 75 leaves no doubt about this: 75% of working women see their career chances in the labour market restricted due to their maternity.
The study discloses that there are major differences in deciding to start a family and the number of children depending on whether the women work or not and, if so, what kind of profession they do.
Those of them who do not do any other activity outside the house, work only half-day or have no fixed employment contract, are more likely to become mothers and have more children.
In contrast, the women with a permanent job, especially in the public sphere, postpone the establishment of a family and motherhood and therefore also have fewer children.
“Women in the age group between 35 and 49 years of age with a permanent employment only get their first child on average 3.7 years after contraction, a number that increases to 4.1 years for women with permanent employment, who have taken longer to achieve this and who have realized their contract and the first child much later.
In addition, the type of work also has a major impact on maternity, albeit less than the calendar with regard to conception," explains CSIC researcher Margarita Delgado.
The level of education is another factor that must be taken into account when marrying and the first child.
According to the survey, age at the time of marriage and the first child is higher, the higher the level of education.
This difference could also be noted among women of the same age.
For example, the women in the group between 35 and 49 years of age, depending on whether they only have secondary school graduation or a higher education, on average of 25.1 or 32.1 for the first time.
Furthermore, depending on their level of education, there is another difference between women.
While the women with a main degree are already getting married and having their first child before their first, the women with a middle or high school graduation make it exactly the other way around and first look for a permanent job and then get their first child, Delgado notes.
